hadiths[554]{hadithnumber,text}:
"3601","2424 - Sa'id narrated: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated, from Shu'bah, from Thabit al-Bunani, who said: I heard Anas saying: ""Abu Talhah would hardly fast in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) because of campaigning; when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died, I never saw him breaking fast but on the day of Fitr or Adha."""
"3602","2425 - Sa'id narrated: Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad ibn An'um, from Abd Allah ibn Yazid, from Abdullah ibn Amr, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: ""Whose head is split in the path of Allah and he is devoted, Allah forgives him what came before of sin."""
"3603","Chapter: Horses: good is tied in their forelocks until the Day of Resurrection. 2426 - Sa'id narrated: Abu al-Ahwas narrated: Shabib ibn Gharqadah narrated, from Urwah al-Bariqi, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Good is knotted in the forelocks of horses until the Day of Resurrection."""
"3604","2427 - Sa'id narrated: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated, from Shu'bah, saying: Abu al-Tayyah informed me, saying: I heard Anas narrating from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: ""Blessing is in the forelocks of horses."""
"3605","2428 - Sa'id narrated: Hudayj ibn Mu'awiyah narrated: Abu Ishaq informed us, from Urwah al-Bariqi, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: ""Good is tied in the forelocks of horses until the Hour comes."""
"3606","2429 - Sa'id narrated: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated, from Sa'id al-Bazzar, from Makhul, who said: It reached us that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Horses: good is tied in their forelocks until the Day of Resurrection, and their owner is aided upon them. So collar them, but do not collar with bowstrings."""
"3607","2430 - Sa'id narrated: Sufyan narrated, saying: I heard Shabib ibn Gharqadah saying: I heard Ibn Abi al-Ja'd saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: ""Good is tied in the forelocks of horses until the Day of Resurrection."""
"3608","2431 - Sa'id narrated: Sufyan narrated, from Mujalid, from al-Sha'bi, from Urwah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it, with the addition: ""Reward and booty."" [Flag: source text breaks off after the addition; translated literally]"
"3609","2432 - Sa'id narrated: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from al-Harith ibn Ya'qub, from Abu al-Aswad al-Ghifari, from al-Nu'man al-Ghifari, from Abu Dharr, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: ""O Abu Dharr, understand what I say to you: a she-kid that comes to a man among the Muslims is better for him than Uhud in gold that he leaves behind. O Abu Dharr, understand what I say to you: the wealthy are the fewest on the Day of Resurrection, except one who says so-and-so. Understand, O Abu Dharr, what I say to you: horses have good in their forelocks until the Day of Resurrection"" - thrice."
"3610","2433 - Sa'id narrated: Hushaym narrated, from Ibn Awn, from Makhul, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Collar the horses, but do not collar them with bowstrings."""
"3611","Chapter: Whoever ties a horse in the path of Allah. 2434 - Sa'id narrated: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, that Bukayr ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Ashajj narrated to him, from his father, from Ata' ibn Yasar, from Abdullah ibn Abbas, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: ""Shall I not inform you of the best of people? Among the best of people is a man holding the rein of his horse in the path of Allah. I inform you of the next: a man secluded with his sheep, rendering Allah's due in them. And I inform you of the worst of people: a man asked by Allah who does not give thereby."""
"3612","2435 - Sa'id narrated: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Tie horses; whoever ties a horse shall have the harvest of a hundred and fifty wasqs."" [Flag: measure phrase obscure in source; translated literally]"
"3613","2436 - Sa'id narrated: Ya'qub ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated, from Abu Hazim, from Bahjah ibn Abd Allah al-Juhani, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""The best life people live for: a man holding the rein of his horse in the path of Allah; whenever he hears a cry or alarm, he flies upon his horse's back, seeking death and killing where they are likely; or a man in a mountain-pass of these passes, or in the belly of a valley of these valleys, with a few sheep of his, establishing prayer, giving zakat, and worshipping Allah until certainty comes to him - with nothing of people but in good."""
"3614","2437 - Sa'id narrated: Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated, from Ibrahim ibn Nashit, from a man, from Abdullah ibn al-Harith ibn Jaz' al-Zubaydi - who had companionship - who said: Two men entered upon him; he pulled a cushion he was leaning on and cast it to them. They said: We do not want this; we came only to hear something to benefit by. He said: ""Whoever does not honor his guest is not of Muhammad nor of Ibrahim. Blessed is one who spends the evening clinging to the halter of his horse in the path of Allah, breaking fast on a crust and cold water; and woe to the slanderers who wallow like cattle - 'Lift, boy; put, boy' - therein never remembering Allah, mighty and majestic."""
"3615","Chapter: Honoring horses and caring for them. 2438 - Sa'id narrated: Sufyan narrated, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Muhammad ibn Yasar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out one night wiping the face of his horse with his garment, saying: ""Jibril reproached me concerning horses last night."""
"3616","2439 - Sa'id narrated: Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated, from Shurahbil ibn Muslim, from Tamim al-Dari, who said: Rawh ibn Zinba' visited him and found him sifting barley for his horse with his family around him. He said: Was there none among these to suffice you? He said: ""Indeed, but there is no Muslim man who sifts a barley-grain for his horse then hangs it upon him but Allah writes for him for every grain a good deed."""
"3617","2440 - Sa'id narrated: Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated, from Amr ibn Qays al-Sakuni, who said: I heard Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz forbidding racing the horse except by right."
"3618","2441 - Sa'id narrated: Hushaym narrated, from Abu Bishr, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, who said: ""You have seen us cutting the bowstrings from the necks of our mounts."""
"3619","2442 - Sa'id narrated: Ibrahim ibn Sulayman Abu Isma'il al-Mu'addib narrated, from al-Ahwas ibn Hakim, from Rashid ibn Sa'd, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Do not cut the forelocks of horses, for they are their warmth; nor their tails, for they are their fly-whisks."""
"3620","Chapter: The supplication of horses. 2443 - Sa'id narrated: Faraj ibn Fadalah narrated: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad ibn An'um narrated, from Ulayy ibn Rabah, from Mu'awiyah ibn Hudayj, who said: I passed by Abu Dharr while he was rolling a horse of his in the dust, then he took to wiping with his garment. I said: By Allah, you love this horse of yours. He said: ""Yes; by Allah, I see this one has been answered."" I said: Do horses supplicate? He said: Yes; there is no horse but it has a supplication it supplicates; some of them are answered and some are not. It says: O Allah, You have owned me to the son of Adam and made my provision in his hand, so make me dearer to him than his family and wealth. And I see this horse of mine has surely been answered."""
"3621","2444 - Sa'id narrated: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Shimasah, from Mu'awiyah ibn Hudayj, that he passed by him a man at the racetrack with his horse; he held its rein in the shade of a dune and sent his boy to see who he was - and it was Abu Dharr. Ibn Hudayj came to him and said: O Abu Dharr, I see this horse has wearied you, and I see nothing with it. Abu Dharr said: This is a horse that has been answered. Ibn Hudayj said to him: What is the supplication of a mute beast among beasts? Abu Dharr said: ""There is no horse but it calls upon Allah every dawn, saying: O Allah, You have put me in the charge of a servant of Your servants and made my provision in his hands. O Allah, make me dearer to him than his child, his family, and his wealth."""
"3622","Chapter: Endowing mounts and weapons in the path of Allah, mighty and majestic. 2445 - Sa'id narrated: Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated, from Hisham ibn al-Ghaz, from Makhul, who said: ""Nothing of endowed mounts shall be sold, nor shall you exchange them."""
"3623","2446 - Sa'id narrated: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated: Amr informed me, that Bukayr narrated to him, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, who said: He had a shield with him, saying: ""Were it not that Umar said to me: Endow your weapon, I would have given it to one of my sons."""
"3624","2447 - Sa'id narrated: Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated, from Safwan ibn Amr, that the horses Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz carried upon in the path of Allah left him branded on their thighs: ""Equipment for Allah, mighty and majestic."""
"3625","Chapter: Archery and its excellence. 2448 - Sa'id narrated: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Abu Ali Thumamah ibn Shufi al-Hamdani, that he heard Uqbah ibn Amir al-Juhani saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), while upon the pulpit, saying: ""And prepare against them whatever you are able of power"" - ""Indeed power is archery; indeed power is archery; indeed power is archery."""
"3626","2449 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Abu Ali al-Hamdani, from Uqbah ibn Amir, that he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Lands will be opened for you, Allah will suffice you, so let none of you fail to amuse himself with his arrows."
"3627","2450 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Jabir narrated to me, he said: Abu Sallam narrated to me, from Khalid ibn Zayd, he said: I was a man skilled in archery, and Uqbah ibn Amir al-Juhani used to pass by me and say: O Khalid, come out with us to shoot. Then one day I was slow in coming to him, so he said: Come, I shall narrate to you a hadith I heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Indeed Allah admits three persons into Paradise on account of a single arrow: its maker who seeks good in making it, the one who shoots it and supplies it. Shoot and ride, and that you shoot is more beloved to me than that you ride. And there is no amusement except in three things: a man training his horse, his playing with his wife, and his shooting with his bow and arrows. And whoever abandons shooting after having learned it out of dislike for it, then it is a blessing he has abandoned, or he said: he has been ungrateful for it."
"3628","2451 - Saeed narrated, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir - he raised it (to the Prophet) - he said: Everything of the amusement of this world is false except a man training his horse, his playing with his wife, and his amusement with his bow. Indeed three enter Paradise on account of a single arrow: its maker seeking reward, the one who shoots it, and the one who supplies it."
"3629","2452 - Saeed narrated, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Layth, from Mujahid, he said: The angels do not attend anything of your amusement except shooting or racing."
"3630","2453 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Mujahid, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Indeed the angels do not attend any of your amusement except racing and shooting."
"3631","2454 - Saeed narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Husayn, from a man, from Jabir ibn Zayd, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Every amusement with which the believer amuses himself is false except his shooting from his bow, his training his horse, and his playing with his wife."
"3632","2455 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Abdullah ibn Ayyash, from men among the jurists, one of them Hakim ibn Hakim ibn Abbad al-Ansari, that Umar ibn al-Khattab wrote to Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah: Teach your fighters archery, and teach your boys swimming."
"3633","2456 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Ziyad ibn Husayn, from Abu al-Aliyah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by some youths shooting, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Shoot, O sons of Ismail, for your father was an archer."
"3634","2457 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, from his father, he said: I saw Hudhayfah at al-Madain running between the two targets without an outer waist-wrap on him."
"3635","2458 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, he said: al-Amash narrated to us, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, he said: I saw Hudhayfah running between the two targets saying: I hit it, in a shirt."
"3636","2459 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Mujahid, he said: I saw Ibn Umar running between the two targets, saying: I hit it."
"3637","2460 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Mujahid, from Ibn Umar, he said: I saw him running between the two targets in a shirt, and whenever he hit a successful shot he said: I hit it, I hit it."
"3638","Chapter: The warrior who prolongs his absence from his family. 2461 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, wrote to the commanders of the frontiers ordering them to make the men return to their wives; if they did so, well and good, otherwise they were to compel them to provide maintenance; if they provided maintenance, well and good, otherwise they were to compel them to divorce; if they divorced, well and good, otherwise they were to compel them to pay the maintenance for the time past."
"3639","2462 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me that Bukayr narrated to him that Umar ibn al-Khattab was on night patrol with Abdullah ibn al-Arqam with him, and he saw a dark figure, so he said: O Abdullah, see what this is? So he went, and there was a woman, so he said: What is your matter? She said: What is it to you and to your companion with you? He said: And who is he? She said: Umar; is it right before Allah that he detains my husband from me for a year while I desire what women desire? So he returned to Umar and informed him, and he asked her: Where was he sent? She informed him, so he wrote to him and brought him back."
"3640","2463 - Saeed narrated, he said: Attaf ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Zayd ibn Aslam narrated to us that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, went out one night guarding the people, and passed by a woman in her house saying verses of longing (the transmitted verse is fragmentary in the source). When morning came, Umar sent for the woman and asked about her, and it was said: This is so-and-so, daughter of so-and-so, and her husband is on campaign in the path of Allah. So he sent a woman to her, saying: Stay with her until her husband comes, and he wrote to her husband and brought him back. Then Umar went to Hafsah, his daughter, and said to her: O my daughter, how long can a woman endure without her husband? She said to him: O father, may Allah forgive you, does one like you ask one like me about this? He said to her: Were it not something I wish to decide for the subjects, I would not have asked you about this. She said: Four months, or five months, or six months. So Umar said: The people campaign, marching a month going and staying in their campaign four months and returning a month. So he fixed that for the people in their year for their campaigns. [Flag: the woman's verse is truncated/garbled in the source; translated literally.]"
"3641","Chapter: When may a boy go to battle? 2464 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: I was presented to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Badr when I was thirteen years old, and he sent me back and did not permit me among the fighters. And I was presented to him on the day of al-Khandaq when I was fifteen years old, and he permitted me among the fighters."
"3642","2465 - Saeed narrated, he said: Ismail ibn Zakariyya narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: I was presented to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when I was fourteen years old, and he did not permit me in fighting, and I was presented to him when I was fifteen years old, and he permitted me in fighting. Nafi said: So I narrated this hadith to Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, and he said: This is the dividing line between men and boys. Then he wrote to his governors not to permit in fighting anyone younger than fifteen years."
"3643","2466 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu Sufyan, from Jabir, he said: I used to draw water for my companions on the day of Badr."
"3644","Chapter: The Quran is not to be taken on a journey to enemy land. 2467 - Saeed narrated, he said: Ismail ibn Zakariyya narrated to us, from Layth, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not travel with the Quran into enemy land, for I fear that one of them may lay hands on it. And Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, wrote to the garrisons not to travel with the Quran to enemy land, for I fear that one of them may lay hands on it."
"3645","Chapter: Whoever narrows a dwelling place or cuts off a road while in the path of Allah. 2468 - Saeed narrated, he said: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Asid ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Khathami, from Farwah ibn Mujahid al-Lakhmi, from Sahl ibn Muadh al-Juhani, he said: I campaigned with my father in the summer campaign in the time of Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan, under the command of Abdullah ibn Abd al-Malik, and we halted at Hisn Sinan, and the people crowded the lodgings and cut off the road. So my father stood among the people and said: O people, I campaigned with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in such-and-such a campaign, and the people crowded the lodgings and cut off the road, so the Prophet of Allah sent a caller calling among the people: Whoever narrows a dwelling place or cuts off a road, he has no jihad."
"3646","2469 - Saeed narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Uthman ibn Abi Sulayman, from some of the family of al-Zubayr, that al-Zubayr used to go ahead of the riders, come to the halting place, take hold of this tree and that tree, and place something by it; then when they came to him and asked him: Give us, he would give them."
"3647","Chapter: What is reported about inviting the polytheists before fighting. 2470 - Saeed narrated, he said: Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Abu al-Bakhtari, he said: Salman al-Farisi, may Allah be pleased with him, besieged one of the castles of Persia, and said: Let me invite them as I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) invite. I inform you: if you wish, embrace Islam and you shall have what the Muslims have and upon you shall be what is upon the Muslims; but if you refuse, then pay the jizyah from your hands while you are humbled; but if you refuse, then we declare war against you openly on equal terms, indeed Allah does not love the treacherous. They refused to answer, so his companions leapt up to fight them, but he forbade them until he had invited them for three days with what he had first invited them to; they refused to answer him, so they fought, and Allah granted victory to the Muslims. [Flag: the printed text has a garbled phrase rendered smoothed as refused to answer.]"
"3648","2471 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Saeed ibn Abi Hilal, that it reached him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say: Do not steal from the spoils, and do not act treacherously. And when you halt among a people, invite them to Islam; if they embrace Islam, invite them to move to the abode of migration; if they refuse, then they are like the Bedouin Muslims, with no share for them in the fay; if they refuse, then seek Allah's help in fighting them. And if they ask you that they come down upon the judgment of Allah, do not do so, for you do not know whether you will hit the judgment of Allah or not; rather let them come down upon your judgment and the judgment of your people. And if a people ask you that they come down on the condition that they have the covenant of Allah, do not do so; rather give them your covenant and the covenant of your fathers, for if you break your covenant and the covenant of your fathers, that is better for you than to break the covenant of Allah. And never give a people the covenant of Allah."
"3649","Chapter: What is reported about the merit of Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him. 2472 - Saeed narrated, he said: Yaqub ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Abu Hazim, that Sahl informed him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said on the day of Khaybar: I shall give the banner tomorrow to a man through whom Allah will grant victory. So the people spent the night discussing which of them would be given it. In the morning the people went early to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), each hoping to be given it, and he said: Where is Ali ibn Abi Talib? They said: O Messenger of Allah, he is complaining of his eyes. He sent for him and he was brought, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) spat in his eyes and prayed for him, and he was cured, as though he had no pain. And he gave him the banner. Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Shall I fight them until they become like us? He said: Proceed calmly until you halt in their courtyard, then invite them to Islam and inform them of what is due upon them of Allah's right in it; for that Allah guides a single man through you is better for you than that red camels be yours."
"3650","2473 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Hazim narrated to us, from his father, from Sahl ibn Sad, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it, except that he said: By Allah, that Allah guides through your guidance a single man is better for you than red camels."
"3651","2474 - Saeed narrated, he said: Khalid ibn Abdullah narrated to us, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I shall give the banner tomorrow to a man who loves Allah and His Messenger, through whom Allah will grant victory - Umar ibn al-Khattab said: I never loved leadership before that day - so he called Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, and handed it to him, saying: Go forth and do not turn back. So he walked a while, then stopped without turning back, and said: O Messenger of Allah, upon what shall I fight the people? He said: Fight them until they testify that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah; when they do that, they will have protected from you their blood and their wealth except by its right, and their reckoning is upon Allah."
"3652","2475 - Saeed narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ayyub ibn Musa, from Bukayr ibn Abdullah ibn al-Ashajj, from Saeed ibn al-Musayyib, he said: A man came to him and said: O Abu Muhammad, shall I not inform you what we do in our campaigns? He said: No. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he halted at a town, would invite its people to Islam; if they followed, he mingled them with himself and his companions; if they refused, he invited them to the jizyah; if they gave it, he accepted it from them; if they refused, he declared war against them openly on equal terms. And the humblest of his companions, when he gave a covenant, they all fulfilled it together."
"3653","The hadith of the two chests. 2476 - Saeed narrated, he said: Shihab ibn Khirash ibn Hawshab narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj ibn Dinar, from Mansur ibn al-Mutamir, he said: Shaqiq ibn Salamah al-Asadi narrated to me, from the messenger who went between Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, and Salamah ibn Qays al-Ashjai, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab dispatched the people with Salamah ibn Qays al-Ashjai at al-Harrah against some of the people of Persia, saying: Go forth in the name of Allah and in the path of Allah; you fight those who disbelieve in Allah. Do not steal from the spoils, do not act treacherously, do not mutilate, do not kill a woman, nor a child, nor an aged elder. When you reach the people, invite them to Islam and to jihad; if they accept, they are of you, with what is yours and upon them what is upon you. If they refuse, invite them to Islam without jihad; if they accept, accept from them, and inform them they have no share in the fay. If they refuse, invite them to the jizyah; if they accept, impose upon them according to their capacity, place among them an army to fight on their behalf, and leave them with what you imposed. If they refuse, fight them. If they ask you to give them the covenant of Allah and the covenant of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), do not give them the covenant of Allah nor the covenant of Muhammad; rather give them your own covenants, then fulfill to them. If they refuse you, fight them, for Allah will aid you over them. When we reached the land, we invited them to all we were commanded, and they refused. When the siege pressed them, they called to us: Give us the covenant of Allah and the covenant of Muhammad. We said: No; rather we give you our own covenants, then we shall fulfill to you. They refused, so we fought them; a Muslim man was killed, then Allah granted us victory, and the Muslims filled their hands with goods, slaves and silver as they wished. Then Salamah ibn Qays, the commander of the people, entered and went stepping over their fire-temples, and there were two chests hanging at the top of the house. He said: What are these two chests? They said: Things with which kings used to venerate their fire-temples. He said: Lower them to me; on them were the seals of kings after kings. He said: I think they sealed them only over something precious. Bring the Muslims to me. When they came, he told them the news of the two chests, saying: I wanted to open them in your presence. So he opened them, and they were full of jewels the like of which had never been seen. He turned to the Muslims and said: O company of Muslims, you know what Allah has tried you with in this campaign of yours; will you willingly give up these two chests to the Commander of the Believers for his needs and affairs and what befalls him? They answered with the voice of one man: We call Allah to witness that we have done so, and our souls are content for the Commander of the Believers. So he called me and said: You witnessed the Commander of the Believers on the day of al-Harrah, and what he enjoined upon us, and how we followed his instruction, and the matter of the two chests, and the Muslims willingly giving them to him; so take them to the Commander of the Believers and tell him the news truthfully, then return to me with what he says to you. I said: I must have a companion. He said: Take by the hand whom you love. So I took a man of the people by the hand, and we set out with the two chests, shaking them, until we arrived with them at Madinah. I seated my companion with the two chests and went seeking the Commander of the Believers, Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, and there he was feeding the people, leaning on a staff, saying: O Yarfa, put here; O Yarfa, put here. I sat among the people eating nothing. He passed by me and said: Will you not eat of the food? I said: I have no need of it. He saw to the people while standing over them going about among them, then said: O Yarfa, take your leather mat and bowls. Then he turned away and I followed him; he went threading the roads of Madinah until he reached a large spacious house and entered it, and I entered after him. Then he reached a room of the house and entered it; I stood a while until I thought the Commander of the Believers had settled in his seat, then I said: Peace be upon you. He said: And upon you; enter. So I entered, and there he was sitting on a cushion leaning on another. When he saw me, he threw to me the one he had been leaning on, and I sat on it, and it pricked me, for its stuffing was palm-fibre. He said: O slave-girl, feed us. She brought a bowl with pieces of dry bread, and he poured oil over it, with no salt nor vinegar in it, saying: Yet if she were content she would have fed us better than this. He said to me: Come near. So I came near. I went to take a piece, but by Allah I could not swallow it; I kept chewing it, now from this side, now from that side, and could not get it down, while he ate the best of eating, nothing of food sticking to his garment or hair, until I saw him licking the sides of the bowl. Then he said: O slave-girl, give us drink. She brought barley-meal drink of hulled barley, and he said: Give him. She handed it to me; when I stirred it, husks rose to the top, and if I left it, it separated. When he saw me finding it unpleasant, he laughed and said: What is wrong with you, show it to me if you wish. So I handed it to him, and he drank until he placed it upon his forehead like this, then said: Praise be to Allah who fed us and satisfied us, gave us drink and quenched us, and made us of the nation of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). I said: The Commander of the Believers has eaten and is full, and drunk and is quenched; my need, may Allah make me your ransom - Shaqiq said: In the messenger's narration to me there were three oaths, this in one place of them - He said: May your father be lost, who are you? I said: The messenger of Salamah ibn Qays. He said: By Allah, it is as though you came out of his belly in tenderness to me and love of my news from those you came from; and he kept saying while crawling toward me: Come on, may your father be lost, how did you leave Salamah ibn Qays? How are the Muslims? What did you do? How are you? I said: As you would love, O Commander of the Believers. I recounted to him the news until they confronted us in battle and a Muslim man was killed; he said: To Allah we belong, and it weighed upon him as Allah willed, and he asked mercy for the man at length. I said: Then Allah granted us a great victory, O Commander of the Believers, and the Muslims filled their hands with goods, slaves and silver as they wished. He said: Woe to you! How is meat there? For it is the tree of the Arabs, and the Arabs thrive only on their tree. I said: A sheep for two dirhams. Then he said: Allah is Most Great. Then he said: Woe to you, was any other Muslim man killed? I came to the mention of the two chests and told him their news; the messenger swore at that another oath, by Allah besides whom there is no god, it was as though I had loosed upon him vipers, black snakes and spotted snakes, that he leapt up from his place, then turned to me holding his waist-wrapper, saying: May your father be lost, what business have they with Umar? By Allah, the Muslims will face thirst, hunger and fear in the throats of the enemy, while Umar goes in the morning from his family and returns to them following the shades of Madinah! Go back with what you brought; I have no need of it. I said: O Commander of the Believers, my camel and my companion's have broken down, so carry us. He said: No, nor any honour for the other, since you did not bring what pleases me that I should carry you. I said: O servants of Allah, is a man to be left between two lands? He said: Were it not that you said it - O Yarfa, go with him and mount him and his companion on two sturdy she-camels of the charity camels, then drive them until you take them out of al-Harrah. Then he turned to me and said: If the Muslims spend the winter in their winter quarters before the two are divided among them, I shall surely punish you and your companion. Then he said: When you reach the land, look for the neediest Muslim you see and hand the two camels to him. So we came to him (Salamah) and told him the news, and he said: Call the Muslims for me. When they came, he said: The Commander of the Believers has spared you your two chests, and deemed you more entitled to them than he; so divide them with Allah's blessing. They said: May Allah set you right, O commander, they need eyesight, valuation and division. He said: By Allah, you shall not leave until you claim from me a stone of it. So he counted the people and counted the stones; sometimes they threw two stones to a man, and split a stone between two men."
"3654","2477 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abu al-Hatrush Shamlah ibn Hazzal narrated to us, he said: Qatadah narrated to us, tracing the hadith to Umar ibn al-Khattab, that he had a courier going between him and the king of the Romans, and that the wife of Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, borrowed a dinar and bought perfume with it, put it in small bottles, and sent it with the courier to the wife of the king of the Romans. When it reached her, she emptied them and filled them with jewels, saying: Take them to the wife of the Commander of the Believers, Umar. When they reached her, she emptied them onto her carpet; Umar entered upon that scene and said: What is this, woman? She said: I borrowed a dinar from so-and-so, bought perfume with it, put it in bottles, and sent it - meaning with your courier - to the wife of the king of the Romans, and she sent these to me. Umar thereupon said: O so-and-so, take this, go sell it, repay so-and-so a dinar, and put the remainder in the treasury of the Muslims; the family of Umar has no more right to it than the Muslims."
"3655","2478 - Saeed narrated, he said: Suwayd ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, he said: Husayn narrated to us, from Abu Wail, he said: al-Saib ibn al-Aqra was a governor for Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, over some district (the place-name is garbled in the source), and he was brought gold found buried, saying: I see no right in it except for the Commander of the Believers; it is neither fay, nor jizyah, nor charity. Then he called the people and consulted them, and sent it to Umar. His messenger came with it, and Umar said to the messenger: What is this you have brought me? You have not brought me what pleases me. I said: O Commander of the Believers, my camel broke down on me, so mount me. He said: Were you not a messenger, I would not mount you. So he wrote to the people of the watering places that I be carried from water to water, and he wrote to al-Saib ibn al-Aqra to come. He said: So I came until I entered upon Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, and before him was a bowl with coarse bread and broken pieces of his lean camel meat. He said to me: Eat. I ate a little, then could not eat. He said: Eat, for it is not the fine white bread of Iraq which you and your companions eat. Then he said: Look who is at the door? They said: Shepherds of sheep. He said: The black ones? They said: Yes. He said: Call them. They began eating with him, until I looked at them licking the bowl with their fingers. Then he said: So he entered, and mentioned nothing to me; I went to my lodging. When he went out to the people, I entered upon him, and he said: What is this you sent to me? I said: We found it as buried wealth. I said: It is neither fay, nor jizyah, nor charity. I said: None has any right in it but the Commander of the Believers. He said: May you lose your father, what makes me more entitled to it, while I am in Madinah and they are in the throats of the enemy? I said: O Commander of the Believers, I made it lawful (i.e. cleared it by counsel). He said: Do you recognize the seal of your messenger? So he opened it, and in it was something wondrous. He said: I adjure you to take it to Kufah and divide it. Abu Wail said: So I saw al-Saib bringing out pieces of gold until he gave a man. [Flag: some phrases in the source are compressed/garbled; translated literally.]"
"3656","Chapter: The letters of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his invitation. 2479 - Saeed narrated, he said: Khalid ibn Abdullah narrated to us, from Husayn, from Abdullah ibn Shaddad, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote to the ruler of the Romans: From Muhammad, the Messenger of Allah, to Heraclius, ruler of the Romans: I invite you to Islam; if you embrace Islam, you shall have what the Muslims have and upon you shall be what is upon them; but if you refuse, then leave the peasants, let them embrace Islam or pay the jizyah. When the letter reached him, he read it, and a brother of his stood up and said: Do not read this letter; he began with himself before you, did not name you king, and made you ruler of the Romans. He said: You have lied; that he began with himself is because he wrote to me; and if he named me ruler of the Romans, then I am the ruler of the Romans, they have no ruler but me. He kept reading the letter while his forehead sweated from the distress of the letter, though in severe cold. He said: Who knows this man? So he sent for Abu Sufyan, saying: Do you know this man? He said: Yes. He said: What is his lineage among you? He said: Of the noblest lineage among us. He said: Where is his house relative to your town? They said: In the middle of our town. He said: This is among his signs. He said: Does anyone of them come to you, and does anyone of you go to them? I said: They of us go to them, and none of them comes to us. He said: Did you fight him? He said: Yes. He said: Did you prevail over them, or did they prevail over you? I said: Rather they prevailed over us. He said: And this is among his signs. He said: I said: Do you not hear that he says: He will prevail over the whole earth? He said: If it is he, he will indeed prevail over the earth until he prevails over what is under my feet. Had I known it was he, I would have walked to him until I kissed his head and washed his feet. Abu Sufyan said: It was the first day I feared Muhammad; I said: Here in his authority, his kingdom and his fortresses, his forehead drips sweat from the distress of the sheet! I remained in fear of Muhammad until I embraced Islam. And in the letter were: O People of the Book, come to a word common between us and you, that we worship none but Allah, associate nothing with Him, and take none of us others as lords besides Allah; but if they turn away, say: Bear witness that we are Muslims; and: He it is who sent His Messenger with guidance and the religion of truth to make it prevail over all religion, though the polytheists dislike it; and: Fight those who do not believe in Allah nor the Last Day, nor forbid what Allah and His Messenger forbade, nor follow the religion of truth, from those given the Book, until they pay the jizyah from their hands while humbled. And the Romans had a bishop over one of their churches in which their kings prayed, called Baghatir. He met one of the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: Write for me a chapter of the Quran. So they wrote for him a chapter, and he said: This is what we know as the Book of Allah. So he embraced Islam and concealed it. When Sunday came, he feigned illness and did not come to their church; when the next Sunday came, he did not come. It was said: He has no illness. So he was sent for: You shall surely come or be carried. So he came walking, and he was asked: What is wrong with you? He said: This is the Book of Allah, the command of Allah, the description of the Messiah, and it is the religion we know. He said: Woe to you, if I said this, the Romans would kill me. He said: But I say it. He said: Do you not hear what this one says? So they seized him when he spoke thus, and kept torturing him until they tore a rib from his ribs with the kidneys, yet he refused to apostatize from his religion until they killed him and burned him."
"3657","2480 - Saeed narrated, he said: Yaqub ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Harmalah, from Saeed ibn al-Musayyib, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote: From Muhammad, the Messenger of Allah, to Caesar: Come to a word common between us and you, up to His saying: Muslims. And he wrote to Khosrow and to the Negus with this verse. As for Khosrow, he tore the letter of Allah and did not look into it, so he (the Prophet) said: Torn apart, and torn apart shall be his nation. As for Caesar, when he read the letter - meaning of the Messenger of Allah - he said: This is a letter the like of which I have not heard since Sulayman, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So he called Abu Sufyan and al-Mughirah ibn Shubah, who were merchants there, and asked them about some of the affair of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and they informed him. He said: By my father and mother, he shall surely rule what is under my feet. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: They have a respite. As for the Negus, he commanded whoever of the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was with him, and sent to him his letter; so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Leave them as long as they leave you."
"3658","2481 - Saeed narrated, he said: Hudayj ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abdullah ibn Utbah, from Ibn Masud, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent us to the Negus, we being about eighty men, among them Abdullah ibn Masud, Jafar ibn Abi Talib, Abdullah ibn Urfutah, Uthman ibn Mazun, and Abu Musa al-Ashari. They came to the Negus, while Quraysh sent Amr ibn al-As and Umarah ibn al-Walid with a gift. When the two entered upon the Negus, they prostrated, then rushed to him from his right and his left, then said to him: Some men of our cousins have settled in your land, turning away from us and our religion. He said: Where are they? They said: They are in your land. He said: So he sent for them. Jafar said: I am your spokesman today; so they followed him. He greeted but did not prostrate. They said to him: Why do you not prostrate to the king? He said: We prostrate to none but Allah, Mighty and Majestic. He said: And what is that? He said: Allah sent among us a Messenger, and commanded us to prostrate to none but Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and commanded us with prayer and almsgiving. Amr ibn al-As said: They oppose you concerning Jesus son of Mary and his mother. They said: We say he is as Allah said. They said: He is the word of Allah and His spirit, which He cast to Mary, the virgin, the chaste, whom no man touched and to whom no child was falsely attributed. He said: So he lifted a stick from the ground, then said: O company of Abyssinians, priests and monks, by Allah, they do not exceed what we say about him by even this much. Welcome to you and to him from whom you came. I bear witness that he is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and that he is the one we find in the Gospel, and the one whom Jesus son of Mary gave glad tidings of. Settle wherever you wish. By Allah, were it not for the kingship I am in, I would have come to him until I myself carried his sandals and performed his ablution. And he ordered the gift of the other two, and it was returned to them. Then Abdullah ibn Masud hastened until he reached Badr; and it is claimed that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sought forgiveness for him when his death reached him."
"3659","2482 - Saeed narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Mujalid, from al-Shabi, he said: Ibn Buqaylah, the chief of al-Hirah, read to me a letter like this - meaning the length of the palm -: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate. From Khalid ibn al-Walid to the nobles of Persia: Peace upon whoever follows guidance. As for what follows: Praise be to Allah who stripped your kingdom, weakened your plot, scattered your gathering, and broke your servitude. Accept protection from me and pay me the jizyah, and he mentioned taking hostages for something; otherwise, by Allah besides whom there is no god, I shall surely come to you with a people who love death as you love life."
"3660","2483 - Saeed narrated, he said: Fudayl ibn Iyad narrated to us, from Layth, from Mujahid, he said: The people of idols are fought for Islam, and the People of the Book are fought for the jizyah."
"3661","Chapter: Permission to leave inviting the polytheists. 2484 - Saeed narrated, he said: Ismail ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Ibn Awn informed me, he said: I wrote to Nafi asking him about inviting the polytheists at fighting, and he wrote: That was at the beginning of Islam, and the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raided Banu al-Mustaliq while they were heedless, their cattle drinking at the water; he killed their fighters, took their captives, and on that day he took Juwayriyah bint al-Harith. Abdullah narrated that to me, and he was in that army."
"3662","2485 - Saeed narrated, he said: Ismail ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Sulayman al-Taymi, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, he said: We used to invite and to leave off."
"3663","2486 - Saeed narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Yunus informed me, from al-Hasan, he said: The Romans have no invitation; they have been invited since ages past."
"3664","2487 - Saeed narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Juwaybir informed me, from Abu Sahl, from al-Hasan, he said: He used to proclaim loudly: The Romans have no invitation."
"3665","2488 - Saeed narrated, he said: Khalid ibn Abdullah narrated to us, from Sulayman al-Taymi, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, he said: We used to campaign, inviting and leaving off."
"3666","Chapter: What is reported about obedience to the commander. 2489 - Saeed narrated, he said: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Abu Bakr ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Maryam, from Damrah ibn Habib, from a freedman of Abu Rayhanah, the companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that Abu Rayhanah was stationed at the coast, and he sought permission from the commander of his station: Permit me to visit my family, or grant me a night's leave. So he did. He arrived at Bayt al-Maqdis in the evening, went to the mosque, and did not go to his family. He began one chapter, then another chapter, until morning overtook him while he was in the mosque. When morning came, he set out returning to his station at the coast. It was said to him: O Abu Rayhanah, had you but visited your family, greeted them, and glanced at them. He said: My commander granted me leave only for a night, and its term has passed; I am not one to lie, nor to stay away from my station. So he set out, did not visit his family, nor see them, until he returned; and his dwelling was Bayt al-Maqdis."
"3667","2490 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me that Bukayr narrated to him that al-Hasan ibn Ali ibn Abi Rafi narrated to him from Abu Rafi, that he said: I was once in a dispatched force, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Go and bring me Maymunah. I said: O Prophet of Allah, I am in the force. He said: Go and bring me Maymunah. He said: O Prophet of Allah, I am in the force. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do you not love what I love? I said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Go and bring me Maymunah. So I went and brought her to him."
"3668","2491 - Saeed narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, about His statement, Mighty and Majestic: And when they are with him upon a collective matter, they do not go until they seek his permission - he said: That is about campaigning and Friday prayer; and the commander's permission in Friday prayer is that he gesture with his hand."
"3669","Chapter: What is reported about one who opposes the commander. 2492 - Saeed narrated, he said: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Rashid ibn Dawud al-Sanani, from Abu Salih al-Ashari, from Abu Amir al-Ashari, he said: I went out in a detachment, with us Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas. We halted at a place, and a youth among us said: I want to go graze the animals. Ibn Amir said to him: Do not do so until you seek the permission of our companion - meaning Abu Musa al-Ashari - and they were a company. So he sought his permission. Abu Musa said to him: Perhaps you want your family. He said: No. He said: Consider. He said: No. So the youth set out, went to his family, stayed with them four nights, then came back. Abu Musa asked him, saying: Did you visit your family? He said: I did not. Abu Musa said: You shall surely tell me. He said: I did not. He said: You shall surely tell me truthfully. He said: I did. Abu Musa said to him: You travelled in fire, fell upon your family in fire, and returned in fire; so start your deeds anew."
"3670","2493 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me that Sulayman narrated to him from al-Qasim, freedman of Abd al-Rahman, that he narrated to him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he intended to raid Khaybar, said: Let no weak or feeble one follow us. An Arab Bedouin followed him on a refractory young camel of his, which threw him and killed him. That reached the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after the conquest of Khaybar, so he commanded Bilal to call out: Verily Paradise is not lawful for a disobedient one."
"3671","2494 - Saeed narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said when he set out to Tabuk: None shall go out with us except one well-provisioned. A man went out on a refractory young camel of his, which threw him and he died. The people said: A martyr, a martyr. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded Bilal to call out: Verily none but a believing soul enters Paradise, and no disobedient one enters it. Mujahid said: I never heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a hadith harder than this, and the hadith of Sa'd ibn Muadh: He was indeed squeezed a squeezing."
"3672","2495 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Bukayr ibn al-Ashajj, from Busr ibn Saeed, from Junadah ibn Abi Umayyah, that he was with Amr ibn al-As at Alexandria, and he ordered the people: Do not fight. The rabble of the people flew off and fought. Amr saw them and said: O Junadah, reach the people, that none of them be killed disobedient. When Junadah came back, Amr looked out for him, then called to him: Was anyone of the people killed? He said: No. He said: Praise be to Allah."
"3673","2496 - Saeed narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Kawthar ibn Hakim informed me, from Makhul, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever separates from the community of the Muslims has no prayer until he returns to them, nor has one disobedient at a frontier post of the Muslims until he returns to his post."
"3674","2497 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Shimasah al-Mahri, that they besieged a fortress, and Uqbah ibn Amir passed by two men fighting from a place where the enemy could reach them but they could not reach the enemy. Uqbah said: This is no fighting ground for you two. One of them turned away, and the other remained until he was killed; Uqbah refused to pray over him."
"3675","2498 - Saeed narrated, he said: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Shurahbil ibn Muslim, that a man disobeyed orders from the coastal detachment, and death overtook him while he was among his family. Kab al-Ahbar was asked about praying over him, and he said: The servant is driven to his pit, and the pit is not driven to him; so pray over your companion."
"3676","Chapter: Dislike of carrying out hadd punishments in enemy land. 2499 - Saeed narrated, he said: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Maryam, from Humayd ibn Uqbah ibn Ruman, from Abu al-Darda, that he used to forbid carrying out hadd punishments on a man while he was campaigning in the path of Allah, until he returned, fearing that zeal might carry him to join the disbelievers; if they repent, Allah turns to them, and if they return, Allah's punishment is behind them."
"3677","2500 - Saeed narrated, he said: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from al-Ahwas ibn Hakim, from his father, that Umar wrote to the people: No commander of an army or detachment shall flog any Muslim man with a hadd punishment while he is campaigning, until he crosses the mountain pass returning, lest the zeal of Satan carry him to join the disbelievers."
"3678","2501 - Saeed narrated, he said: Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, he said: We were in an army in Roman land, with us Hudhayfah ibn al-Yaman, and over us al-Walid ibn Uqbah. He drank wine, so we wanted to apply the hadd to him. Hudhayfah said: Do you apply the hadd to your commander, when you have drawn near your enemy and they will covet you? It reached him, and he said: I shall surely drink though it be forbidden, and I shall surely drink despite whoever dislikes it."
"3679","2502 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Muhajir narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Sad, from his father, he said: Sad was brought Abu Mihjan on the day of al-Qadisiyyah, having drunk wine, so he ordered him to fetters. And Sad had wounds, so he did not go out to the people that day. He said: They took him up above al-Udhayb to look at the people, and he appointed Khalid ibn Urfutah over the cavalry. When the people met, Abu Mihjan said in grief, addressing the daughter of Khasfah, wife of Sad: Free me, and by Allah upon me, if Allah keeps me safe, I shall return until I place my feet in the fetters; and if I am killed, you are rid of me. He said: So she freed him - when the people met against him - and he leapt upon a mare of Sad called al-Balqa, then took a spear, then went out. He would charge no side of the enemy but he routed them, the people saying: This is an angel, for what they saw him do. And Sad kept saying: The gallop is the gallop of al-Balqa, the thrusting is the thrusting of Abu Mihjan, yet Abu Mihjan is in fetters. When the enemy was routed, Abu Mihjan returned until he placed his foot in the fetters, and the daughter of Hasfah informed Sad of his affair. Sad said: By Allah, I shall never strike after today a man through whose hands Allah benefited the Muslims as He benefited them. So he freed him. Abu Mihjan said: I used to drink it when the hadd was carried out upon me and I was purified of it; but since you have let me off without punishment, by Allah I shall never drink it again."
"3680","Chapter: The fear prayer. 2503 - Saeed narrated, he said: Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from Mansur, from Mujahid, from Abu Ayyash al-Zuraqi, he said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Usfan, with Khalid ibn al-Walid over the polytheists. We prayed Zuhr, and the polytheists said: We caught a moment of inattention; we caught an unawareness; had we charged them while they were in prayer. So the verse of shortening came down between Zuhr and Asr. When Asr came, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood facing the qiblah, the polytheists before him. A row formed behind the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and after that row another row. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bowed and they all bowed; then he prostrated and the row next to him prostrated, while the others stood guarding them. When these performed the two prostrations and stood, the others who were behind them prostrated. Then the row next to him moved back to the station of the others, and the last row advanced to the station of the first row. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bowed and they all bowed; then he prostrated and the row next to him prostrated, while the others stood guarding them. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the row next to him sat, the others prostrated, then they all sat, and he greeted them all together. He prayed it at Usfan, and he prayed it on the day of Banu Sulaym."
"3681","2504 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, from Sulayman ibn Qays, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fought Muharib Khasafah, and they saw a moment of inattention from the Muslims. A man called Ghawrath ibn al-Harith came until he stood over the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: Who will protect you from me? He said: Allah. So the sword fell from his hand, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took it, saying: Who will protect you from me? He said: Be the best of takers. He said: Do you testify that there is no god but Allah and that I am the Messenger of Allah? He said: No; but I pledge to you that I shall not fight you, nor be with a people who fight you. So he freed him. He returned and said: I have come to you from the best of people. When prayer came, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed the fear prayer: the people were two parties, a party facing the enemy, and a party praying with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He prayed with the party with him two rakahs, then they left and stood in the place of those facing their enemy, while those who had been facing their enemy came and prayed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) two rakahs. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had four rakahs, and the people two rakahs each."
"3682","2505 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me that Bakr ibn Sawadah narrated to him from Ziyad ibn Nafi, from Abu Musa, that Jabir ibn Abdullah narrated to them that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed for them the fear prayer on the day of Muharib and Thalabah, one rakah and two prostrations for each party."
"3683","2506 - Saeed narrated, he said: Muhammad ibn Aban al-Jufi narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Sulaym ibn Abd al-Saluli, he said: We were with Saeed ibn al-As at Tabaristan, and one day he said to us: Which of you prayed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the fear prayer? Hudhayfah said: I. He said: How did you see him do? He said: He split us into two groups, advanced, stationed a party of them with him, and stationed the other party behind them facing the enemy. He prayed with those with him one rakah and two prostrations, then these stood to the station of their companions, and those others came, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed with them one rakah and two prostrations. And he had said to them: If the enemy rouses you to turmoil, then fighting and speaking are lawful for you."
"3684","2507 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us; Amr ibn al-Harith said, and Bakr ibn Sawadah narrated to me: that Ziyad ibn Nafi narrated to him from Kab - who was of the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), whose hand was cut off on the day of al-Yamamah -: that the fear prayer is one rakah and two prostrations for each party. Amr said: And Bakr ibn Sawadah narrated to me that an elder narrated to him that they prayed the fear prayer on the day of Alexandria likewise with Amr ibn al-As."
"3685","2508 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Bukayr ibn al-Akhnas, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Allah made prayer obligatory upon the tongue of your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): four in residence, two on journey, and one in fear."
"3686","2509 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali, he said: I prayed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the fear prayer two rakahs by two, except Maghrib, for he prayed it three; and I prayed with him the travel prayer two by two, except Maghrib, for he prayed it three."
"3687","2510 - Saeed narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, he said: I heard Jabir say: The fear prayer is like what these commanders of yours do."
"3688","2511 - Saeed narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: al-Harith al-Ghanawi informed me, from Bukayr ibn al-Akhnas, from Mujahid, he said: Ibn Abbas said: The prayer of the resident is four, the prayer of the traveller two, and the fear prayer one."
"3689","Chapter: Practice in the fear prayer. 2512 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from al-Awzai, he said: Sabiq al-Barbari narrated to me, he said: Makhul wrote to al-Hasan, and the answer to his letter came to him while we were at Dabiq among the people seeking the enemy. He said: If they are the seekers, they dismount and pray on the ground; and if they are the sought, they pray upon their mounts."
"3690","2513 - Saeed narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Mughirah informed me, he said: I asked Ibrahim about His saying: Then on foot or mounted - he said: At the moment of pursuit, one prays wherever his face is, riding or on foot, gesturing with gestures, making prostration lower than bowing."
"3691","2514 - Saeed narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Yunus informed me, from al-Hasan, he said: One prays one rakah wherever his face is, gesturing with gestures."
"3692","2515 - Saeed narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Juwaybir informed me, from al-Dahhak, he said: If it is at sword-fighting, or he is pursuing, or a beast pursues him, let him pray one rakah wherever his face is, gesturing with gestures; if he cannot, let him utter two takbirs."
"3693","2516 - Saeed narrated, he said: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Shuayb ibn Dinar, he said: I heard Abd al-Wahhab ibn Bukht al-Makki say: If there is sword-fighting, if they are able they pray standing; otherwise mounted; otherwise with takbir; if they cannot, let them not leave it off within themselves."
"3694","2517 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, about His saying: But if you fear, then on foot or mounted - he said: That is in fighting, that a man prays wherever his face is, and upon his mount wherever he directs it, gesturing with his head."
"3695","Chapter: Who said: Do not wish to meet the enemy, and supplication when meeting them. 2518 - Saeed narrated, he said: Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from Abu Hayyan al-Taymi, from one who narrated to him, from Abdullah ibn Abi Awfa, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not wish to meet the enemy; ask Allah for well-being; and know that Paradise is under the shades of swords. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when the sun declined, would wait, then rise against his enemy, saying: O Allah, Revealer of the Book, Mover of the clouds, Defeater of the confederates, defeat them and aid us over them."
"3696","2519 - Saeed narrated, he said: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from al-Awzai, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not wish to meet your enemy, for you do not know, perhaps you will be tried by them; but say: O Allah, suffice us against them and restrain their might from us. When they come to you piping, chanting and shouting, then keep to the ground, saying: O Allah, our forelocks and their forelocks are in Your hand, and it is only You who kill them. When they overwhelm you, then leap up in their faces; and know that Paradise is under the flashing swords."
"3697","2520 - Saeed narrated, he said: Jarir narrated to us, from Mansur, from Mujahid, he said: It used to be said: Swords are the keys of Paradise."
"3698","2521 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Abu Hani al-Khawlani narrated to me, from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Hubuli, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not wish to meet the enemy; ask Allah for well-being. But if you are tried by them, say: O Allah, You are our Lord and their Lord; their forelocks and our forelocks are in Your hand, so fight them for us, defeat them for us. Lower your gazes, charge upon them with Allah's blessing, and seek Paradise under the flashing swords."
"3699","2522 - Saeed narrated, he said: Marwan ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, he said: Imran ibn Hudayr informed me, from Abu Mijlaz, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when fighting came, would say: O Allah, You are my support and my helper; by You I move, by You I charge, and by You I fight."
"3700","2523 - Saeed narrated, he said: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Maryam, from Ali ibn Abi Talhah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to like to meet the enemy after the sun declined, when the winds blow."
"3701","2524 - Saeed narrated, he said: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Maryam, from Ali ibn Abi Talhah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he fought, would fight from the breaking of dawn until sunrise, then hold back from fighting until the sun declined, then fight until sunset."
"3702","2525 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Bukayr ibn Iyad al-Fazari, that it reached him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he overlooked a town to enter it, would say: O Allah, Lord of the sky and what it shades, and Lord of the earth and what it bears, I ask You its good and the good of what is in it, and I seek refuge in You from its evil and the evil of what is in it."
"3703","2526 - Saeed narrated, he said: Khalid narrated to us, he said: Husayn narrated to us, from Awn ibn Abdullah, he said: Whoever overlooks a town and says: Grant me the love of its best people, and keep me from its worst, I hope he shall be given that."
"3704","2527 - Saeed narrated, he said: Khalid ibn Abdullah narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from Abdullah ibn Abi Awfa, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) supplicated against the confederates, saying: O Allah, Revealer of the Book, Swift in reckoning, O Allah, defeat them and shake them."
"3705","Chapter: What is reported about banners and turbans. 2528 - Saeed narrated, he said: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Safwan ibn Amr, he said: I heard Khalid ibn Ma'dan and Fudayl ibn Fudalah say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, honoured this nation with turbans and banners."
"3706","2529 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me that Yazid ibn Abi Habib narrated to him that the first to tie a white banner was Muawiyah ibn Abi Sufyan; banners had only been black."
"3707","2530 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from Abbad ibn Hamzah ibn al-Zubayr, he said: Upon al-Zubayr on the day of Badr was a yellow garment with which he had wrapped his head; and the angels descended wearing yellow turbans."
"3708","2531 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, he said: He had a cloak of brocade whose lining was fine silk stuffed with raw silk, which he used to wear in war. [Flag: the pronoun's antecedent is unclear in the source; translated literally.]"
"3709","Chapter: What is reported about cowardice and courage. 2532 - Saeed narrated, he said: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Maryam, from Fudayl ibn Fudalah al-Hawzani, that Abu al-Darda used to say: May the eyes of cowards never sleep."
"3710","2533 - Saeed narrated, he said: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: It was narrated to me from Aishah that she said: If any of you fears cowardice in himself, let him not campaign."
"3711","2534 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq al-Hamdani, from Hassan al-Absi, he said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Al-jibt is sorcery, and al-taghut is Satan. Courage and cowardice are inborn traits in a man: the brave man fights for one he does not know, while the coward flees from his own father. A man's nobility is his religion, and his lineage is his character, though he be Persian or Nabataean."
"3712","2535 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abu Shihab narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq al-Shaybani, from a shaykh, from Umar, he said: By Allah, to die on my bed is more beloved to me than to advance at the head of a squadron to face the enemy until I am killed."
"3713","2536 - Saeed narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, that a man wanted to charge the polytheists alone, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do you think you will kill them alone, until your companions charge and you charge with them?"
"3714","Chapter: A man does not flee from two men of the enemy. 2537 - Saeed narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ibn Abbas, he said: About Allah's statement, Mighty and Majestic: O Prophet, urge the believers to fight; if there be twenty steadfast among you they shall overcome two hundred - Allah wrote upon them that ten shall not flee from a hundred; then Allah lightened from them, then said: Now Allah has lightened from you, and He knows there is weakness in you - so a hundred ought not to flee from two hundred."
"3715","2538 - Saeed narrated, he said: Sufyan and Ismail ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, he said: If a man flees from three, he has not fled; but if he flees from two, he has fled."
"3716","Chapter: Who said: The commander is the refuge of every Muslim. 2539 - Saeed narrated, he said: Khalid ibn Abdullah narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ibn Umar, he said: We met the enemy, and the people swerved aside in a swerve; I was among those who swerved. We entered Madinah and presented ourselves to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as he came out to prayer, saying: O Messenger of Allah, we are the fleers. He said: Rather you are the returners to battle; I am a refuge for you."
"3717","2540 - Saeed narrated, he said: Ismail ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, he said: Umar said: I am the refuge of every Muslim."
"3718","Chapter: What is reported about showing off in jihad. 2541 - Saeed narrated, he said: Faraj ibn Fudalah narrated to us, from Asad ibn Wadaah, from Abu Bahriyyah al-Sakuni, from Abu al-Darda, he said: A man came to him and said: A man fights the enemy wishing to be praised and rewarded. He said: He has no reward, though he strike with his sword until it breaks."
"3719","2542 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abu al-Nadr, that Umar ibn Ubaydullah asked Abdullah ibn Umar, saying: May Allah set you right: a campaign is announced, so I spend seeking Allah's face, and go out for that; but when fighting comes, I desire that my prowess and presence be seen. He said: I hear you are a man who shows off."
"3720","2543 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, from Shubah, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Abu Wail, from Abu Musa al-Ashari, that a Bedouin came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: O Messenger of Allah, a man fights to gain spoils, a man fights to be remembered, and fights that his station be seen; which is in the path of Allah? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Whoever fights that the word of Allah be supreme, he is in the path of Allah, Mighty and Majestic."
"3721","2544 - Saeed narrated, he said: Salih ibn Musa narrated to us; al-Amash narrated to us, from Shaqiq, he said: It was said: O Messenger of Allah, a man fights out of zeal, courage, and to be seen. He said: Whoever fights that the word of Allah be the highest shall enter Paradise."
"3722","2545 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, he said: Ashath ibn Sulaym narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Ma'qil, he said: We were sitting with Abdullah ibn Masud, and a man of the people said: So-and-so was killed a martyr. Abdullah said: And how do you know he was killed a martyr? A man fights in anger, fights out of zeal, and fights to show off; the martyr is only one who fights that the word of Allah be the highest."
"3723","2546 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, he said: al-Amash narrated to us, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, from his father, he said: Hudhayfah said to Abu Musa: What if a man went out with his sword seeking Allah's face, then was struck and killed - would he enter Paradise? Abu Musa said to him: Yes. Hudhayfah said: No; rather when he goes out with his sword seeking by it Allah's face, then meets Allah's command and is killed, he enters Paradise."
"3724","2547 - Saeed narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Mansur informed me, from Ibn Sirin, he said: Abu al-Ajfa al-Sulami narrated to us, he said: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab addressing the people; he praised Allah and extolled Him, saying: Beware, do not go to excess in women's dowries, for had it been an honour in this world or piety with Allah, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would have been most entitled to it among you; he did not give any of his wives, nor was any of his daughters given, more than twelve ounces. Beware, one of you inflates his wife's dowry until enmity remains for her in himself, saying: I toiled for you to the sweat of the water-skin; and another thing you say in your campaigns: So-and-so was killed a martyr, so-and-so died a martyr - though perhaps he loaded the back or rump of his mount with gold or silver, seeking dinars and dirhams. Beware, do not say that; rather say as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever dies in the path of Allah or is killed, he is a martyr."
"3725","Chapter: What pride is liked and what is disliked. 2548 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from al-Awzai, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Taymi, he said: Ibn Atik narrated to me, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Of jealousy, some is what Allah, Mighty and Majestic, loves, and some is what Allah hates; and of pride, some is what Allah loves and some is what Allah hates. As for what Allah loves of jealousy, it is jealousy in suspicion; as for what Allah hates of jealousy, it is jealousy without suspicion. As for what Allah loves of pride, it is a man priding himself at fighting and at charity; as for what Allah hates, it is wanton arrogance."
"3726","Chapter: What is reported about the merit of martyrdom. 2549 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated to us, from his father, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah laughs at two men, one of whom killed the other, yet both entered Paradise: this one fights in the path of Allah and is killed, made a martyr; then Allah turns to this one, so he embraces Islam, fights in the path of Allah, is killed and made a martyr."
"3727","2550 - Saeed narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ali al-Sulami, from Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Do you know that Allah brought your father to life and said: Wish? So he wished to return to this world and be killed another time. He said: I have decreed that they shall not return."
"3728","2551 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated to us, from his father, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: By Him in whose hand is my soul, I would love to fight in the path of Allah and be killed, then be brought to life and be killed, then be brought to life and be killed. Abu Hurayrah used to say thrice: I bear witness for Allah."
"3729","2552 - Saeed narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar; he heard Jabir say: A man said on the day of Uhud: O Messenger of Allah, if I am killed, where am I? He said: In Paradise. So he threw away dates in his hand, then fought until he was killed."
"3730","2553 - Saeed narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Muhammad ibn Qays, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - and Ibn Ajlan from Muhammad ibn Qays, from Abdullah ibn Abi Qatadah, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), one of them adding over the other - that a man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was on the pulpit, saying: What if I strike with this sword of mine in the path of Allah, patient, seeking reward, advancing, not retreating - will Allah expiate my sins from me? He said: Yes. Then he called him, saying: Come; this is Jibril saying: Except if you owe a debt."
"3731","2554 - Saeed narrated, he said: Hazm ibn Abi Hazm narrated to us, he said: I heard al-Hasan say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No servant who dies, having good with Allah, loves to return to this world though he have the like of the kingdom of the world, except one slain in the path of Allah, for he loves to return and be killed another time."
"3732","2555 - Saeed narrated, he said: Hudayj ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated to us, from al-Bara ibn Azib, he said: A man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was fighting, saying: Is it better for me to embrace Islam? He said: Yes. He said: I bear witness that there is no god but Allah and that you are the Messenger of Allah. Then he said: Is it better for me to fight until I am killed? He said: Yes. He said: Though I have not prayed a prayer? He said: Yes. He said: So he charged, fought, killed, then they gathered against him and he was killed. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He did little, and was rewarded much."
"3733","2556 - Saeed narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Misar, from Abu Bakr ibn Hafs ibn Umar ibn Sa'd, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited in one of the two battlegrounds, the day of Badr or the day of Uhud: Race to forgiveness from your Lord and a Garden whose width is as the width of heaven and earth. An Ansari man called Ibn Qusham stood up, saying: Excellent, excellent. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: What did you mean by your saying excellent, excellent? He said: I said: If I enter it, I shall have ample room in it. O Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), what is between me and it? He said: That you meet this enemy and be true to Allah. So he threw away dates in his hand, saying: ... of the food of this world (the source is fragmentary here), then fought until he was killed. [Flag: a short clause before of the food of this world is garbled in the source; translated literally.]"
"3734","2557 - Saeed narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr, from Ubayd ibn Umayr, it was said: Which martyrs are most excellent? He said: One whose blood is shed and whose horse is hamstrung."
"3735","2558 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Saeed ibn Abi Hilal, that Sulayman ibn Aban ibn Abi Hudayr narrated to him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he set out to Badr, Sa'd ibn Khaythamah and his father both wanted to go out, and that was mentioned to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he ordered them that one of them go out. They cast lots, and the lot of Sa'd came out. He said: Will you prefer me with it, O my son? Sa'd said: It is Paradise; had it been other than it, I would have preferred you with it. So Sa'd went out with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and was killed on the day of Badr; then Khaythamah was killed the next year on the day of Uhud."
"3736","Chapter: What is reported about the souls of martyrs. 2559 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abdullah ibn Murrah, from Masruq, he said: Abdullah was asked about His saying: And think not those slain in the path of Allah dead; rather alive with their Lord, provided for. He said: As for us, we asked about that, and he said: Their souls are as green birds roaming in Paradise wherever they wish, then sheltering to lamps hung from the Throne. While they are thus, their Lord looks upon them a look, saying: Ask Me whatever you wish. They say: O our Lord, what shall we ask You while we are in Paradise roaming wherever we wish? While they are thus, your Lord looks upon them again, saying: Ask Me whatever you wish. They say: O our Lord, what shall we ask You while we are in Paradise roaming wherever we wish? When they saw they would not be left without asking, they said: We ask You to return our souls into our bodies in this world until we are killed in Your path. When He saw they would ask nothing but this, they were left."
"3737","2560 - Saeed narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ibn Shihab, from Ibn Kab ibn Malik, conveying to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): The souls of martyrs feed upon the fruits of Paradise."
"3738","2561 - Saeed narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Yazid; he heard Ibn Abbas say: The souls of martyrs are transformed into green birds feeding upon the fruits of Paradise."
"3739","Chapter: The martyr's reward. 2562 - Saeed narrated, he said: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Buhayr ibn Sa'd, from Khalid ibn Ma'dan, from al-Miqdam ibn Madi Karib, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The martyr has with Allah special merits: he is forgiven at the first gush of his blood, shown his seat in Paradise, adorned with the garment of faith, married to the wide-eyed houris, protected from the punishment of the grave, safe from the Greatest Terror, a crown of dignity placed upon his head, a single ruby of which is better than this world and what is in it, married to seventy-two wives of the wide-eyed houris, and permitted to intercede for seventy persons of his relatives."
"3740","2563 - Saeed narrated, he said: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Buhayr ibn Sa'd, from Khalid ibn Ma'dan, from Kathir ibn Murrah, from Ubadah ibn al-Samit, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of that."
"3741","2564 - Saeed narrated, he said: Khalid ibn Abdullah narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Mujahid, from Yazid ibn Shajarah, that he said: A blessing from Allah has come upon you, of yellow, green and red, and in the houses is what is in them; so when you meet the enemy tomorrow, then forward, forward. For the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No servant takes a step forward in the path of Allah but the wide-eyed houris look upon him; if he retreats, they veil themselves from him; if he is killed, the first drop falling of his blood is expiation for his sins, and two of the wide-eyed houris come to him, with each one seventy garments, not passing beyond her fingertip (?), dusting the dust from him, saying: Welcome, your time has come; and he says: Welcome, your time has come. [Flag: one garment phrase is obscure in the source; translated literally.]"
"3742","2565 - Saeed narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, he said: In Paradise is a house entered only by a prophet, or a truthful one, or a martyr, or a just ruler, or one given choice between killing and disbelief who chose killing."
"3743","2566 - Saeed narrated, he said: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Buhayr ibn Sa'd, from Khalid ibn Ma'dan, from Kathir ibn Murrah, from Nuaym ibn Hammar, that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - and a man came to him, saying: Which martyrs are most excellent? He said: Those who are met in the ranks and do not turn their faces until they are killed; those are the ones who revel in the highest chambers of Paradise; your Lord laughs to them, and when your Lord laughs to a servant in a place, there is no reckoning upon him."
"3744","2567 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Mujahid, from Yazid ibn Shajarah, he said: He used to preach, and his deed confirmed his word; he used to say: Swords are the keys of Paradise. And he used to say: When the two ranks meet in the path of Allah and prayer is established, the wide-eyed houris descend and look on; when a man advances, they say: O Allah, make him firm; O Allah, aid him; O Allah, help him. When he retreats, they veil themselves from him, saying: O Allah, forgive him. When he is killed, he is forgiven with the first drop issuing of his blood for every sin of his, and two of the wide-eyed houris descend to him, wiping the dust from his face, saying: Your time has come; and he says: Your time has come."
"3745","2568 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, from Shubah, from Umarah ibn Abi Hafsah, from Hujr al-Hajari, from Saeed ibn Jubayr, about His saying: Then all in the heavens and on earth shall swoon except whom Allah wills - he said: The martyrs are the exception of Allah, around the Throne, wearing swords."
"3746","2569 - Saeed narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: al-Awwam informed me, from one who narrated to him, from Abu Hurayrah, about His saying: Then all in the heavens and on earth shall be terrified except whom Allah wills - he said: They are the martyrs."
"3747","2570 - Saeed narrated, he said: Khalid narrated to us, from al-Awwam, from Abdullah ibn Abi al-Hudhayl, he said: The prophets shall intercede on the Day of Resurrection, then the martyrs shall intercede; each martyr interceding for forty."
"3748","Chapter: Who is wounded in the path of Allah. 2571 - Saeed narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No one is wounded in the path of Allah - and Allah knows best who is wounded in His path - but he shall come on the Day of Resurrection with his wound flowing blood; the blood the colour of blood, the scent the scent of musk."
"3749","2572 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated to us, from his father, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No one is wounded in the path of Allah - and Allah knows best who is wounded in His path - but he shall come on the Day of Resurrection, the colour the colour of blood, the scent the scent of musk."
"3750","2573 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me that Umar ibn al-Saib narrated to him that it reached him that Malik, the father of Abu Saeed al-Khudri, when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was wounded on the day of Uhud, sucked his wound until he cleaned it and it showed white. It was said to him: Spit it out. He said: By Allah, I shall never spit it out. Then he turned to fight. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever wishes to look at a man of the people of Paradise, let him look at this one. So he was martyred."
"3751","Chapter: Washing the martyr and the garments he is shrouded in. 2574 - Saeed narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Mughirah informed me, from Ibrahim, he said: If a man is killed in battle, he is not washed nor embalmed, and is shrouded in his clothes in an odd number of them; removed from him is whatever fur or boots were upon him. But if he is carried with life still in him, he is washed, embalmed, and prayed over."
"3752","2575 - Saeed narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ayyub al-Tai, from Qays ibn Muslim, from Tariq ibn Shihab, that Sa'd ibn Ubayd al-Qari - who was called in the time of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) al-Qari - was killed on the day of al-Qadisiyyah, and he had said to them: Do not wash blood from me, nor remove a garment from me except leather."
"3753","2576 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abu Waki narrated to us, from Qays ibn Muslim, from Tariq ibn Shihab, he said: Sa'd ibn Ubayd addressed us at al-Qadisiyyah, saying: We shall meet the enemy tomorrow, Allah willing, and I do not see myself but martyred; so do not remove a garment from me except boots."
"3754","2577 - Saeed narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Mansur and Yunus informed me, from al-Hasan, that he used to say about the martyr: He is washed."
"3755","2578 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abu al-Ahwas informed me, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: Removed from the slain are the fur, the two boots, the two wide sleeves (?), and the two socks, unless the two socks complete an odd number, in which case they are left upon him; and he is buried in his clothes. [Flag: one garment term is obscure in the source; translated literally.]"
"3756","2579 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Umarah ibn Umayr, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, he said: We went out in an army toward Persia, among them Alqamah ibn Qays, Midad al-Ijli, Yazid ibn Muawiyah al-Nakhai, and Amr ibn Utbah ibn Farqad. We besieged a castle; with us was a sick companion, so we dug him a grave. Yazid ibn Muawiyah saw as though a small white gazelle until it was buried in that grave - and Yazid was fair and slight - so he kept exposing himself to the castle, and a stone struck him and killed him. We brought him and buried him in that grave. Amr ibn Utbah went out exposing himself to the castle, wearing a new white cloak, saying: How lovely is the running of blood upon it! A stone struck him and killed him, and blood ran upon his cloak, so we buried him. Midad went out exposing himself to the castle, and a stone struck him and wounded him; he kept wiping it with his hand, saying: It is small, and Allah, Mighty and Majestic, blesses the small. He died of it, and we buried him."
"3757","2580 - Saeed narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: al-Aswad ibn Qays narrated to us, from Nubayh al-Anazi, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered that the slain of Uhud be returned to their places of falling after they had been carried to Madinah."
"3758","2581 - Saeed narrated, he said: Salih ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Mansur narrated to us, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, he said: We campaigned in Khurasan in the time of Muawiyah; we were besieging a fortress of Khwarizm, staying two years praying two rakahs, not fasting the obligatory fast. With us was Midad al-Ijli, wearing a white cloak of his, saying: How lovely is the trace of blood upon this cloak! His words were no sooner spoken than we were shot at with the catapult from the fortress; a piece broke into three fragments, one fragment struck him. He kept touching it, saying: It is small, and Allah makes in the small much good. We turned back with him, and he died. Alqamah used to wear that cloak at Kufah, having washed the trace of blood from it, though its trace remained, saying: The memory of the blood of Midad in it endears to me the wearing of this cloak."
"3759","Chapter: What is reported about practice in burial. 2582 - Saeed narrated, he said: Ismail ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from Hisham ibn Amir al-Ansari, he said: They complained to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of wounds on the day of Uhud, asking: How do you command us with our slain? He said: Dig, widen, do well, bury two or three in one grave, and advance the one with most Quran. Hisham said: So my father was advanced before two men."
"3760","2583 - Saeed narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: I heard al-Zuhri, but did not master it; Mamar said: He narrated from Ibn Suayr or Ibn Abi Suayr that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) looked upon the slain of Uhud, saying: I have borne witness over these; so wrap them with their blood and wounds."
"3761","2584 - Saeed narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed me, from al-Zuhri, he said: Abdullah ibn Thalabah ibn Suayr informed me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said about the slain of Uhud: Wrap them with their blood, and advance the one with most Quran."
"3762","Chapter: What is reported about conquests. 2585 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad, from his father, that Abu Musa, when he conquered Tustar, sent to Umar ibn al-Khattab. The messenger found Umar in a garden. He said: So I uttered takbir until I entered the garden, and Umar uttered takbir; then I uttered takbir and Umar uttered takbir. When I reached him, I informed him of the conquest of Tustar. He said: Was there any strange news? I said: A man of us disbelieved after his Islam. He said: So what did you do with him? He said: I said: We brought him forward and struck his neck. He said: O Allah, I did not see, nor witness, nor approve when it reached me. Would that you had shut him in a house, bringing him each day a loaf, that he might repent and return. Then he said: How do you deal with fortresses? I said: We draw near them; when a stone is thrown, we say: It crushes its fellow whom it strikes. He said: I would not love that a town of a thousand be opened at the loss of a Muslim man."
"3763","2586 - Saeed narrated, he said: Yaqub narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, from his father, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab sent Abu Musa al-Ashari to Basrah, and sent Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas to Kufah. When Abu Musa conquered Tustar, Abu Musa wrote to Umar to make it of the district of Basrah, and Sa'd wrote to Umar to make it of the district of Kufah. The messenger of Abu Musa arrived first - Majzaah ibn Thawr or Shaqiq ibn Thawr - and asked about the Commander of the Believers; it was said he was in a garden. He came to him; when he saw him, the messenger uttered takbir, and Umar uttered takbir. He said: O Commander of the Believers, shall Tustar be of the district of Basrah? He said: Yes, it is of the district of Basrah. He handed him the letter. Umar said to him: Inform me of the condition of the people. He said: A man of the Arabs apostatized from Islam, so we brought him near and struck his neck. He said: Would that you had put him in a house, sealing it upon him for three days, then throwing to him each day a loaf, that he might return. O Allah, I did not witness, nor command, nor approve when it reached me."
"3764","2587 - Saeed narrated, he said: Khalid ibn Abdullah narrated to us, from Dawud, from Amir, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: Six men of Bakr ibn Wail apostatized on the day of Tustar. I came to Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, and he asked me, saying: What became of the men? I turned to other talk, then he said: What became of the men? I said: They were killed. He said: Had I reached them, it would have been more beloved to me than what the sun rises upon. He said: I said to him: And what was their way but killing? He said: I would have offered them to enter by the door from which they went out; if they did, well and good, otherwise I would have lodged them in prison."
"3765","2588 - Saeed narrated, he said: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata al-Khurasani narrated to me: He said: Tustar was by treaty, and its people broke faith, so the Emigrants campaigned against them. The Muslims took their women, until they bore them children; I saw some of their children among them. Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, ordered whoever of them was named, and they returned them to their jizyah, separating them from their masters."
"3766","2589 - Saeed narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: al-Awwam ibn Hawshab informed me, he said: Ibrahim al-Taymi narrated to us, he said: When the Muslims opened al-Sawad, they said to Umar ibn al-Khattab: Divide it among us. He refused. They said: We opened it by force. He said: Then what for those Muslims who come after you? I fear you will dispute among yourselves over waters, and I fear you will fight one another. So he left the people of al-Sawad in their lands, striking upon their heads taxes - meaning the jizyah - and upon their land the tasq - meaning the kharaj - and did not divide it among them."
"3767","2590 - Saeed narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, he said: Whatever city is opened by force, and its people embrace Islam before it is divided, they are free, and their wealth belongs to the Muslims."
"3768","2591 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, that Amr ibn al-As entered Egypt with three thousand five hundred men. Umar had feared for him when he informed him, so he sent al-Zubayr with twelve thousand, and he reached him. Al-Zubayr witnessed the conquest of Egypt, and al-Zubayr marked out plots at al-Fustat."
"3769","Chapter: Who embraces Islam and stays in his land or leaves it. 2592 - Saeed narrated, he said: Jarir narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: If a man of the people of al-Sawad embraces Islam and stays in his land, kharaj is taken from him; if he leaves his land, kharaj is lifted from him."
"3770","2593 - Saeed narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Sayyar, from al-Zubayr ibn Adi, that a dihqan embraced Islam in the time of Ali, and Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said to him: If you stay in your land, we shall lift the jizyah from your head and take it from your land; if you move, we are more entitled to it."
"3771","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: In the book of Mu'adh: 'Whoever subjugates a people' - Ibn al-Mubarak said: meaning whoever enslaves a people whose first members were free and weak neighbours - 'then whoever of them remains confined in his house until Islam enters his house is a slave, and whoever was left to himself paying the kharaj is free; and whichever slave flees to the Muslim woman as a Muslim is free.'"
"3772","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from 'Abdullah ibn Dinar, he said: 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz wrote: 'Whoever of the people of the land accepts Islam keeps what he accepted Islam upon, of family and wealth; as for his land and dwelling, it remains part of Allah's fay' for the Muslims.'"
"3773","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn al-Harith, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from 'Awf ibn Hittan: he had some people from Egypt, among them a beardless youth; between . and . Balhib there was a covenant; and when 'Umar ibn al-Khattab heard that, he wrote to 'Amr ibn al-'As: 'Order that they be given the choice: if they enter Islam, that is it; and if they refuse, return them to their villages.' [Flag: the clause about Balhib is garbled/truncated in the source]"
"3774","Chapter on gesturing to the polytheists and fulfilling the covenant. Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, from 'Umar ibn Abi Salamah, from his father, he said: 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, said: 'By Allah, if one of you gestured with his finger to the sky to a polytheist, and he came down to him on that basis, then he killed him, I would kill him for him.'"
"3775","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Marwan ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn 'Ubaydah al-Rabadhi narrated to us, from Talhah ibn 'Ubaydullah ibn Kurayz al-Khuza'i, he said: 'Umar ibn al-Khattab said: 'Whichever Muslim man gestures with his finger to the sky and calls a man of the polytheists, and he comes down, then if he says: By Allah I will surely kill you, he is safe; he only comes down under Allah's covenant and pact.'"
"3776","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu Shihab narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Wa'il Shaqiq ibn Salamah, he said: The letter of 'Umar ibn al-Khattab reached us while we were at Khanqin, at the crescent of Ramadan; some of us were fasting and some were not, and neither the fasting blamed the non-fasting nor the non-fasting the fasting: 'The crescents, some are greater than others; when you see the crescent in daytime, do not break the fast until two witnesses testify that they saw it yesterday; and when you besiege the people of a fortress and they ask you to bring them down under Allah's judgement, do not bring them down under Allah's judgement, for you do not know what Allah's judgement concerning them is, but bring them down under your own judgement, then judge concerning them as you wish; and when you say: no harm, or la tadhal, or matras, you have granted them safety, for Allah knows tongues.'"
"3777","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Shaqiq, with this hadith, he said: When a man says to a man: fear not, he has granted him safety; and when he says: matras, he has granted him safety; and when he says: la tadhal, he has granted him safety, for Allah knows tongues."
"3778","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Jami' ibn Abi Rashid narrated to us, from Maymun ibn Mihran, he said: 'Three things are rendered to the righteous and the wicked: the covenant, which you fulfil to the righteous and the wicked; kinship, which you join whether it is righteous or wicked; and the trust, which you render to the righteous and the wicked.'"
"3779","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from 'Umarah ibn 'Umayr, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, he said: I went out in an army in which was Salman; we besieged a castle and granted them safety, and we opened the castle, and we left therein a sick companion of ours, then we departed; then after us came an army of the people of Basra who did not know of our grant of safety. He said to them: your companions granted us safety, but they did not accept that from him; they opened the castle by force, killed the sick man, then carried the offspring until they brought them to Salman al-Farisi at the camp. Salman said to them: 'Carry the offspring and return them to the castle; as for the blood, 'Umar will judge concerning it.'"
"3780","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, from Mansur, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, from a man of Thaqif, from a man of Juhaynah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'Perhaps you will fight a people and prevail over them, and they will shield themselves from you with their wealth rather than their lives and children, and make peace with you on a settlement; then take nothing from them beyond that, for it is not lawful for you.' He said: So I accompanied the Juhani to the land of the Romans, and I never saw a man more careful about the land, that he take anything from it, than him."
"3781","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Khalid ibn Abi 'Imran, that 'Amir ibn 'Abdullah al-Yahsabi narrated to him that a man came to him with a feed-bag containing grass or straw which he had taken from one of the dhimmis. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to the man: 'What is this?' He said: I took it, and it is nothing. He said: 'Have you violated my protection, have you violated my protection, have you violated the protection of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)?' He said: So the man went and gave it to its owner, then came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and told him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'Did you not need what you took from him?' He said: Yes indeed. He said: 'Then its owner needs it more.'"
"3782","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: 'Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: al-Awza'i narrated to us, he said: Ibn Suraqah narrated to me that Abu 'Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah wrote for the people of Dayr Tayaya: This is a writ from Abu 'Ubaydah for the people of Dayr Tayaya: I have granted you safety for your blood, your wealth, and your churches, that they not be inhabited nor destroyed, so long as you do not innovate anything nor shelter an innovator who deceives; if you innovate or shelter a deceiving innovator, the protection is free of you; and upon you is the entertainment of the guest for three days; and our protection is free of the army's harm. Witnessed Khalid ibn al-Walid, Yazid ibn Abi Sufyan, Shurahbil ibn Hasanah, and Quda'i ibn 'Amir."
"3783","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Suqah, he said: I was sitting with 'Ata' ibn Abi Rabah when a man came to him and said: O Abu Muhammad, a man captured by the Daylam; they took a covenant from him that he bring them such-and-such wealth, otherwise he return to them; so they released him, but he found nothing. He said: 'He must fulfil the covenant to them.' He said: They are polytheists! But he insisted that he fulfil the covenant to them."
"3784","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Khalid and Hushaym narrated to us, from Husayn, from Abu 'Atiyyah al-Hamdani, that 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, wrote: 'Matras is safety; whoever you say it to is safe.'"
"3785","Chapter on the slave's grant of safety. Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu Shihab narrated to us, from 'Asim al-Ahwal, from Fudayl ibn Zayd al-Raqashi, he said: We besieged a fortress in the time of 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, and a slave of ours shot an arrow containing safety; they came out, so we said: What brought you out? They said: You granted us safety. We said: That was only a slave, and we do not validate his act. They said: We do not distinguish your slave from your freeman. So we wrote to 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, asking him about that, and he wrote: 'The slave is a man of the Muslims; your protection is their protection.'"
"3786","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, he said: 'Asim al-Ahwal narrated to us, from Fudayl ibn Zayd: 'A slave granted safety to a people, and 'Umar validated his grant of safety.'"
"3787","Chapter on a woman granting protection over a people. Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn 'Ubaydullah, from Sa'id ibn Abi Hind, that Abu Murrah, the freedman of 'Aqil ibn Abi Talib, informed him that Umm Hani' bint Abi Talib informed him that she granted protection to two men of Banu Makhzum on the day the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) conquered Mecca. 'Ali ibn Abi Talib came to her and said: What is this, O Umm Hani'? I will surely kill them both! She said: So I shut them in, then I went to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and found him bathing while his daughter Fatimah screened him with a garment. He bathed, then took the garment and wrapped himself in it, then prayed eight rak'ahs of Duha, then said: 'What is the matter with you, O Umm Hani'?' I said: I have granted protection to two men of my in-laws, and 'Ali has come wanting to kill them. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'We have granted safety to whom you granted safety, and granted protection to whom you granted protection.'"
"3788","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu Shihab narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad ibn Yazid, from 'A'ishah: 'A woman would indeed grant protection over the Muslims and it would be valid.'"
"3789","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, he said: On the day of the Conquest, Umm Hani' bint Abi Talib came and said: O Messenger of Allah, I have granted protection to my in-laws and shut them in, but my mother's son wants to kill them. The Messenger of Allah said to her: 'We have granted protection to whom you granted protection, O Umm Hani'; the lowest of the Muslims grants protection over them.' Then he came to her, performed ablution at her place, then wrapped himself in his garment and prayed eight rak'ahs."
"3790","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'idah narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman, from 'Ata' ibn Abi Rabah, he said: Thumamah ibn Uthal was brought as a captive to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: 'If we wish we kill you, if we wish we ransom you, if we wish we free you, and if we wish you accept Islam.' He said: If you join ties you join a great one, if you take ransom you take a great one, if you free you free a great one; but if I accept Islam under compulsion, no. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) freed him; then he accepted Islam and said: O Messenger of Allah, no grain nor date shall be carried to Quraysh until Allah and His Messenger permit. So Quraysh wrote to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asking him by their kinship, saying: You command joining kinship, yet we have perished and our dependents have perished. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote to Thumamah: 'Leave for Allah's sanctuary and its security their supplies, and do not withhold from them.' So he carried to them."
"3791","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Zakariyya narrated to us, from Khanas ibn Sulaym al-'Abdi, from a man of Banu Sa'd ibn Zayd Manat, he said: I was with Ibn 'Abbas when a man of the people of al-Rayy asked him: The enemy raids and captures the covenant people and drives off cattle and sheep; the cavalry pursues them and catches them, but they slaughter the cattle and sheep and have intercourse with the covenant women's women. Ibn 'Abbas said: 'The Muslim restores to the Muslim, and the Muslim restores to the covenant people; and whoever has intercourse with a dhimmi woman is an adulterer.'"
"3792","Chapter on what equals martyrdom. Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Salih ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Mansur narrated to us, from Mujahid, from 'Abdullah ibn Salam, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) visited him during an illness he fell ill with, and some of his companions said: We had hoped for a different death for you, O Ibn Sallam! The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'What were you hoping for him?' They hesitated to answer him, then 'Abdullah ibn Salam said: They say: being killed in the way of: martyrdom. He said: 'The martyrs of my ummah would then be few; killing is indeed part of martyrdom, and collapsing, drowning, burning, belly pain, the woman dying in childbirth, and the plague.'"
"3793","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, from Abu Bakr ibn Hafs ibn 'Umar ibn Sa'd, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: 'Whom do you count as martyrs of my ummah?' They said: Whoever is killed in the way of Allah. He said: 'The martyrs of my ummah would then be few.' Then he mentioned the plague, and mentioned those burned, and mentioned those drowned, and mentioned the belly, and mentioned the woman who dies bijum' (pregnant)."
"3794","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Muhajir, from Tariq ibn Shihab, he said: The martyrs were mentioned before Ibn Mas'ud, and they said: Martyrdom is being killed. 'Abdullah said: 'Your martyrs would then be few.' Then 'Abdullah said: 'Whoever drowns in the sea, falls from mountains, or is eaten by beasts is a martyr with Allah on the Day of Resurrection.'"
"3795","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, he said: al-A'mash narrated to us, from Abu al-Mukhariq, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out in the expedition of Tabuk and his she-camel strayed, so he stayed with her seven (days). He passed by some of his companions talking, saying: We have never seen like today a stronger or more powerful man; if only this were in the way of Allah! The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard it and said: 'If he strives for his small children to enrich them, he is in the way of Allah; if he strives for his parents to enrich them, he is in the way of Allah; if he strives for himself to enrich it and suffice people, he is in the way of Allah; but if he strives for repute and show, he is for Satan.'"
"3796","Chapter on gentleness to beasts in travel. Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu Shihab narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Hassan, from al-Hasan, he said: It used to be said: 'When there is plenty, give the mounts their due in the resting place; and if there is drought, save yourselves with the mounts; and upon you is travelling at night, for the earth is folded up at night.'"
"3797","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn 'Ajlan, from Aban ibn Salih, from Khalid ibn Ma'dan, raising it, he said: 'Allah, Mighty and Majestic, is Gentle and loves gentleness, and aids through it what He does not aid through harshness. When you ride these dumb beasts, bring them down at their proper stops on the earth; if the land is barren, move swiftly upon them while they have marrow; and beware of camping on the roads, for they are the abode of snakes and beasts.'"
"3798","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Khalid narrated to us, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'I employ a man though another is dearer to me than him, because he is more wakeful of eye, stronger in stratagem, and better in travel gear; and I give him though another is dearer to me than him, conciliating him.'"
"3799","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'There is nothing better than a thousand like it, of man.' [Flag: the pronoun reference in the source is obscure]"
"3800","Chapter on killing women and children. Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Mughirah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Khuzami narrated to us, from Abu al-Zinad, he said: Muraqqi' ibn Sayfi narrated to me, he said: My grandfather Rabah ibn al-Rabi', the brother of Hanzalah the scribe, informed me that he was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in an expedition, with Khalid ibn al-Walid over its vanguard. Rabah and his companions passed by a slain woman struck down by the vanguard, and they stood over her marvelling at her. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came on his she-camel; when he came they parted from the woman, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood over her and looked at her and said: 'Was this one fighting? Was there no one of mature judgement among the people?' Then he said to a man: 'Catch up with Khalid: let him not kill offspring nor a hireling.'"
"3801","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj informed us, he said: Qatadah narrated to us, from al-Hasan, from Samurah ibn Jundub, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'Kill the old men of the polytheists and spare their young.'"
"3802","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Jarir narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Zayd ibn Wahb, he said: 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, wrote: 'Do not steal from the booty, do not treacherously break faith, do not mutilate, do not kill a child, and fear Allah concerning the farmers who do not wage war against you.'"
"3803","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Juwaybir informed us, from al-Dahhak, he said: 'The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade killing women and children, except one who charges with the sword.'"
"3804","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn Ka'b ibn Malik, from his uncle, he said: 'The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade killing women and children when he sent (the expedition) against Ibn Abi al-Huqayq.'"
"3805","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from a man, from his father, he said: 'The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade killing the 'usafa' and the wusafa' (aged hirelings and servants)."
"3806","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Maryam, from Damrah ibn Habib, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) 'forbade killing women, children and old men, and hamstringing a beast that stands firm in the way of Allah.'"
"3807","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ibn Mas'ud, that his nephew overpowered him in an expedition he campaigned, and he said: 'Perhaps you burned a crop?' He said: Yes. He said: 'Perhaps you drowned date-palms?' He said: Yes. He said: 'Perhaps you killed a woman or a child?' He said: Yes. He said: 'Let your expedition be neutral (profitless).'"
"3808","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Abdullah, from Ibn 'Abbas, from al-Sa'b ibn Jaththamah, he said: I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - or I heard him asked - about the households of the polytheists raided by night, so that their women and offspring are hit. He said: 'They are of them.'"
"3809","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu Shihab narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj, from Nafi', from Aslam the freedman of 'Umar, that 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, used to write to the commanders of the armies that they kill none but him upon whom the razor runs (i.e. adult males), take jizyah from none but him upon whom the razor runs, and take nothing from a child nor a woman."
"3810","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, from al-Hasan ibn Muhammad, he said: 'A man would even meet the polytheist's child with his spear.' [Flag: the sense appears to censure this practice in context of the chapter]"
"3811","Chapter on killing monks and deacons. Sa'id narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Bakr ibn Sawadah, that he said: 'We never saw the armies disturb the monks upon the pillars, and we continued to be forbidden from killing them unless they fight.'"
"3812","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from Safwan ibn 'Amr, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from his father, from Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, that he used to kill the deacons of the enemy, saying: 'For me to kill one man of them is dearer than to kill seventy others, for Allah, Mighty and Majestic, says: {So fight the leaders of disbelief; indeed they have no oaths}.'"
"3813","Chapter on the prohibition of plunder. Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, from 'Asim ibn Kulayb, from his father, from a man of the Prophet's companions (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: We campaigned with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and hunger struck us; Allah granted us conquest and we took sheep, and the people plundered. We took a sheep from them while it boiled in our pots, when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to us walking on his bow until he prodded our pots with the bow and overturned them, saying: 'Plunder is no more lawful than carrion.' He kept looking at a bone raised from the earth and trampling it with his bow until he buried it in dust."
"3814","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, from Tha'labah ibn al-Hakam, he said: We took enemy sheep and plundered them, and set up our pots. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by the pots while they boiled, ordered them overturned, then said to them: 'Plunder is not lawful.'"
"3815","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Abdullah, that a man slaughtered a camel in the land of the Romans; when it cooled he said: O people, take from the slaughter of this camel; we have permitted you. Makhul said: O Ghassani, will you not bring us some meat of this camel? The Ghassani said: O Abu 'Abdullah, what do you think about plunder in it? Makhul said: 'There is no plunder in what is permitted.'"
"3816","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Maryam, from 'Atiyyah ibn Qays, he said: 'When we went out in a detachment and took sheep, the imam's caller would call: whoever wishes to take something of these sheep, let him take; for we cannot drive them.'"
"3817","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj, from al-Hakam, from al-Sha'bi, he said: 'The plunder which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade is only what is taken without the owner's goodwill; but its practice is not good.' Al-Hakam said: Ibrahim used to dislike it."
"3818","Chapter on burning and cutting date-palms. Sa'id narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn al-Harith informed me that Bukayr narrated to him, he said: I heard Sulayman ibn Yasar saying: 'The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) appointed Usamah ibn Zayd over an army and ordered him to burn in Yubna.'"
"3819","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Musa ibn 'Uqbah, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, 'that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cut the date-palms of Banu al-Nadir and burned; and concerning it Hassan says: Sarat; and about that was revealed: {Whatever you cut of date-palms or left standing} the verse.' [Flag: the word 'Sarat' is the remnant of a verse of Hassan quoted in the source]"
"3820","Chapter on the dislike of tormenting with fire. Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Mughirah narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Rahman al-Hizami, from Abu al-Zinad, he said: Muhammad ibn Hamzah al-Aslami narrated to me, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) appointed him over a detachment. I went out in it and he said: 'If you take so-and-so, burn him with fire.' I turned away, then he called me and I returned. He said: 'If you take so-and-so, kill him and do not burn him, for none torments with fire but the Lord of the Fire.'"
"3821","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Yunus informed us, from al-Hasan, he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Mu'adh to Yemen, he said to him: 'If Allah enables you over so-and-so, burn him with fire.' When Mu'adh had gone, he called him and said to him: 'If Allah enables you over him, strike his neck; for none may torment with Allah's torment.'"
"3822","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Bukayr, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Abu Hurayrah, that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent us in a detachment and said: 'If you meet so-and-so and so-and-so - two men of Quraysh whom he named - and capture them, burn them with fire.' We came to him bidding him farewell when we wanted to leave, and he said: 'I had ordered you to burn so-and-so and so-and-so with fire; but none torments with fire but Allah, Mighty and Majestic; so if you find them, kill them.'"
"3823","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, that Habbar ibn al-Aswad struck Zaynab bint the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with something while she was in her litter, and she miscarried. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent a detachment and said: 'If you find him, place him between two bundles of firewood, then kindle fire in him.' Then he said: 'I am shy before Allah; none should torment with Allah's torment.' And he said: 'If you find him, cut off his hand, then cut off his leg, then cut off his hand, then cut off his leg.' The detachment did not catch him, and a move to Medina befell him; he accepted Islam, came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and it was said to him: This is Habbar - he is reviled but does not revile; he was a reviling man. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to him walking until he stood over him and said: 'O Habbar, revile whoever reviles you! O Habbar, revile whoever reviles you!'"
"3824","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, he said: Safwan ibn 'Amr and Hariz ibn 'Uthman narrated to me that Junadah ibn Abi Umayyah al-Azdi, 'Abdullah ibn Qays al-Fazari, and others of the sea commanders after them used to shoot the enemy of the Romans and others with fire and burn them - these against those, and those against these."
"3825","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from Safwan ibn 'Amr, from the elders, from 'Abdullah ibn Qays al-Fazari, 'that he used to campaign over the people at sea in the time of Mu'awiyah, shooting the enemy with fire while they shot him, burning them while they burned him; and he said: The affair of the Muslims remained upon that.'"
"3826","Chapter on carrying heads. Sa'id narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Yazid, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from 'Ulayy ibn Rabah, from 'Uqbah ibn 'Amir, that he came to Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, with the head of Yannaq the patrician, and he disapproved of that. He said: O successor of the Messenger of Allah, they do that to us! He said: 'Following Persia and Rome? No head shall be carried to me; the letter and the report suffice.'"
"3827","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Bakr ibn Sawadah, that 'Ali ibn Rabi'ah (?) narrated to him, from 'Uqbah ibn 'Amir al-Juhani, he said: I came to Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, with the first conquest from Syria, with heads. He said: 'What were you to do with these?' And he said: 'Whoever gives you the jizyah, accept it from him; whoever fights you, fight him; you will not be given the jizyah from beyond the pass, the last of what is upon you.' [Flag: the name 'Ali ibn Abi Rabah' is garbled in the source]"
"3828","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Ma'mar, he said: A companion of mine narrated to me, from al-Zuhri, he said: 'No head was ever carried to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), not even on the day of Badr; a head was carried to Abu Bakr and he disapproved of it; and the first to whom heads were carried was 'Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr.'"
"3829","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Ma'mar, from 'Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, he said: 'A head was brought to Abu Bakr, and he said: You have transgressed.' [Flag: the report is truncated in the source]"
"3830","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from Sulayman ibn Sulaym and Abu Bakr, from al-Zuhri, he said: They came to Abu Bakr with the head of Yannaq the patrician and with heads, so Abu Bakr wrote to his governor in Syria: 'Do not send me any head; the letter and the report suffice you.'"
"3831","Chapter on separating captives between parent and child and relatives. Sa'id narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, from his father, that when captives were brought to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he would line them up, then stand looking at them; if a woman wept, he would say to her: 'What makes you weep?' She would say: My son was sold, my daughter was sold; so he would return (the child) to her. Abu Usayd al-Sa'idi came to him with captives; they were lined up for him, then he stood looking at them and saw a woman weeping. He said: What makes you weep? She said: My son was sold among Banu 'Abs. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'You shall surely ride and bring him to me as you sold him.' So Abu Usayd rode and brought him."
"3832","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from 'Ata', that 'Umar ibn al-Khattab used to forbid separating close relatives."
"3833","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Layla, from al-Hakam, that 'Ali separated (captives), so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'Catch up, catch up!' [Flag: the report is terse/truncated in the source]"
"3834","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, from 'Abdullah ibn Farrukh, from his father, that he said: 'Umar ibn al-Khattab wrote to us: 'Do not separate two brothers, nor a mother and her child, in sale.' And Sufyan once said: Nafi' ibn 'Abd al-Harith wrote to me of that. [Flag: the name 'Abdullah ibn Farrukh' is uncertain in the source]"
"3835","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Sulayman al-Taymi informed us, from Tulayq ibn Muhammad ibn 'Imran, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'Cursed is whoever separates.'"
"3836","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn 'Ubayd informed us, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from Hakim ibn 'Iqal, that 'Uthman ibn 'Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, wrote to him to buy for him a hundred households, then send them to him; and he wrote to him not to buy among them anyone separating him from his mother or his father. [Flag: the report breaks off in the source]"
"3837","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Safwan ibn 'Amr, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from his father, he said: When the cities of Cyprus were conquered, the people fell to dividing the captives, separating them while some wept over others. Abu al-Darda' withdrew, then sat leaning on his sword-harness, weeping. Jubayr ibn Nufayr came to him and said: What makes you weep, O Abu al-Darda'? Do you weep on a day in which Allah honoured Islam and its people and humiliated disbelief and its people? He struck his shoulder and said: 'May your mother be bereaved of you, O Jubayr ibn Nufayr! How insignificant creation is to Allah when they abandon His command! While it was a dominant ummah, outwardly victorious over people, with sovereignty, until they abandoned Allah's command and became what you see; and when captivity is unleashed upon a people, they have left Allah's sight - Allah has no need of them.'"
"3838","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu Shihab narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from 'Abdullah ibn al-Hasan, from his mother Fatimah bint Husayn, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Zayd ibn Harithah to the city of Maqna - Sa'id said: Maqna is Madyan - and he took captives from them, among them Dumayrah the freedman of 'Ali. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered them sold; he went out to them while they wept, and said to them: 'Why do they weep?' They said: We separated them though they are brothers. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'Do not separate them; sell them together.'"
"3839","Chapter on the captive invited to Islam and other matters. Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from Safwan ibn 'Amr, from Damrah ibn Habib, that a man of the polytheists had spat upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Mecca. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to threaten him: if Allah gives me power over him I will surely kill him. While he had one day sent a detachment, a herald came informing him that Allah had made their trial good and honoured their victory, and: I inform you, O Messenger of Allah, that Allah has enabled (us) over so-and-so. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rejoiced at that. They brought him bound in irons; when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw him, he called for a sword and unsheathed it, then removed his cloak from his shoulder, then stood over him brandishing the sword and said: 'Bring him near me.' They brought him near, and he said: 'What do you think, O enemy of Allah - Allah has enabled (us) over you?' He said: Yes; do not kill me, for I testify that there is no god but Allah alone without partner, and that you are the Messenger of Allah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned away swiftly, returning until he sat in his seat, placed his cloak over him, sheathed the sword, then said: 'Free him; my Lord has forbidden me to kill those who pray.'"
"3840","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Mu'tamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent a group to Khath'am; when they saw the Prophet's companions (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) surrounding them, they resorted to prostration, but some of them were killed in that state. The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered for them half the blood-money for their prayer, and said: 'I am free of every Muslim with a polytheist.' It was said: Why, O Messenger of Allah? He said: 'Their two fires should not see each other.'"
"3841","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Mu'tamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from his father, from Abu 'Uthman al-Nahdi, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'Which man fears not in Allah the blame of a blamer?' Al-Dahhak stood and said: I, O Messenger of Allah. Then the Prophet of Allah repeated: 'Which man fears not in Allah the blame of a blamer?' Al-Dahhak stood, and he commanded him with his order, ordering him to kill the fighters. He was a man, either Yahsabi or Muharibi, watering with them at the water; he was virtuous. The army captured two sons of his and took camels of his. He came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Prophet of Allah, I am a Muslim man. He said: 'No, by Allah, until our mother (?) no longer waters with them, and their two fires do not see each other; by Allah you shall not take them until you bring such-and-such.' [Flag: the water clause is garbled in the source]"
"3842","Chapter on killing captives and the prohibition of mutilation. Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from Abu Bakr ibn 'Abdullah, from 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz, that a Magian captive from the land of Persia was brought to him; while 'Umar conversed with him, he said: By Allah, many a Muslim man have I killed! So 'Umar ordered him and his neck was struck, saying: 'I shall not spare him for what he said.'"
"3843","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from Umayyah ibn Yazid al-Qurashi, that a Muslim man came with a captive, his hand bound to his neck, to Habib ibn Maslamah while he was at his lunch. Habib said to him: 'Sit and partake of this lunch.' He sat and took a meat-bone and handed it to the captive. Habib saw him and said: 'What is wrong with you? May Allah kill you - you meant to make his blood forbidden to us!'"
"3844","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn al-Harith, from Bukayr ibn al-Ashajj, from Ibn Ya'la, that he said: We campaigned with 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Khalid ibn al-Walid, and four stout men of the enemy were brought; he ordered them killed patiently (bound) with arrows. That reached Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbidding killing by sabr (binding and shooting); by Him in whose hand is my soul, were it a chicken I would not kill it by sabr. That reached 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Khalid ibn al-Walid, so he freed four slaves."
"3845","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Mujalid informed us, from al-Sha'bi, he said: 'The captives on the day of Badr were seventy-one, and the slain sixty-nine. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered 'Uqbah ibn Abi Mu'ayt and his neck was struck; so the slain were seventy and the captives seventy.'"
"3846","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from al-Awza'i, from al-Zuhri, that 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, 'was brought captives and divided them, killing none of them.'"
"3847","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Humayd al-Tawil informed us, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: When Abu Musa conquered Tustar, al-Hurmuzan was brought captive. I brought him to 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, who said to him: 'What is the matter with you?' Al-Hurmuzan said: Shall I speak with a dead tongue or a living tongue? He said to him: 'Speak; no harm.' Al-Hurmuzan said: We and you, O Arabs, while Allah left us to you, you had no power over us; but when Allah was with you, we had no power over you. He ordered him killed, but Anas ibn Malik said: There is no way to that; you have granted him safety. He said: No indeed; rather you took a bribe from him, and did this and that! I said: O Commander of the Faithful, there is no way to killing him. He said: 'Woe to you - am I to spare him after his killing al-Bara' ibn Malik and Majza'ah ibn Thawr?' Then 'Umar said: Bring proof of what you say. Al-Zubayr ibn al-'Awwam said to him: I said to him: Speak; no harm. So 'Umar averted killing from him; he accepted Islam, and 'Umar assigned him a stipend of one or two thousand - the doubt is from Hushaym."
"3848","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from Sulayman ibn Sulaym, from Yahya ibn Jabir, he said: Husayn ibn Numayr al-Sakuni, while commanding the people in the land of the Romans, was brought a captive while at his lunch; one of the people handed him a meat-bone. Husayn saw him eating and said: 'How shall we kill him while our food is between his teeth?' So he freed him."
"3849","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Yusuf, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'Let none of you take his companion's captive when he seizes him and then kill him.'"
"3850","Chapter on the Prophet's share (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the choice portion. Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Mutarrif al-Harithi informed us, he said: I asked al-Sha'bi about the Prophet's share (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the choice portion. He said: 'As for the share, his share was like the share of a Muslim man; as for the choice portion, it was a choice item he selected from the booty.'"
"3851","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Mutarrif, from al-Sha'bi, he said: He was asked about the choice portion. He said: 'It is a superior part of the wealth which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) chose.'"
"3852","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Khalid al-Hadhdha' informed us, from Ibn Sirin, 'that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) chose Safiyyah bint Huyayy on the day of Khaybar.'"
"3853","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Zuhri narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Abi 'Amr, from Anas ibn Malik, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Abu Talhah: 'Seek for me a boy of your boys to serve me' when he went out to Khaybar. Abu Talhah took me out riding behind him; I was a boy nearing puberty. I would serve the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he alighted; I would often hear him say: 'O Allah, I seek refuge in You from worry and grief, incapacity and laziness, miserliness and cowardice, the burden of debt and the domination of men.' Then we came to Khaybar; when Allah opened the fortress, the beauty of Safiyyah bint Huyayy ibn Akhtab was mentioned to him - her husband had been killed and she was a bride - so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) chose her for himself. He went out with her until we reached Sadd al-Sahba'; she became lawful, and he consummated with her. Then he made hays in a small leather mat, then said: 'Invite those around you.' That was the wedding feast of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for Safiyyah. Then we went out to Medina; I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) padding for her behind him with a cloak, then sitting by his camel and placing his knees, so Safiyyah would place her foot upon his knees until she mounted. We travelled until we overlooked Medina; he looked at Uhud and said: 'This is a mountain that loves us and we love it.' Then he looked at Medina and said: 'I declare sacred what is between its two lava plains, as Ibrahim declared Mecca sacred. O Allah, bless them in their mudd and sa'.'"
"3854","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Mughirah informed us, from Ibrahim, he said: 'The fifth is divided into five fifths; the share of Allah and the Messenger is one.'"
"3855","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, from Musa ibn Abi 'A'ishah, he said: I asked Yahya ibn al-Jazzar about the Prophet's share (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of the fifth. He said: 'A fifth of the fifth.'"
"3856","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Ash'ath informed us, from Ibn Sirin, he said: 'The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to be struck a share of the spoils, whether he attended or was absent.'"
"3857","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Khalid al-Hadhdha' informed us, from 'Abdullah ibn Shaqiq, he said: A man of Bulqayn informed me, from a man of them, that he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he besieged Wadi al-Qura and said: O Muhammad, to what do you call? He said: 'To Allah alone.' He said: Is anyone more entitled to this wealth than another? He said: 'A fifth is for Allah and four fifths are for these - meaning his companions - and if an arrow is pulled from your side, you are no more entitled to it than anyone.'"
"3858","Chapter on what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took as extra grant. Sa'id narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated to us, from his father, from 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Abdullah, from Ibn 'Abbas, he said: 'The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took as extra grant his sword Dhu al-Fiqar on the day of Badr.'"
"3859","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, from 'Ikrimah, that 'the sword of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Dhu al-Fiqar, belonged to Abu al-'As ibn Munabbih; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) killed him on the day of Badr and armed himself with it.'"
"3860","Chapter on the practice concerning what the detachment takes. Sa'id narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Abu al-Nadr, that 'Awf ibn Malik al-Ashja'i came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I fear I shall not see you after this day of mine; advise me. He said: 'Upon you is the mountain of al-Khamar?' He said: What is the mountain of al-Khamar? He said: 'The land of the gathering.' He advised him, then said: 'Beware the detachment of extra grant; if they meet they flee, and if they take booty they steal from it.' [Flag: the phrase 'mountain of al-Khamar' is unclear in the source]"
"3861","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Hisham, from al-Hasan, he said: 'When the detachment goes out in secret with the imam's permission, theirs is what they take; and when the detachment goes out in secret without his permission, he takes the fifth from them and they are like the (rest of the) people.'"
"3862","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: 'When the detachment goes out in secret, if the imam wishes he grants them extra, and if he wishes he takes the fifth from them.'"
"3863","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Ash'ath ibn Sawwar informed us, from al-Hasan, he said: 'No detachment goes out in secret except with its commander's permission; and whatever he grants them extra of anything is theirs.'"
"3864","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, concerning the detachment sent out in secret, he said: 'If the imam wishes he grants them extra before the fifth, and if he wishes he takes the fifth from them.'"
"3865","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Yunus informed us, from al-Hasan, he said: 'The imam used to grant extra to a man, and likewise the detachment.'"
"3866","Chapter on extra grant and spoils in raiding and jihad. Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq al-Shaybani narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn 'Ubaydullah al-Thaqafi, from Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, he said: On the day of Badr I killed Sa'id ibn al-'As and took his sword, which was called Dhu al-Katifah. I brought it to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), while my brother 'Utbah had been killed before that. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: 'Go and throw it into the collection.' He said: So I returned, with in me what none but Allah, Mighty and Majestic, knows, of my brother's killing and my spoils taken. I had gone only a short distance when Surat al-Anfal came down; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called me and said: 'Go and take your sword.'"
"3867","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Artah informed us, from Nafi', that Ibn 'Umar duelled a man on the day of al-Yamamah and killed him, and his spoils were yielded to him."
"3868","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid informed us, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, he said: I saw 'Amr ibn Ma'dikarib on the day of al-Qadisiyyah urging the people to fight, saying: O people, be lions; a lion suffices its sheep; the Persian is but a goat - when he throws (his spear) he abandons it. While he was thus, an aswar of the asawirah of Persia aimed at him with an arrow; we said to him: O Abu Thawr, this aswar has aimed his arrow at you. The other shot his arrow and hit the lower end of 'Amr's bow, breaking it. 'Amr charged him, pierced his spine, threw him down, dismounted to him, cut off his hands, and took two bracelets upon him, a brocade outer garment and a girdle; that was yielded to him. [Flag: the exhortation wording is uncertain/garbled in the source]"
"3869","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from al-Aswad ibn Qays, from Shibr ibn 'Alqamah, he said: I duelled a man on the day of al-Qadisiyyah and killed him, and took his spoils. I brought them to Sa'd; Sa'd addressed his companions, then said: 'This spoils of Shibr is better to him than twelve thousand, and we have granted it to him as extra.'"
"3870","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Aswad ibn Qays, he heard a man of his people called Shibr ibn 'Alqamah saying: 'I duelled a man of the people of Persia on the day of al-Qadisiyyah; his spoils reached twelve thousand, and Sa'd granted it to me as extra.'"
"3871","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from 'Ikrimah, that a Jew said on the day of Khaybar: Is there a dueller? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'Go out to him, O Zubayr!' Safiyyah said: My only one, O Messenger of Allah! He said: 'Yes.' So he went out to him and killed him, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave him his spoils."
"3872","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from 'Umar ibn Kathir ibn Aflah, from Abu Muhammad, from Abu Qatadah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) granted him as extra the spoils of a man he killed on the day of Hunayn, without taking the fifth."
"3873","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id informed us, from 'Umar ibn Kathir ibn Aflah, he said: Abu Muhammad al-Ansari, who was a companion of Abu Qatadah, informed me, he said: I heard Abu Qatadah saying: When the Muslims were exposed on the day of Hunayn, I saw 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: What is this? He said: Allah's decree. Then the people rallied afterwards. I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was in a circle of his companions, and heard him saying: 'Whoever establishes proof of a slain one he killed, his spoils are his.' I had seen a polytheist man stalking a Muslim man to kill him; I came to him from behind and struck his hands, cutting them both off. He turned upon me and embraced me; I said: I shall surely die. Then he released me, and I knew he had bled. When he left me, I turned upon him with the sword and struck his neck. I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: 'Whoever establishes proof of a slain one he killed, his spoils are his.' So I stood and looked, saying: Who will testify for me? Then I sat. Then I stood a second time, looked, and said: Who will testify for me? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'What is the matter with you, O Abu Qatadah?' I said: O Messenger of Allah, I cut off a polytheist's hand and killed him, but I have no proof of killing him. A man said: He speaks truth, O Messenger of Allah; the spoils of this one he mentions are with me - or he said: are with me. Abu Bakr said to the man: By Allah, that is not yours - a man fighting for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and for the Muslims. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Abu Bakr speaks truth; hand him his spoils. So I took the spoils; the first orchard I acquired of Medina was from the price of those spoils."
"3874","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from Safwan ibn 'Amr, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from his father, from 'Awf ibn Malik al-Ashja'i, he said: We campaigned an expedition to the edge of Syria; Khalid ibn al-Walid was appointed over us. A man of the reinforcements of Himyar joined us, lodging at our dwellings; he had nothing with him but a sword, no weapon besides it. A Muslim man slaughtered a camel and the man kept scheming until he took from its hide like the shape of a shield, spread it on the ground, then kindled fire upon it until it dried; he made for it a grip like a buckler. It was decreed for us that we met our enemy, among them a mixture of Romans and Arabs of Quda'ah. They fought us fiercely; among the people was a Roman man upon a blond horse, with a gilded saddle, a stained girdle and a like sword. He kept charging the people and inciting them. That reinforcement man kept stalking that Roman until he passed by him; he got behind him, struck his horse's hock with the sword, then it fell, and he followed with sword blows until he killed him. When Allah granted the conquest, he came stripping the spoils, and the people testified for him that he was his killer. Khalid gave him some of his spoils and held back the rest. When he returned to 'Awf's dwelling, he mentioned that to him. 'Awf said: Go back to him; let him give you the rest. He went back to him but he refused him. He walked until he came to Khalid and said: Do you not know that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) decreed the spoils for the killer? He said: Yes indeed. He said: What prevented you from handing him his slain's spoils? Khalid said: I deemed it much for him. 'Awf said: If I see the face of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), I shall surely mention that to him. When he came to Medina, he sent him and sought justice from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He summoned Khalid, with 'Awf sitting, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'What prevented you from handing this man his slain's spoils?' He said: I deemed it much, O Messenger of Allah. He said: 'Hand it to him.' He said: Then he passed by 'Awf, and 'Awf pulled his cloak, then said: I have fulfilled for you what I mentioned to you of the Messenger of Allah's affair (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard him and became angry, saying: 'Do not give him, O Khalid! Do not give him, O Khalid! Will you leave my commanders for me? Your likeness is but that of a man entrusted with camels and sheep; he pastured them, then awaited watering them, brought them to his pond, and they entered it, drinking its clear and leaving its turbid; the clear of their affair is for you, and its turbid upon them.' And when two men dispute over the slain, each saying: I killed him, while the infidel has no life left in him nor either has proof, the spoils are between them; if the infidel still has life, the spoils belong to whom the infidel says killed him."
"3875","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, another time, from Safwan ibn 'Amr, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from his father, from 'Awf ibn Malik al-Ashja'i and Khalid ibn al-Walid, 'that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) decreed the spoils for the killer and did not take the fifth from the spoils.'"
"3876","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Rafi', from al-Zuhri, he said: ''Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, duelled a Jewish man called Marhab, killed him and took his spoils.'"
"3877","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Bukayr ibn Sulayman (?) - he narrated to him - that they were with Mu'awiyah ibn Hudayj in an expedition in the west; he granted the people extra, and with us were companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); none rejected that but Jabalah ibn 'Amr ibn al-Ansari. [Flag: the transmitter name is garbled in the source]"
"3878","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Yazid ibn Jabir, from Makhul, from Ziyad ibn Jariyah, from Habib ibn Maslamah, he said: I witnessed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) granting a third as extra at the outset."
"3879","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Ubayd, from Makhul, from Ziyad ibn Jariyah al-Tamimi, from Habib ibn Maslamah, he said: 'The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) granted extra a third and a fourth.' 'Ubaydullah said: Sulayman ibn Yasar heard me mentioning this hadith and said: 'The fourth at the outset, and the third on the return.'"
"3880","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Tamim, from Makhul, he said: I asked al-Hajjaj ibn 'Abdullah al-Nadri about the extra grant. He said: 'The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) granted extra a third and a fourth.' Nothing prevented me from asking him who ascribes it but reverence for him."
"3881","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) 'granted them extra in a detachment in which they went out toward Najd; they took many camels as booty, and he granted them extra a camel each; their shares were twelve camels, and they had not gone out upon (promise of) extra anything.'"
"3882","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn 'Uthman, from Raja' ibn Haywah, 'Ubadah ibn Nusayy, 'Adi ibn 'Adi al-Kindi, Makhul, Sulayman ibn Musa, Yazid ibn Yazid ibn Jabir, Yahya ibn Jabir, al-Qasim ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, Yazid ibn Abi Malik, al-Mutawakkil ibn al-Layth, Ibn 'Utaybah and al-Muharibi, that they used to say: 'There is no extra grant except in the first of the booty.' [Flag: one name is garbled in the source]"
"3883","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, he said: 'They used to grant extra only from the fifth.'"
"3884","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated to us, from Hisham ibn 'Urwah, from his father, he said: 'A'ishah said to me: 'O son of my sister, 'Umar ibn al-Khattab granted as extra to my brother 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr Layla bint al-Judi; she was of the captives of Damascus; I saw her with me - I knew no value for her, for her beauty, excellence and loveliness.'"
"3885","Chapter on what is taken the fifth from of extra grant. Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Ibn 'Awn, Yunus and Hisham informed us, from Ibn Sirin, that al-Bara' ibn Malik duelled Marzuban al-Za'rah in Bahrayn, pierced him and broke his spine, threw him down, dismounted to him, cut off his hand, and took his two bracelets and spoils. When 'Umar prayed Zuhr, he came to Abu Talhah in his house and said: 'We used not to take the fifth from spoils, but the spoils of al-Bara' have reached wealth; so I shall take its fifth.' The spoils of al-Bara' was the first spoils from which the fifth was taken in Islam."
"3886","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn 'Ubayd informed us, from Anas ibn Malik, that the spoils of al-Bara' reached about thirty thousand or about that."
"3887","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Yunus informed us, from Ibn Sirin, he said: 'I saw the Marzuban's bracelet on the hand of one of Anas ibn Malik's women.'"
"3888","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from al-Awza'i, he said: When 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz sent back the army that had been with Maslamah, the vessel of some of them broke; the polytheists took some Nabateans who were their servants. One day they went out to their festival, leaving the Copts in their vessel, while the others drank; the Copts raised the sail, and in the vessel were the others' goods and weapons. They did not lower their sail until they came to Beirut. That was written to 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz, and 'Umar wrote: 'Grant them extra the vessel and what is in it, and everything they came with, except the fifth.'"
"3889","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from Ibrahim, meaning Ibn Abi 'Ablah, from Makhul, he said: 'Spoils are booty, and in them is the fifth.'"
"3890","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, from Abu al-Juwayriyah, from Ma'n ibn Yazid, he said: I pledged allegiance to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), I, my father and my grandfather; I litigated to him and he judged me victorious; he addressed (a proposal) for me and married me. Ma'n said: 'No spoils are lawful until divided, and no extra grant is lawful until divided among the people in one equal share; when divided, it is lawful for me to give you.' [Flag: the pact wording is terse in the source]"
"3891","Chapter on what has no extra grant in it, and practice concerning it. Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Husayn ibn 'Abd al-Rahman informed us, from one who witnessed al-Qadisiyyah, he said: After the fighting, while a man bathed, the water and dust under his feet uncovered a brick of gold. He brought it to Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas and told him. He said: 'Place it among the Muslims' spoils.'"
"3892","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from Hisham ibn al-Ghaz, from Makhul, he said: 'There are no spoils for anyone except him who captures a stout infidel or kills him; as for him who neither kills nor captures, he has no spoils; spoils are not in a day of defeat nor of conquest; of spoils are permitted clothes, weapons, the girdle and the mount; whatever extra the infidel has besides this, there are no spoils in it except what was upon the infidel's back; and there are no spoils in merchandise - meaning wealth.'"
"3893","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn 'Uthman, he said: I heard Raja' ibn Haywah, 'Ubadah ibn Nusayy, Makhul, Sulayman ibn Musa and Yahya ibn Jabir: 'There is no extra grant in gold ...' [Flag: the report breaks off in the source]"
"3894","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Ibn ... narrated to us, from Raja' ibn Haywah, Ibn 'Adi, Makhul, al-Qasim ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, Yazid ibn Abi Malik and Yahya ibn Jabir, they said: 'The fifth is of the sum of the booty, and the extra grant is after the fifth; then the booty is among the army after that.' [Flag: one transmitter name is missing/truncated in the source]"
"3895","Chapter on people disputing over the slain, to whom his spoils belong. Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, he said: I asked Hariz ibn 'Uthman about a man who kills a man and another finishes him off. He said: 'The spoils belong to him who killed him when he wounded him; nothing belongs to him who finished him off. Thus the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged concerning the spoils of Abu Jahl.'"
"3896","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from Hisham ibn al-Ghaz, from Makhul, he said: 'When a man kills a man of the enemy and another finishes him off, his spoils belong to him who killed or hamstrung him.'"
"3897","Chapter on stealing from the booty. Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr, that a man in charge of the Prophet's baggage (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), called Karkarah, died. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'He is in the Fire.' They looked and found with him a cloak he had stolen."
"3898","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn al-Harith, from Sa'id ibn Abi Hilal, that Shaybah ibn Nasah, the freedman of Umm Salamah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), narrated to him, from Khalid ibn Mughith, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'I have seen Quzman wrapped in a velvet cloak in the Fire' - meaning a black one he stole on the day of Hunayn."
"3899","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abu Marzuq the freedman of Tujib, from Hanash al-San'ani, he said: We conquered a city in the west called Jarbah. Ruwayfi' ibn Thabit al-Ansari stood among us and said: I tell you only what I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say on the day of Hunayn: 'Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him not tread a captive girl until he declares her free of pregnancy by a course; whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him not sell his share of the booty until he takes possession of it; whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him not ride a beast of the Muslims' fay until, when he emaciates it, he returns it to it; whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him not wear a garment of the Muslims' fay until, when he wears it out, he returns it to it.'"
"3900","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, he said: On the day of Badr, a man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, so-and-so stole a velvet cloak of the booty. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked him: 'Did you do it?' He said: No. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) looked at the man who had informed him and said: 'Dig here.' They dug and brought out the cloak. They said: O Messenger of Allah, seek forgiveness for him. He said: 'Leave us from the other (affairs).' [Flag: the closing phrase is terse/obscure in the source]"
"3901","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Muhajir, he said: I heard Abu Sallam narrating to 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz, he said: I campaigned with 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Khalid in the land of the Romans; when he reached the pass, he stood among the people and said: 'O people, we shall not leave the enemy's land with thread nor needle; for it is stealing from the booty.'"
"3902","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi 'Amr al-Shaybani, from Ibn Muhayriz, that he used to say concerning a man in need in the enemy's land, when the Muslims take booty of thread, needle, hair and tent-ropes: 'Let him not deem it lawful until he pays its price.'"
"3903","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sharik ibn 'Abdullah narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn al-Muhajir, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, that a man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a tuft of hair of the booty and said: O Messenger of Allah, we work with hair; grant it to me. He said: 'My share of it is yours.'"
"3904","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Bakr ibn Sawadah, that Hanash narrated to him that Ruwayfi' ibn Thabit used to say: 'One of you rides the beast until, when he impairs it, he returns it to the division; what stealing is graver than that? And one of you wears the garment until, when he wears it out, he returns it to the division; what stealing is graver than that?'"
"3905","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Mutarrif, from al-Dahhak, concerning His saying: {Is he who follows Allah's pleasure} he said: 'He who does not steal' {like him who returns with wrath from Allah} he said: 'Like him who steals.'"
"3906","Chapter on the punishment of him who steals. Sa'id narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Salih ibn Muhammad ibn Za'idah informed me, he said: I was with Maslamah ibn 'Abd al-Malik in campaign; he found a man who had stolen. He summoned Salim ibn 'Abdullah and asked him about that. He said: My father narrated to me, from 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'Whom you find having stolen, beat him and burn his goods.' A copy of the Qur'an was found in his saddle-bag; Salim was asked about that and said: Sell it and give its price in charity."
"3907","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn 'Abdullah narrated to us, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, concerning him who steals, he said: 'His saddle-bag is burned.'"
"3908","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from Ishaq ibn 'Abdullah ibn Abi Farwah, 'that a man called Ziyad stole hair (?) of the sheep; he was brought to Sa'id ibn 'Abd al-Malik; all he had was gathered and burned, with 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz present at that, and he did not disapprove of it.' [Flag: the phrase 'hair of the sheep' is obscure in the source]"
"3909","Chapter on him who steals and repents. Sa'id narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Safwan ibn 'Amr, from Hawshab ibn Sayf, he said: 'The people campaigned against the Romans under 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Khalid ibn al-Walid; a man stole a hundred dinars. When the booty was divided and the people dispersed, he repented. He came to 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Khalid and said: I have stolen a hundred dinars; take them. He said: The people have dispersed; I shall not take them from you until you meet Allah with them on the Day of Resurrection. He came to Mu'awiyah and mentioned that to him, and he said to him the like. He went out weeping and passed by 'Abdullah ibn al-Sha'ir al-Saksaki, who said: What makes you weep? He said: I have stolen a hundred dinars; he told him. He said: To Allah we belong and to Him we return! Will you obey me, O servant of Allah? He said: Yes. He said: Go to Mu'awiyah and say to him: Take your fifth from me; give him twenty dinars; look at the remaining eighty and give them in charity on behalf of that army, for Allah, Mighty and Majestic, knows their names and place; Allah accepts repentance from His servants. Mu'awiyah said: He did well, by Allah! Had I given him that fatwa, it would have been dearer to me than my owning everything I own.'"
"3910","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad ibn An'um, from 'Ikrimah, from Ibn 'Abbas, concerning the stealing which a man commits when the army has dispersed, he said: 'He returns it to the Muslims' booty.'"
"3911","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from al-Awza'i, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from al-Hasan, concerning a man who takes booty when the army disperses, he said: 'He gives it in charity on behalf of that army.'"
"3912","Chapter on the permissibility of food in the enemy's land. Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, he said: 'We would find fruits in the campaigns, eating them without storing them.'"
"3913","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from Ibn 'Awn, from al-Hasan, he said: 'We would find wheat, barley, ghee and honey in our campaigns, eating them.'"
"3914","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ibn 'Awn, he said: I asked Muhammad ibn Sirin about food we find in the enemy's land. He said: Ask al-Hasan, for he used to campaign. So I asked him, and he said: 'We would find it, eating it without storing it.'"
"3915","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, that Bakr ibn Sawadah narrated to him, that Ziyad ibn Nu'aym narrated to him, that a man of Banu Layth narrated to him, that they were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in an expedition. Some groups would find great sheep, while others found only a sheep. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'If only you fed your brothers!' So we threw them sheep by sheep, until what was with them became more than what was with us. Bakr said: We never saw anyone dividing all the food, nor disapproving taking it; rather he enjoys it, but it is not sold. As for other than food of the enemy's goods, it is divided. Bakr said: I saw the people returning with carrying-poles and sticks; nothing of that of our share was sold."
"3916","Saeed narrated, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, that Ibn Harshaf al-Azdi narrated to him, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman, from some of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: ""We used to eat carrots during military expeditions and would not divide them (as spoils), so that we would return to our mounts with our provision bags filled with them."""
"3917","Saeed narrated, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, and Abu Ishaq al-Shaybani reported, from Muhammad ibn Abi Mujalid, from Abdullah ibn Abi Awfa, who said: I said: Did you used to set aside the fifth (khums) from food in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: ""We obtained some food on the day of Khaybar, and a man would come and take from it enough to suffice him, then leave."""
"3918","Saeed narrated, he said: Jarir narrated to us, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, who said: ""They used to divide food and fodder before the fifth (khums) was taken from it."""
"3919","Saeed narrated, he said: Jarir narrated to us, from Layth, from Mujahid, who said: ""They used to eat from honey and fruits, and feed (animals) fodder, except wheat; for they would not take from it until the fifth had been taken."""
"3920","Saeed narrated, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Abdullah ibn Salamah, who said: When Salman obtained a sheep from the spoils, whether it had been slaughtered or they slaughtered it, he would take its skin and make a bag from it, and its hair and make a rope from it, and its meat and dry it as jerky; so he would benefit from its skin, and he would take the rope and find a man with a horse that had thrown him and give it to him, and he would take the meat and eat it over several days. When asked about that he would say: ""I find eating dried meat over several days preferable to spoiling it and then needing what is in people's hands."""
"3921","Saeed narrated, he said: Khalid ibn Abdullah narrated to us, from al-Harith, from an old shaykh who had met Uthman ibn Affan and the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: ""We used to go on campaigns and take of fruits and grapes that were exposed/outside, but when they (the enemy) brought them inside houses, we would not take them except for a price."""
"3922","Saeed narrated, he said: Khalid ibn Abdullah narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik, from Ata, concerning people who go on campaign and come upon food and cheese, and he said: ""They may eat, and whatever remains they should raise (deliver) to the imam (commander)."""
"3923","Saeed narrated, he said: Jarir narrated to us, from Mansur, from Abu Wa'il, who said: ""We used to go on campaigns and take of fruits, and we saw no harm in that."""
"3924","Chapter on what is to be avoided of the food of the enemy and their vessels. 2747 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Abd al-Malik ibn Maysarah, from Zayd ibn Wahb, who said: A letter from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, reached them while they were on one of the campaigns: ""It has reached me that you are in a land where you eat a food called cheese; so look into what is lawful and unlawful of it, and you wear furs, so look into (distinguish) the slaughtered (lawful) thereof from the carrion."""
"3925","Saeed narrated, he said: Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Asim al-Ahwal, from Abu Uthman, who said: I asked Subayh: What did you used to do with ghee and fat? He said: ""We used to eat the ghee and leave the fat."" He said: I am only asking you about the containers. He said: ""We did not ask about containers in those days."""
"3926","Saeed narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha', from Abu Qilabah, from Abu Tha'labah al-Khushani, who said: I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the vessels of the polytheists: may one cook in them? He said: ""Wash them with water, then cook in them."""
"3927","Chapter on what is sold of the enemy's goods of gold or silver. 2750 - Saeed narrated, he said: Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us, he said: Usayd ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to me, from Muqbil ibn Abdullah, from Hani' ibn Kulthum, that the commander of the army of Syria wrote to Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him: We have conquered a land of abundant food and fodder, and I disliked to proceed regarding any of that except by your command. So Umar wrote to him: ""Let the people eat and feed (their animals), but whoever sells anything of that for gold or silver should return it to the spoils of the Muslims, for Allah's fifth and the shares of the Muslims have become due on it."""
"3928","Saeed narrated, he said: Ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Musa ibn Yasar, from Makhul, who said: ""Division applies to everything the Muslims obtain in the land of their enemy, except what is food or drink; and whoever sells anything of that for gold or silver must deliver it to the spoils of the Muslims."""
"3929","Saeed narrated, he said: Jarir narrated to us, from Layth, who said: I said to Mujahid: We are in the land of the enemy and we obtain spoils, but they become too much for us so that the commander and the people cannot carry them and are unable to do so, so the commander says: whoever takes something, it is his. He said: ""Not even a needle/thread."""
"3930","Saeed narrated, he said: Ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Jabir, who said: I heard Makhul say: ""Whatever you cut of a tree in the land of the enemy and make from it a cup, or a staff, or a peg, or a hammer, there is no harm in it; but whatever you find there already made, deliver it to the spoils."""
"3931","Chapter on what is reported about the division of spoils. 2754 - Saeed narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Ibn Ajlan from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from his father, from his grandfather - one of them adding over the other - that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he turned back from Hunayn while upon his camel, a samurah (acacia) tree caught his cloak, and he said: ""Return my cloak to me; do you fear miserliness from me? By Allah, if Allah had granted me livestock like the samur (acacia) trees of Tihamah, I would divide it among you, then you would not find me miserly, nor cowardly, nor a liar."" Then when it was time for the division of the fifth, a man came to him asking to be absolved regarding a needle or thread, and he said: ""Beware of misappropriation (ghulul), for it is disgrace, shame and Fire,"" then he lifted a camel's hair from the back of his camel and said: ""Nothing is lawful for me of what Allah has granted you, not even (as much as) this, except the fifth, and it is returned to you."""
"3932","Saeed narrated, he said: Salih ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Sharik ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Namir narrated to us, from Anas ibn Malik, who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prevailed over the people of Hunayn, the people asked him and crowded him until they pushed him against a tree which caught his cloak, and he said: ""Why are you pressing me against this tree, until it caught my cloak? By the One in whose hand is the soul of Muhammad, if this valley were entirely livestock I would divide it among you."""
"3933","Saeed narrated, he said: Ya'qub ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Amr ibn Abi Amr, from al-Muttalib ibn Abdullah, that it reached him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sat on the day of Hunayn while spoils were brought to him; he took a small camel-hair from the ground and held it between his fingers, and said: ""O people, by Allah, nothing of the fay' (spoils) is lawful for me, (not even) the measure of this hair, except the fifth, and indeed the fifth is returned among you; so fear Allah, and return the needle and the thread, and know that misappropriation (ghulul) on the Day of Resurrection is disgrace and Fire and shame."""
"3934","Saeed narrated, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Abu Hani' al-Khawlani informed me, that he heard Ulayy ibn Rabah al-Lakhmi say: I heard Fadalah ibn Ubayd al-Ansari say: A necklace containing beads and gold was brought to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was at Khaybar, and it was of the spoils being sold; so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered the gold in the necklace to be removed alone, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them: ""Gold for gold, weight for weight."""
"3935","Saeed narrated, he said: Ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Sulayman ibn Sulaym, from Yahya ibn Jabir, that he was in charge of the spoils in the land of the Romans, and no Muslim would come to him buying from the spoils an animal, or a servant, or goods, or a garment in which there was a disease or defect, wanting to return it, except that he accepted it and erased the price from him."
"3936","Saeed narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Yazid ibn Jabir, from Makhul, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the sale of spoils before they are divided."
"3937","Chapter on what is reported about the shares of men and horses. 2760 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Umar informed me, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, who said: I know only from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he ""assigned two shares for the horse and one share for the foot-soldier."" Abd al-Aziz said: I do not know whether I doubted or Ubaydullah did."
"3938","Saeed narrated, he said: Ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Sawadah ibn Ziyad, who said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote to Abd al-Hamid ibn Abd al-Rahman: ""To proceed: the shares of horses are an obligation of what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) obligated: two shares for the horse and one share for the men. By my life, it was recent history and I did not know that any of the Muslims intended to reduce that; so whoever intends to reduce it, punish him. Peace be upon you."""
"3939","Saeed narrated, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Umar narrated to us, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ""gave on the day of Khaybar, to the man one share and to the horse two shares."""
"3940","Saeed narrated, he said: Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Ishaq ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Farwah, that Abu Hazim, freedman of Abu Ruhm, informed him from Abu Ruhm and his brother, that they ""were two horsemen on the day of Khaybar, so they were given six shares: four for their two horses and two for themselves; then they sold the two shares for two young camels."""
"3941","Saeed narrated, he said: Ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Salih ibn Kaysan, that the horses with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Khaybar were thirty-six horses, and that ""he assigned for each horse two shares; and on the day of Hunayn there were two hundred horsemen, and he assigned for each horse two shares and for the man one share."""
"3942","Saeed narrated, he said: Hudayj ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Harithah ibn Mudarrib, from Umar that he ""assigned for the horseman two shares and for the (foot) man one share."""
"3943","Saeed narrated, he said: Hudayj narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, who said: ""I was with Ibn Uthman, and with me were two horsemen, so he gave me for each horse two shares, four shares."" [Flag: the phrase ""I was with Ibn Uthman and with me were horsemen"" is unclear/possibly garbled in the source.]"
"3944","Saeed narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Bushayr ibn Yasar, that ""the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of al-Hudaybiyah were one thousand four hundred."""
"3945","Saeed narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Salih ibn Kaysan, who said: ""There were with them on that day two hundred horses, so he divided for each horse two shares."""
"3946","Saeed narrated, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Usamah ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Makhul, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ""assigned for the horse among them two shares, and for the foot-soldier one share."""
"3947","Saeed narrated, he said: Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from Layth, from Mujahid, who said: ""The first to suggest to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) two shares for the horse was Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him."""
"3948","Chapter on what is reported about preferring horses over mules/half-breeds (baradhin). 2771 - Saeed narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Ash'ath ibn Sawwar narrated to us, from al-Hasan, who said: ""For the horse two shares, and for the half-bred horse one share, and there is nothing for the mule."""
"3949","Saeed narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: I heard it from Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn al-Muntashir, from his father, or from Ibn al-Aqmar, he said: and I heard it from al-Aswad ibn Qays, from Ibn al-Aqmar, who said: The horses raided in Syria; the Arab horses reached (the enemy) in their day, while the heavy work-horses (kawadin) reached on the morning of the next day. Over the horses was a man from Hamdan called al-Mundhir ibn Abi Himsah, and he said: I will not make what caught up (in time) like what did not catch up; so he preferred the (Arab) horses. He wrote about that to Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: ""May al-Wadi'i's mother be bereaved of him, he has reminded me of it; carry it out as he said."""
"3950","Chapter on whoever said: horses and half-breeds (baradhin) are of one status. 2773 - Saeed narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Juwaybir ibn Sa'id informed us, he said: A letter from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz reached us while we were in Khurasan: ""Peace be upon you; to proceed: it has reached me that some of your governors reduced the shares of half-bred horses, and they were fit for what they did of that. And it has reached me from a trustworthy source, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he"" assigned shares for all horses, Arab and cross-bred, two shares for the horse. ""So assign shares for them as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) assigned. Allah Almighty said in His Book: {and horses and mules}, so He made them all horses. By my life, the half-bred horse was no less able for work than the Arab horse in what there was of guard duty or patrol. Peace be upon you."""
"3951","Chapter on whoever said: no share for more than two horses. 2774 - Saeed narrated, he said: Ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from al-Awza'i, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to ""assign shares for horses, and he would not assign shares for a man beyond two horses, though he (the man) had ten horses with him."" [Flag: the last clause about ten horses is obscure in the source.]"
"3952","Saeed narrated, he said: Faraj ibn Fadalah narrated to us, from Azhar ibn Abdullah, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, wrote to Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah: ""Assign for the horse two shares, and for two horses four shares, and for their owner one share; that is five shares. Whatever is beyond two horses are reserves/led horses (jana'ib)."""
"3953","Saeed narrated, he said: Faraj ibn Fadalah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Walid al-Zubaydi narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, that Umar ibn al-Khattab wrote to Abu Ubaydah to that effect."
"3954","Chapter on whoever said: no shares are assigned for half-bred horses. 2777 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, that Bukayr narrated to him, that Sulayman ibn Yasar informed him, that Malik ibn Abdullah al-Khath'ami was spoken to about the shares of cross-bred horses (hujun), and he said: ""I will not assign a share for it; the share is only for the Arab horse."""
"3955","Saeed narrated, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, that Bukayr narrated to him, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, that the daughter of Qarazah, wife of Muawiyah ibn Abi Sufyan, sent to Malik ibn Abdullah to allow a cross-bred horse of a freedman of theirs in the divisions; when he inspected it he said: ""Do you want me to allow this? I will never allow it."""
"3956","Chapter on the share of the slave when he fights. 2779 - Saeed narrated, he said: Ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad ibn An'um, who said: Umar wrote to us: ""Every slave who fights, with whom his master is not present, strike for him his share, the share of a freeman;"" so a boy of ours was given as a freeman was given."
"3957","Saeed narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, who heard al-Hasan narrating from Makhlad al-Ghifari, ""that three slaves of Banu Ghifar were present at Badr, and Umar would give each man of them every year three thousand."""
"3958","Saeed narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Mughirah informed us, from Ibrahim, concerning the slave, the hired worker, and the trader who witness the spoils, and he said: ""A share is assigned, and the slave's share belongs to his master."""
"3959","Chapter on the slave and the woman who attend the conquest. 2782 - Saeed narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Umayyah, from Sa'id al-Maqburi, or another, from Yazid ibn Hurmuz, that Najdah wrote to Ibn Abbas asking him about the woman and the slave who attend the conquest: do they have anything of the spoils? He said: ""They are given a small gift, and they have no (fixed) share."""
"3960","Saeed narrated, he said: Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Umayyah, from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id al-Maqburi, from Ibn Abbas, concerning the slave and the woman who attend battle, who said: ""They have no share, though they may be given a gratuity (radkh)."""
"3961","Chapter on what is reported about the shares of women. 2784 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr informed me, that Sa'id ibn Abi Hilal narrated to him, that Shibl narrated to him, that Sahlah bint Asim gave birth on the day of Khaybar, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""She has been eased (tasahhalat),"" then he struck (assigned) a share for her; whereupon a man of the people said: You gave Sahlah like my share."
"3962","Saeed narrated, he said: Ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Abu Bakr ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Maryam, ""that some Muslim women were present at al-Yarmuk with Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah; some of them fought, and some gave water to drink, and they chanted war songs, saying in their chanting: ..."" 2786 - Saeed narrated, he said: Ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Maryam, that they were given shares on that day. [Flag: the wording of their chant (al-namariq...) is truncated/garbled in the source.]"
"3963","Saeed narrated, he said: Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Amr ibn Muhajir, from his father, that Asma' bint Yazid al-Ansariyyah ""was present at al-Yarmuk with the people, and she killed seven of the Romans with the pole of a tent she was under."""
"3964","Saeed narrated, he said: Ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Damdam ibn Zur'ah, from Shurayh ibn Ubayd al-Hadrami, that Abdullah ibn Qurt al-Azdi narrated to him, who said: ""I campaigned against the Romans with Khalid ibn al-Walid, and I saw the wives of Khalid ibn al-Walid and the wives of his companions with their garments tucked up, carrying water for the Emigrants, chanting."""
"3965","Saeed narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed us, from al-Zuhri, ""that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) assigned a share for two Jewish men on the day of Khaybar."""
"3966","Saeed narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Yazid ibn Jabir, from al-Zuhri, ""that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sought the help of some Jews in his war, and assigned shares for them."""
"3967","Chapter on what is reported about whoever comes after the conquest. 2791 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Qays ibn Muslim, who said: I heard Tariq ibn Shihab say: The people of Basra campaigned to Nahawand, and the people of Kufa reinforced them; the people of Basra wanted not to divide (shares) for the people of Kufa, while Ammar was over the people of Kufa. A man of Banu Utarid said: O stump-eared one (ajda'), do you want to share in our spoils? He said: You have reviled my two best ears, as if it (the ear) was injured with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He wrote about that to Umar, and Umar wrote that ""the spoils belong to whoever witnessed the battle."""
"3968","Saeed narrated, he said: Ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Maryam, from Atiyyah ibn Qays, and Rashid ibn Sa'd, and Habib ibn Ubayd, and Hakim ibn Umayr, and Damrah ibn Habib, who said: ""If the army of the people enters after they have taken spoils, even if they did not witness the fighting and the conquest, they have nothing of the spoils."""
"3969","Saeed narrated, he said: Ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Walid al-Zubaydi, from al-Zuhri, that Anbasah ibn Sa'id informed him that he heard Abu Hurayrah narrating to Sa'id ibn al-As, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Aban ibn Sa'id ibn al-As on a detachment from Medina toward Najd; then Aban ibn Sa'id and his companions came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Khaybar after he had conquered it, with the bundles of their horses of fiber; Aban said: Divide for us, O Messenger of Allah. Abu Hurayrah said: Do not divide for them, O Messenger of Allah. Aban said: You (say) this, O desert hyrax (wabr), come down from the top of a wild lote-tree. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Sit, O Aban,"" and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not divide for them."
"3970","Saeed narrated, he said: Hibban ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Mujalid narrated to us, from al-Sha'bi, who said: Qays ibn Makshuh al-Muradi came to Sa'd with eighty men, while he had three hundred with him; he hastened to Sa'd with eighty and witnessed the battle, then the rest of his companions came after the battle and asked Sa'd to assign shares for them, but he refused until he wrote to Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, who wrote: Assign shares for whoever came to you before the slain of Persia burst open (before their corpses swelled/putrefied), and whoever comes after the inspection of the slain has nothing."
"3971","Saeed narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Mujalid informed us, from al-Sha'bi, that Umar wrote to Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas: ""Assign shares for whoever came to you before the slain of Persia burst open."""
"3972","Chapter on what is reported about the share of the guide and the courier. 2796 - Saeed narrated, he said: Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Maryam, from Atiyyah ibn Qays, and Rashid ibn Sa'd, that ""the courier, the guide, and the messenger he sends to the imam from the camp: their shares continue with the Muslims. And Uthman stayed behind on the day of Badr, yet he (the Prophet) assigned him a share of the spoils."""
"3973","Chapter on what the polytheists seized of the Muslims' property, then Allah returns it to the Muslims. 2797 - Saeed narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi', that a slave boy of Ibn Umar fled and went to the enemy; then Allah gave victory to the Muslims and he was returned to him. And his horse plunged with him into a ravine and went to the enemy; then Allah gave victory to the Muslims and it was returned to him."
"3974","Saeed narrated, he said: Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, that he used to say about weapons, or a slave, or goods seized by the enemy from the Muslims, then Allah returns them to the Muslims, and a man establishes proof for the thing, he said: ""If he finds it before it is divided, it is returned to him; but if it has been divided, he has nothing, and it becomes part of the spoils of the Muslims."""
"3975","Saeed narrated, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Matar al-Warraq, from Raja' ibn Haywah, that Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah wrote to Umar ibn al-Khattab about what the polytheists had seized, then the Muslims prevailed over them afterward, who said: ""Whoever finds his property itself, he is most entitled to it so long as it has not been divided."""
"3976","Saeed narrated, he said: Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj, from Abu Ishaq, from Salman ibn Rabi'ah, who said: If the polytheists seize something belonging to one of the Muslims, then he (the Muslim side) prevails over them, it belongs to its owner so long as it has not been divided; but if it has been divided, he has no right in it."
"3977","Saeed narrated, he said: Ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj, from al-Hakam, from Ibrahim, the like of it."
"3978","Saeed narrated, he said: Jarir narrated to us, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, who said: ""If the enemy captures a slave of the Muslims and the Muslims triumph and recover the slave, he said: if his master finds him before he falls into the division, his master is most entitled to him."""
"3979","Saeed narrated, he said: Uthman ibn Matar al-Shaybani narrated to us, he said: Abu Hariz narrated to us, from al-Sha'bi, who said: The people of Mah aided the people of Jalula' against the Arabs, and they took captives of the Arabs' captives, and slaves, and goods. Then al-Sa'ib ibn al-Aqra', the governor of Umar ibn al-Khattab, campaigned against them and conquered Mah; he wrote to Umar about the Muslims' captives, their slaves and their goods which the merchants of the people of Mah had bought, and about a man who found a treasure in white (barren) land. Umar wrote: ""A Muslim is the brother of a Muslim; he does not betray him nor forsake him. So whichever Muslim man finds his slave and his goods themselves, he is most entitled to them over others. If he finds them in the hands of the merchants after they have been divided, there is no way to them. And whichever freeman the merchants bought, he (the original owner/family) returns their capital to them; for a freeman is neither sold nor bought. And whichever man finds an ancient (adiy) treasure before the war lays down its burdens, a fifth is taken from it and the rest is among them, and he is a man of them; but if he finds it after the war has laid down its burdens, take its fifth and the rest is his exclusively."""
"3980","Chapter on whoever of slaves and freemen joins the enemy, then they are granted safety. 2804 - Saeed narrated, he said: Ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Safwan ibn Amr, that Raja' ibn Haywah, and Adi ibn Adi, and Makhul said about an owned slave who joins the enemy then is granted safety, they said: ""He is given the choice to be returned to his master, or to be returned to his place, and he is not given safety on condition of going off by himself. He said: if this door is opened for slaves, all of them or most of them will act upon it."" [Flag: the ending of this report is compressed/obscure in the source.]"
"3981","Saeed narrated, he said: Ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Maryam, from Atiyyah ibn Qays, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ""when a man of his Companions joined the enemy and killed among them, or committed adultery, or stole, then took safety for himself for what he had done, and he was given safety, nothing of what he did in polytheism would be enforced against him; but if he did something of that in Islam, then joined polytheism, then took safety for himself, then what he fled from would be enforced against him."""
"3982","Chapter on the slave and his master of the enemy who come out of the land of the enemy. 2806 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj, from Abu Sa'id al-A'sam, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave two judgments about the slave and his master: he judged that when the slave comes out of the abode of war before his master, he is free, and if his master comes out afterwards he is not returned to him; and he judged that when the master comes out of the abode of war before the slave, then the slave comes out after him, he is returned to his master."""
"3983","Saeed narrated, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj, from al-Hakam, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas, who said: ""He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to free slaves when they came before their masters and accepted Islam; and on the day of al-Ta'if he freed two slaves."""
"3984","Saeed narrated, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Mughirah, from Shibak, from Amir, from a man of Thaqif, who said: We asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) three things, and he did not permit us any of them: we asked him to permit us regarding purification, for our land was a cold land, but he did not do so; and he never permitted us regarding (drinking from) gourds at all; and we asked him to return to us Abu Bakrah, who had been a slave of ours who came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was besieging Thaqif and accepted Islam, but he refused to return him to us. He said: He is Allah's freedman, then His Messenger's freedman, and he did not return him to us."
"3985","Chapter on what is reported about the freeman captured by the polytheists. 2809 - Saeed narrated, he said: Jarir narrated to us, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, about a Muslim man captured by the enemy, or one under covenant, whom a Muslim merchant buys, who said: ""He works for him for what he bought him for."""
"3986","Saeed narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Mughirah informed us, from Ibrahim, about a man captured by the enemy, whom a Muslim man buys, who said: He works for him for what he bought him for."
"3987","Saeed narrated, he said: Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, from Ash'ath ibn Sawwar, from al-Hasan, who said: ""If a man enters the abode of war and buys a Muslim captive,"" he said: ""He sells him for the price (he paid)."" [Flag: source wording is terse; meaning: he may only recover the purchase price.]"
"3988","Chapter on the slave-girl bought from the captives with gold or silver on her. 2812 - Saeed narrated, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'idah, from al-Sha'bi, that he was asked about a man who bought a slave-girl from the captives with gold and silver on her, who said: ""He should place it in the public treasury."""
"3989","Saeed narrated, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Muhammad ibn Zayd, who said: ""I bought a slave-girl from a fifth that had been divided, and I found with her fifteen dinars; so I came to Abd al-Rahman ibn Khalid ibn al-Walid and mentioned that to him, and he said: It is yours."""
"3990","Saeed narrated, he said: Ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Basri, from Makhul, and Haram ibn Hakim, and Yazid ibn Abi Malik, and al-Mutawakkil, who said about a slave-girl a man buys from the spoils and finds with her jewelry or wealth, they said: ""It is spoils, so let him return it to the spoils of the Muslims."""
"3991","Saeed narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Yazid ibn Jabir, from Makhul, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ""forbade intercourse with pregnant (captive) women until they give birth, and the sale of spoils before they are divided, and the meat of domestic donkeys, and every carnivore with fangs."""
"3992","Chapter on what is reported about captive Magian women, whether they may be approached. 2816 - Saeed narrated, he said: Jarir narrated to us, from Mughirah, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, who said: ""When Magian women and idol-worshippers are taken captive, they are compelled to (enter) Islam; if they accept Islam they may be approached and employed, and if they do not accept Islam they are employed but not approached. When Jewish and Christian women are taken captive, they are compelled to (enter) Islam; whether they accept Islam or not, they may be approached and employed."""
"3993","Saeed narrated, he said: Abu Awanah and Jarir narrated to us, from Musa ibn Abi A'ishah, who said: I asked Murrah al-Hamdani about a Magian slave-woman, whether a man may approach her. He said: ""No."" And I asked Sa'id ibn Jubayr, and he said: They (who do so) are no better than them; and he (Musa) was the severest of the two in statement. [Flag: the second half of this report is terse/obscure in the source.]"
"3994","Saeed narrated, he said: Abu Ayyash narrated to us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Ubaydullah, from Muhammad ibn Ali and al-Sha'bi, that ""captivity annuls the marriage of the two spouses."""
"3995","Chapter on what is reported about ransom. 2819 - Saeed narrated, he said: Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Safwan ibn Amr, from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, that he said: ""If the Roman comes out with a Muslim captive, it is not lawful for the Muslims to return him to disbelief; rather they should ransom him with what they can; Allah Almighty said: {And if they come to you as captives, you ransom them}."""
"3996","Saeed narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Abu al-Muhallab, from Imran ibn Husayn, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave (in exchange) a man of Banu Uqayl, and took two Muslim men. [Flag: wording is terse; evidently an exchange/ransom of captives.]"
"3997","Saeed narrated, he said: Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad ibn An'um, from Hibban ibn Abi Jabalah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""It is upon the Muslims, from their fay' (spoils), to ransom their captive and to pay on behalf of their debtor."""
"3998","Saeed narrated, he said: Ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn An'um, from al-Mughirah ibn Salamah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Amrah, who said: When Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz sent him with the ransom of Muslim captives from Constantinople, I said to him: What do you think, O Commander of the Faithful, if they refuse to ransom a man for a man, what should I do? Umar said: ""Increase for them."" I said: If they refuse to give a man for two? He said: ""Then give them three."" I said: If they refuse except four? He said: ""Then give them, for every Muslim, what they ask of you; for by Allah, one Muslim man is dearer to me than every polytheist I have. Whatever you ransom the Muslim with, you have succeeded; you are only buying Islam."" He said: Yes, ransom them for like what you ransom others. I said: The women? He said: ""Yes, ransom them for what you ransom others."" I said: What if I find a woman who became Christian, but wants to return to Islam? He said: ""Ransom her for like what you ransom others."" I said: What about slaves, shall I ransom them if they are Muslims? He said: ""Ransom them for like what you ransom others."" I said: What if I find among them one who became Christian, but wants to return to Islam? He said: ""Do with them as you do with others."" So I made peace with the chief of the Romans for every Muslim man for two Roman men. Isma'il said: And some of our companions added in it, from Abd al-Rahman, that he asked Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz about the dhimmis, and he said: Ransom them for like what you ransom others."
"3999","Chapter on trade in the land of the enemy and carrying weapons and food. 2823 - Saeed narrated, he said: Ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Maryam, who said: ""I did not see Makhul and our elders disliking trade during campaigns."""
"4000","Saeed narrated, he said: Abu Shihab narrated to us, from Ash'ath ibn Sawwar, from al-Hasan, about one who carries food to the land of the enemy, and he said: ""They are the defiantly disobedient."""
"4001","Saeed narrated, he said: Ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, who said: ""I dislike carrying weapons to the land of the enemy."" I said: Are horses carried to them? He refused that, and said: ""Whatever they gain strength with for fighting is not carried to them; as for other than that, there is no harm."""
"4002","Chapter on a man of the enemy who enters the abode of Islam with safety, then is killed, and whoever comes out seeking Islam. 2826 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Ma'mar, from Ziyad ibn Muslim, that a man came from India with safety to Aden, and a man killed him for his brother. It was written about to Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, and Umar wrote: ""Do not kill him for him; take blood-money from him and send it to his offspring,"" and he ordered him imprisoned."
"4003","Saeed narrated, he said: Ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj ibn Artah, from Ata and Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, who both said about His saying, Almighty: {But if he was from a people hostile to you and he was a believer}, they said: ""A man who is of the enemy, then accepts Islam, then wants to come to the Muslims, but is killed by mistake. They said: There is no blood-money in it, but freeing a (believing) slave is upon him."""
"4004","Saeed narrated, he said: Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from al-Mughirah, from Ibrahim, about His saying: {And it is not for a believer to kill a believer except by mistake, and whoever kills a believer by mistake, then freeing a believing slave and blood-money delivered to his family}, he said: ""This is for the Muslim whose inheritance the Muslims inherit. {But if he was from a people hostile to you and he was a believer, then freeing a believing slave}, he said: the man who accepts Islam while his people are polytheists, with no treaty between him and the Muslims. {But if he was from a people between you and whom is a covenant, then blood-money delivered to his family and freeing a believing slave}, he said: ""This is the Muslim man whose people are polytheists, while between them and the Prophet of Allah is a covenant; so he is killed, and his inheritance is for the Muslims and his blood-money for his people, because they pay (the liability)."""
"4005","Saeed narrated, he said: Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abdullah informed me, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib and Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, that they both said about a man of the people of war who enters the abode of Islam with safety, while some of his heirs of the dhimmis are in it, they said: ""If he had shown residence in the land of the Arabs before he entered it, he has his inheritance; otherwise not. And they said about a woman of the People of the Book of the people of war who enters the abode of Islam with safety, they said: If she shows residence in the land of the Arabs, there is no harm in a Muslim marrying her; but if she does not show it, then not."" [Flag: the phrase ""before he entered it"" is obscure in the source.]"
"4006","Chapter on the captive in the hands of the enemy and dealing with his inheritance. 2830 - Saeed narrated, he said: Ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Ubaydullah, from al-Sha'bi, about a Muslim captive in the hands of the enemy, who said: ""He inherits and is inherited from, so long as he remains upon his religion."""
"4007","Saeed narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Dawud informed us, from al-Sha'bi, that Shurayh used to let the captive inherit, and he would say: ""He is most in need of his share of inheritance when he is a captive in the hands of the enemy; either they ransom him with it, or they set it aside until there comes of him what comes."""
"4008","Chapter on the captive in the hands of the enemy who becomes Christian. 2832 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Ma'mar, from a man of the people of al-Jazirah, that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote to him about a captive who became Christian in the land of the Romans, and he wrote: ""If that is confirmed to you, divide his wealth among his heirs."""
"4009","Saeed narrated, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Ma'mar, from Ishaq ibn Rashid, from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, about a man who is captured and becomes Christian, who said: ""When that is known, his wife is cleared of him and observes a waiting period of three menstrual cycles."""
"4010","Saeed narrated, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Juwaybir informed us, from al-Dahhak ibn Muzahim, who said: ""When a man apostatizes, his wife is separated from him; then if he accepts Islam, he is a suitor (i.e., must remarry her anew)."""
"4011","Comprehensive chapter on martyrdom. 2835 - Saeed narrated, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Sa'id ibn Abi Hilal, that it reached him that Ibn Rawahah said before they set out for Mu'tah: When they met, Zayd ibn Harithah took the banner and fought until he was killed; then Ja'far ibn Abi Talib took it, and the horse upon which Zayd ibn Harithah had been was brought to him. A man said to him: Know that it is the horse upon which the man was killed. When he mounted it he said: O people, I seek (martyrdom) for myself, so seek for yourselves. He fought until he was killed. Then it was brought to Abdullah ibn Rawahah; when he rode it he swerved aside, and he said: ... Sa'id said: Then he dismounted and leaned his back against a wall; a finger of his fingers was struck and he said: ... (a verse about the path). Then he fought until he was killed. Khalid ibn al-Walid took the banner; when he turned back with it, a man of the people said: I see the ...(?) of a man fighting today. Khalid said: This is not a day for reviling. Then the Muslims returned in a defensive body, with them Waqid ibn Abdullah al-Tamimi, who was of the best archers; he said takbir and said: Lift me upon a shield. They lifted him, and he said: Look at where my arrows land; if you are pleased, inform me. He shot the polytheists until Allah turned them back. Ibn Abi Hilal said: Nafi' informed me that Ibn Umar informed him that he stood over Ja'far on that day while he was slain, and he said: ""I counted on him fifty (wounds), between stab and strike, none of them in his back."" Sa'id said: And it reached me that they buried Zayd, Ja'far and Ibn Rawahah on that day in one pit. [Flag: several words/verses in this report are missing or garbled in the source (lacunae).]"
"4012","Saeed narrated, he said: Abu Ma'shar narrated to us, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, who said: ""I counted on Ja'far, while slain, fifty (wounds), between stab and strike."""
"4013","Saeed narrated, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, that Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah al-Zuhri informed him, from Buraydah ibn Sufyan al-Aslami, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Asim ibn Thabit, and Zayd ibn Dathinah, one of Banu Bayadah, and Khubayb ibn Adi, and Marthad ibn Abi Marthad to Banu Lihyan at al-Raji'. They fought them until they took a covenant for themselves, except Asim, who refused and said: I will not accept today a covenant of a polytheist. He supplicated then and said: O Allah, I defend Your religion today, so defend my flesh. He fought, saying ... and urging himself on ... When they killed him, he was in a ...(? pit) of theirs. Some of them said to others: This is the one about whom the Makkan woman vowed - she is al-Sulafah, one of Banu al-Aqlah ibn Amr ibn Awf. Asim had on the day of Uhud killed three men for her (i.e., of her kin), each the standard-bearer of Quraysh on that day, of Banu Abd al-Dar. He would shoot - and he was an archer - saying: Take it, and I am the son of al-Aqlah. She would be brought (the slain) and whenever a man was brought to her she would say: Who killed him? They would say: We do not know, except that we heard a man saying: Take it, and I am the son of al-Aqlah. She said: ...(?) and she vowed that if she gained power over his head she would drink wine in his skull. They wanted to cut his head to take it to her, but Allah sent a ...(? swarm of hornets) so they could not cut his head. Khubayb ibn Adi and Zayd ibn Dathinah were captured; they were taken until they were brought to Makkah. Khubayb was sold to one of the Jumahis for a black slave-woman. Uqbah ibn ... (? Adi), one of Banu Nawfal ibn Abd Manaf, came asking him to give him to him, to kill him in place of his brother Tu'mah ibn Adi, because he (Khubayb) had killed him on the day of Badr. He refused to sell him to him, but gave him to him as a gift; he treated him badly in his captivity. He said: This is not how noble people treat their captive. He said: So they took him out and treated him well, and placed him with a woman to guard him while in captivity, until it was said: You will be taken out to be killed. He said to the woman: Give me a razor to clean myself with. She gave him, and she had a small son; the boy came to him, he took him and seated him with him. The woman thought he wanted to kill him, so she cried out to him, pleading with him; he had wanted to frighten her, then he released him, saying then: I would not be treacherous. He was taken out to be killed; he passed by women who said: This is Khubayb ...(?) to be killed for Tu'mah. When he drew near the wood (gallows) he said: ... Then he said: Let me prostrate two prostrations - and he was the first to establish that (practice) - then he said: Were it not that you would say Khubayb feared death, I would have added two more prostrations. He said then: O Allah, I find none to convey my greeting to Your Messenger, so convey my greeting to Your Messenger. They claimed the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said then: ""And upon him be peace."" His Companions said: O Prophet of Allah, upon whom? He said: Your brother Khubayb ibn Adi is being killed. When he was raised upon the wood, he faced (the qiblah) in supplication. The man said: When I saw him wanting to supplicate, I clung to the ground. He said: O Allah, count them in number and kill them piecemeal. A year did not pass - they claimed - with any of them alive except that man who clung to the ground. [Flag: several words, verses and names in this long report are missing or garbled in the source.]"
"4014","Saeed narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, who said: I heard Jabir ibn Abdullah say: The one who killed Khubayb was Abu Sirwa'ah. Sufyan said: His name is Uqbah ibn al-Harith."
"4015","Saeed narrated, he said: Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Abu Khaythamah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated to us, he said: I heard al-Bara', when a man asked him: Did you flee, O Abu Umarah, on the day of Hunayn? He said: No, by Allah, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not turn back; but the young and light-armed of his Companions went out bare, without weapons, and came upon archers, the mass of Hawazin and Banu Nasr, whose arrows hardly fell amiss; they showered them with arrows, hardly missing. They then came back to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was upon his white mule, his cousin Abu Sufyan ibn al-Harith ibn Abd al-Muttalib leading him. He dismounted and sought (Allah's) help, then he said: ... (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he arranged his Companions. [Flag: the Prophet's words at Hunayn are obliterated/missing in this print.]"
"4016","Saeed narrated, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Qatadah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said in one of the battle scenes: I am the son of al-Awatik (the Awatik women)."
"4017","Saeed narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id ibn Amr al-Qurashi, Siyabah ibn Asim narrated to us, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said on the day of Hunayn: ""I am the son of al-Awatik."""
"4018","Saeed narrated, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Sa'id ibn Abi Hilal, from a man of Banu Mazin, that it reached him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood on the day of Uhud and said: ""Is there no man to bring me news of Sa'd ibn al-Rabi', for the last I knew of him, I saw him near the mountain pass with spears levelled at him?"" A youth of the Ansar stood and said: I will, O Messenger of Allah. He went and found him under a tree; he conveyed the news to him. He said: Convey greeting to the Messenger of Allah, and inform him that I have been pierced with twelve piercings, and all my vitals have been penetrated; and convey greeting to your people, and say to them: Sa'd ibn al-Rabi' says to you: You have no excuse if the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is killed while any of you remains. Sa'd was struck (martyred); he bequeathed to Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him. A man entered upon Abu Bakr while Sa'd's daughter was on his stomach and he was smelling her, and he said: O Caliph of the Messenger of Allah, is this your daughter? He said: No, rather the daughter of a man better than me. The man said: Who is better than you after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Sa'd ibn al-Rabi'; he was of the chiefs (nuqaba') on the day of al-Aqabah, he witnessed Badr, and was killed on the day of Uhud."
"4019","Saeed narrated, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, from Amr ibn al-Harith, that Sa'id ibn Abi Hilal and Abu al-Nadr narrated to him, that Sa'd ibn Mu'adh said on the day of al-Ahzab: ... Sa'id said: And he said also: ... A'ishah said: O Allah, keep him safe, for I fear for the man only from his extremities. Sa'id said: The mother of Sa'd wept for him at his death, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Every weeping woman is ...(? untruthful) without doubt, except the mother of Sa'd."" Sa'id said, from Abu Hazim, that it reached him that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said on the day of Uhud: ""O Allah, forgive my people, for they do not know."" [Flag: Sa'd ibn Mu'adh's supplications are missing/omitted in the source.]"
"4020","Saeed narrated, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, that Rabi'ah ibn Laqit narrated to him, from Malik ibn Hidm, that he said: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab say: ""What do you think about three men who accepted Islam together and emigrated together, without committing any (evil) deed in Islam: one of them plague killed, another the belly (disease) killed, and the other was killed a martyr?"" They said: The martyr is the best of them. Umar said: ""By the One in whose hand is my soul, they are companions in the Hereafter as they were companions in this world."""
"4021","Saeed narrated, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from al-Aswad ibn Qays, from Jundub ibn Sufyan al-Bajali, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had his finger bloodied in one of the campaigns or battle scenes, and he said: ... (a verse: ... in the path ...). [Flag: the verse is abbreviated/missing in the source.]"
"4022","Saeed narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Aswad ibn Qays, who heard Jundub al-Bajali say: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a journey, and his finger bled, and he said: ... (a verse: ... in the path ...). [Flag: the verse is abbreviated/missing in the source.]"
"4023","Saeed narrated, he said: Ya'qub ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Abu Hazim narrated to me, that he heard Sahl when he was asked about the wound of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: ""By Allah, I know who washed the wound of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and who poured the water, and with what it was treated: Fatimah, daughter of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), washed it, and Ali poured the water with the shield. When Fatimah saw that the water only increased the blood, she took a piece of mat and burned it and stuck it on, and the blood stopped. His incisor tooth was broken on that day, his face was wounded, and the helmet was broken upon his head."""
"4024","Saeed narrated, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Hazim narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me that he heard Sahl ibn Sa'd, asked about the wound of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Uhud, the like of it, except that he said: The helmet was crushed upon his head."
"4025","Saeed narrated, he said: Salih ibn Musa al-Talhi narrated to us, he said: Muawiyah ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from A'ishah and Umm Ishaq, daughters of Talhah, that they both said: Our father was wounded on the day of Uhud with twenty-four wounds: his head was gashed with a square gash, his sciatic vein (nasa) was cut, his finger was paralyzed, and the rest of the wounds were on the rest of his body. Allah Almighty protected him from defeat and fainting. They said: And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had his incisor broken and was gashed in his face, and that faintness overtook him; so Talhah carried him to the mountain pass, going backwards with him; whenever one of the polytheists caught up with him, he fought in his defence until he supported him to the pass."
"4026","Saeed narrated, he said: Khalid ibn Abdullah narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, who said: I saw the hand of Talhah ibn Ubaydullah, paralyzed - the one with which he shielded the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4027","Saeed narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ikrimah, who said: Ali came with his sword on the day of Uhud stained with blood, while Fatimah washed the blood from the face of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Take it, praiseworthy. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""If you did well in fighting today, then Sahl ibn Hunayf, and Asim ibn Thabit, and al-Harith ibn al-Simmah, and Abu Dujanah did well."""
"4028","Saeed narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, who said: Talhah shielded the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with his hand, and his finger was injured, and he said: Hass (an exclamation of pain). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Had he said: In the name of Allah, he would have entered Paradise while the people looked on."""
"4029","Comprehensive chapter on martyrdom. 2853 - Saeed narrated, he said: Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Abu Khaythamah informed me, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated to us, he said: I heard al-Bara' say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) placed Abdullah ibn Jubayr over the archers on the day of Uhud, fifty men, and said to them: ""If you see birds snatching us, do not leave your place until I send to you; and if you see us defeating the people and trampling them, do not leave until I send to you."" He said: Allah defeated them; by Allah, I saw the women running up the mountain, their anklets and shins visible, lifting their garments. The companions of Abdullah ibn Jubayr said: The spoils, O people, the spoils! Your companions have prevailed, what are you waiting for? Abdullah ibn Jubayr said: Have you forgotten what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said? They said: By Allah, we shall go to the people and take of the spoils. When they came to them, their faces were turned, and they returned defeated; and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was calling them from behind them. None remained with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except twelve men. They (the enemy) struck seventy men of us; while his Companions had struck of the polytheists on the day of Badr one hundred and forty men: seventy captives and seventy slain. Abu Sufyan said: Is Muhammad among the people? Three times; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade them to answer him. Then he said: Is the son of Abu Quhafah among the people? Three times. Then he said: Is Umar ibn al-Khattab among the people? Three times. He returned to his companions and said: As for these, they have been killed. Umar could not restrain himself; he said: You lie, O enemy of Allah; those you named are alive, and Allah has kept for you what will grieve you. He said: A day for the day of Badr; war is alternate (sijal). You will find among the people mutilation which I neither ordered nor grieved me. Then he chanted: Exalted be Hubal, exalted be Hubal. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Will you not answer him?"" They said: O Messenger of Allah, what shall we say? He said: ""Say: Allah is Higher and Majestic."" He said: We have al-Uzza, and you have no Uzza. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Will you not answer him?"" They said: O Messenger of Allah, what shall we say? He said: Say: Allah is our Protector, and you have no protector."
"4030","Saeed narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr, who heard Jabir ibn Abdullah say: ... Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: ""There will come upon people a time when a group of people go on campaign, and it is said to them: Is there among you one who accompanied the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? It is said: Yes, and victory is granted them. Then there will come upon people a time when a group of people go on campaign, and it is said to them: Is there among you one who accompanied the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? It is said: Yes, and victory is granted them."" [Flag: the chain between Jabir and Abu Sa'id is compressed/obscure in the source.]"
"4031","Saeed narrated, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Humayd al-Tawil, from Anas, who said: The Ansar would say on the day of al-Khandaq: ... (verses invoking Muhammad for jihad). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) answered them: There is no life but the life of the Hereafter, so honor the Ansar and the Emigrants. [Flag: the Ansar's verse is abbreviated in the source.]"
"4032","Saeed narrated, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Muhammad ibn Abi Ya'qub, who said: One who heard Buraydah al-Aslami, from beyond the river of Balkh, informed me, while he was upon a horse, saying: ""There is no life but chasing horses with horses."""
"4033","Saeed narrated, he said: Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id and Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad, who said: ""The first to dig a trench around his camp was the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."""
"4034","Saeed narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Khusayfah, from al-Sa'ib ibn Yazid, if Allah wills, ""that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wore two coats of mail, one over the other, on the day of Uhud; and once he said: he wore - as Sufyan said - two coats of mail."""
"4035","Saeed narrated, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Mansur, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Yahya ibn Ja'dah, who said: Umar said: Were it not for three things, I would be pleased to have departed (died): were it not to place my forehead for Allah (in prostration), and to sit with people who pick out good speech as good fruit is picked, and travelling in the path of Allah Almighty."
"4036","Saeed narrated, he said: Ya'qub ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Zuhri narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah, who said: On the day of Badr, some Arabs sat in the mountain of Badr, saying: Wherever victory is, we shall be with its people. When Allah made His Messenger's victory mighty, they came to him and informed him of their affair, saying: O Messenger of Allah, we heard something descending from the sky, and we heard the snorting of horses, and the clatter of gear, and we heard something called: Advance, Hayzum. He said: ""That was Jibril, peace be upon him."""
"4037","Saeed narrated, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ibn Awn, from Umayr ibn Ishaq, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Mark yourselves (with distinguishing signs), for the angels have marked themselves."""
"4038","Saeed narrated, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, that Abu Yunus narrated to him, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: ""I have been aided with awe (cast into the enemy), and I have been given the comprehensive words; he said: And while I was asleep, I was brought the keys of the treasuries of the earth and placed in my hand."""
"4039","Saeed narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Abu al-Abbas, a poet who was in Makkah, from Abdullah ibn Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), on the day of al-Ta'if, said: ""We are returning tomorrow, if Allah wills."" The people said: Before we conquer it? He said: Then go forth to fighting in the morning. They went forth in the morning and wounds befell them. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""We are returning tomorrow, if Allah wills;"" they were pleased with that, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) laughed."
"4040","Saeed narrated, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from Juwaybir, from al-Dahhak, about His saying {marked (musawwimin)}, who said: ""Marked with white wool."""
"4041","Saeed narrated, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Ikrimah, who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) besieged the people of al-Ta'if, a woman appeared and uncovered her private part, saying: Here, short of you, so shoot. A Muslim man shot her and did not miss that of her."
"4042","Saeed narrated, he said: Isma'il ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Ikrimah, the like of it; he said: and he made her bleed (struck her down). [Flag: the verb is terse in the source.]"
"4043","Saeed narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ubayd, who said: A prophet of the prophets went on campaign and said: ""Let no man go on campaign with (us) who built a building and did not complete it, or sowed a crop and did not harvest it, or married a woman and did not consummate with her."""
"4044","Saeed narrated, he said: Hudayj ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated to us, he said: I heard al-Bara' ibn Azib say: ""I campaigned with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fifteen campaigns."" And I heard Zayd ibn Arqam say: ""I campaigned with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seventeen campaigns."""
"4045","Saeed narrated, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Sa'id ibn Abi Hilal, that it reached him that the two parties who intended to lose heart, while Allah was their Protector, were ""Banu Salamah and Banu Harithah."""
"4046","Saeed narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, who heard Jabir ibn Abdullah say: ""It was revealed about us, about Banu Harithah and Banu Salamah: {When two parties of you intended to lose heart, while Allah was their Protector}. It would not please me that it had not been revealed."""
"4047","Saeed narrated, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Sa'id ibn Abi Hilal, that it reached him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married Abu Bakr to Asma' bint Umays while they were under the banners (on campaign)."
"4048","Saeed narrated, he said: Ya'qub ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from his father, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah, who said: On the day of Badr, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) looked at the polytheists and their multitude, and looked at the Muslims and deemed them few; he bowed two rak'ahs, with Abu Bakr standing at his right. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said in his prayer: ""O Allah, do not bid farewell to me (do not leave me); O Allah, do not forsake me; O Allah, do not leave me bereft; O Allah, I beseech You what You promised me; O Allah, if this band of polytheists defeats this band of Muslims, You will never be worshipped."" Abu Bakr said: You have persisted, by Allah - may my father and mother be ransom for you; by Allah, He will not bid farewell to you, nor forsake you, nor leave you bereft; He will aid you against your enemy as He promised you. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished, pleased, and said: ""I saw Jibril wrapped (in a turban), descending from the sky, wrapped in a warrior's wrap, with battle-dust upon his teeth; so I knew it was victory."""
"4049","Saeed narrated, he said: Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Maryam, from Atiyyah ibn Qays, that Jibril came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after he finished the fighting of Badr, upon a red horse with knotted forelock, its ...(? incisor/teeth) bound with dust, wearing his armor, and said: O Muhammad, Allah sent me to you and commanded me not to leave until you are pleased; are you pleased? He said: ""Yes."" [Flag: one word describing the horse is obscure in the source.]"
"4050","Saeed narrated, he said: Ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Safwan ibn Amr, from Abu al-Yaman Amir ibn Abdullah ibn Luhayy al-Hawzani, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out on the day of Badr and said to his Companions: ""Count one another."" They found themselves three hundred and fourteen men. Then he said to them: ""Count one another."" They counted likewise twice more; then a man came while they counted, upon a weak young camel of his, completing the count to three hundred and fifteen men. He said: ""You are today upon the number of the prophets, and the number of the companions of Talut."""
"4051","Saeed narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ubayd ibn Umayr, who said: On the day of Badr, the Muslims were shy of the nakedness of their brothers and cast them into a pit; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came and stood over them, saying: ""O so-and-so, O so-and-so, did you not find Allah true to what He promised you? O so-and-so, O so-and-so - naming them by their names - did you not find Allah true to what He promised you?"" They said: O Messenger of Allah, do they hear? He said: ""By the One in whose hand is my soul, (they hear) as you hear."" [Flag: ""Muslims"" here evidently refers by slip to the slain polytheists addressed at the pit; translated literally.]"
"4052","Saeed narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr, from Ikrimah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) killed Ubayy ibn Khalaf with his hand, and said: ""Allah's anger is severe upon a man killed by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with his hand in the path of Allah; and Allah's anger is severe upon a people who bloodied the face of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the path of Allah."""
"4053","Saeed narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr, from Ikrimah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said on the day of Uhud: ""Who will take this sword with its right?"" Abu Dujanah said: I will. He came with it bent. He said: Did you give it its right? He said: ""Yes."" [Flag: the report is compressed; the bent sword and questioner are unclear in the source.]"
"4054","Saeed narrated, he said: Ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ziyad al-Alhani, from his elders, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned, he said: Allah says: ""My servant, wholly My servant, is he who remembers Me even while facing his peer."" A Muslim man heard it and held to it; when the people came to Syria, he went out in a detachment - men on foot - and lagged behind his companions praying. A strong infidel (ilj) of the Romans descended upon him upon a work-horse, fully armed, intending him; between them came a vineyard with a fence of umm-ghaylan thorns. The infidel tied his horse, then cut through the vineyard toward him, threatening him, until nothing remained between them but the fence, while the man remembered the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and remembered much, undistracted by his enemy's threats, saying: O Allah, I am straitened by him, so suffice me against him. The Roman saw an opening in the fence and went to come out to him through it; the thorns caught the sleeve of his hand, and he struggled long to free himself. He went to free his right sleeve, but the thorns seized it, binding him fast by Allah. When the Muslim saw him, he went to him; when the infidel saw the Muslim coming to him, he began to snort, while Allah had pinned him and he could not free himself. The man did not reach him until he stabbed himself with a dagger he had, and fell. The Muslim man remembered Allah and praised Him, saying: O Allah, You killed him. Then he took his weapons and clothes, and Allah carried him upon his horse."
"4055","Saeed narrated, he said: Marwan narrated to us, he said: Sulayman al-Taymi narrated to us, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, who said: Salman al-Farisi said: ""If people knew what Allah's aid to the weak is, they would not go to extremes with mounts."" [Flag: the last clause is terse/obscure in the source.]"
"4056","Saeed narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Anas ibn Malik, that he ""saw Ibn Umm Maktum in one of the Muslims' battle stations, with him the banner of the Muslims."""
"4057","Saeed narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, who heard Jabir ibn Abdullah say: ""People drank wine in the morning of the day of Uhud, then were killed as martyrs at the end of the day."""
"4058","Saeed narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Mansur, from Mujahid, who said: ""The first woman martyred in Islam was the mother of Ammar (Umm Ammar)."""
"4059","Saeed narrated, he said: Ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Safwan ibn Amr, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Malik ibn Yakhamir, from his father, from Mu'adh ibn Jabal, who said: ""Do not shelter the Jews; for Allah has struck their necks with a prior humiliation, and they reviled Allah with a reviling no one of His creation reviled: they called Allah the third of three."""
"4060","Saeed narrated, he said: Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Maryam, from Abu al-Ahwas Hakim ibn Jubayr, who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab wrote: ""Keep nails long in the land of the enemy, for they are a weapon."" [Flag: the narrator's name is uncertain in the print (Hakim vs. Umayr); translated literally.]"
"4061","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, who heard Jabir ibn Abdullah saying: On the day of al-Hudaybiyah we were one thousand four hundred, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to us: You are today the best people on earth. Jabir said: If I could see, I would have shown you the place of the tree."
"4062","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad ibn An'am, he said: Our elders narrated to us that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The purest earning of the Muslim is his share in the path of Allah, and the bargain of his hand, and what his land gives him."
"4063","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Jabir, that Ka'b used to say: The provision of this ummah is in the tips of its spears and at their quivers, as long as they do not sow crops; when they sow crops they become like other people; and Allah Almighty will keep giving this ummah until He gives them the finest gait of riding beasts. [Flag: phrase rendered as quivers is uncertain in source]"
"4064","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, he said: al-A'mash narrated to us, from Ibrahim, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab heard a man saying: O Allah, I spend my wealth and myself in Your path, al-A'mash said: and perhaps he said: and my child, so Umar said: Will not one of you be silent, for if he is tested he is patient, and if he is spared he is grateful."
"4065","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, who heard Jabir ibn Abdullah saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: War is deceit."
"4066","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, that Bukayr narrated to him that it reached him that when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) appointed Usamah ibn Zayd as commander, the people spoke much about it, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You speak about Usamah, that Usamah is young in age; if you speak, you already spoke about his father before him; by Allah, he is fit for command. Bukayr said: It reached me that Ubaydah ibn Sufyan said: I hope this remains until today. Bukayr said: And I heard Sulayman ibn Yasar say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) appointed Usamah over an army and ordered him to burn the settlement of Qaribna [Flag: place-name uncertain in source], so the front of the army went forth while Usamah hesitated until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed away; and Usamah came to Abu Bakr and said: What do you command me? He said: Proceed with the order the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave you; I will neither add to it nor reduce it. The people said: If you send Usamah while with him are the best of the people, these Bedouins will apostatize and attack the baggage of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Abu Bakr said: By Allah, even if I knew wolves and dogs would tear me here, I would not reverse a command ordered by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Proceed, for Allah will help us; but if you see fit to permit Umar ibn al-Khattab to stay, do so. He said: Yes. Usamah said: So I went out to Umar and he said: What did you do? I said: He asked me to permit you to stay, so I did, and he ordered me to proceed. Umar said: May Allah have mercy on you."
"4067","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Abu Ja'far, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood on the minbar and said: Some people criticize the command of Usamah just as they criticized the command of his father before; he and his father are worthy of it."
"4068","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Husayn, from Abu Malik, he said: The first thing revealed of Bara'ah, up to after forty verses: March forth, light and heavy, up to His saying: if you knew."
"4069","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Mis'ar, from Abu Bakr ibn Utbah, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, wrote to them: Place a desert between you and the enemy."
"4070","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Masruq, from Abu al-Duha, he said: This verse was revealed concerning those killed at Uhud: And do not think those killed in the path of Allah are dead; rather they are alive with their Lord, provided for; and there was revealed concerning them: And that He may take martyrs from among you. He said: On that day seventy men were killed, four of the Muhajirun: Hamzah ibn Abd al-Muttalib, Mus'ab ibn Umayr of Banu Abd al-Dar, al-Shammas ibn Uthman al-Makhzumi, and Abdullah ibn Jahsh al-Asadi, and the rest were of the Ansar."
"4071","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Salamah ibn Wardan informed me, he said: I asked Salim ibn Abdullah: May I pray while wearing a horn containing an arrowhead with blood on its tip? He said: No."
"4072","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Sulayman al-Ahwal, from Ikrimah, he said: I heard him say: When there was revealed: If you do not march forth He will punish you with a painful punishment and replace you with other people, the hypocrites said: Some people remained behind and did not march forth, so they perished; and there were people who stayed behind so that they might gain understanding in religion and warn their people when they returned to them, that they might beware; and Allah revealed concerning those: And those who dispute concerning Allah after He has been responded to, their argument is invalid before their Lord."
"4073","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Shurahbil ibn Muslim, from Thawban, the freedman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Sharpen your sword. It was said to him: What is this, O Abu Abdullah? He said: Weakness has been cast into your hearts, and fear has been removed from the hearts of your enemy. They said: How is that? He said: Because of your love of this world and your hatred of death; and blessed is he whose tongue is silent, who weeps over his sin, and whose house suffices him."
"4074","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Anas, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The voice of Abu Talhah in the army is better than a company of men. He used to kneel before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and say: My face is a shield for your face, and my soul a ransom for your soul."
"4075","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Kathir narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, they said: They asked Asma about the hardest day that came upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). She said: I think I remember it: while he was in the mosque with a group among them, they said: He says such-and-such and says such-and-such about what they disliked, so stand up to him and ask him. A group went to him and said: You say such-and-such and you say such-and-such. He said: Yes. He never concealed anything from them. So they dragged him among themselves. The cry for help reached my father: Catch up with your companion. She said: My father went out running, and he had braids of hair, and he called out: Woe to you, would you kill a man for saying: My Lord is Allah? She said: They turned from him and came to my father. He came to us saying: Blessed are You, O Possessor of Majesty and Honor, and his braids were disheveled, and he kept saying thus and stretching them out and they followed him. And Sufyan indicated with his hand."
"4076","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Abu Shihab narrated to us, from Humayd al-Tawil, from Anas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave Uyaynah ibn Badr one hundred camels from the spoils of Hunayn, and he gave al-Aqra' ibn Habis the like of it. Some of the Ansar said: Our spoils are given to people whose blood still drips from our swords, or our blood from their swords. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gathered the Ansar and said: Is there among you anyone except from yourselves? They said: No, except so-and-so, the son of our sister. He said: The son of a people's sister is of them. Then he said: Are you not pleased, O company of Ansar, that the people go with this world while you go with Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to your homes? They said: Yes indeed, O Messenger of Allah. He said: If the people take a valley and the Ansar take a mountain pass, I would take the mountain pass of the Ansar. The Ansar are my inner garment and my receptacle; were it not for the Hijrah I would have been a man of the Ansar."
"4077","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from one who narrated to him, that Abu Bakr, when the people withheld zakat from him, wanted to fight them. It was said to him: Did not the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: I have been commanded to fight people until they say: There is no god but Allah; when they say it, they protect their blood and wealth from me except by its right? He said: This is part of its right, that they not separate what Allah has joined; if they withhold from me anything they acknowledged to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), I will fight them for it."
"4078","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) distributed a distribution at al-Ji'ranah. A man came to him and said: Be just, O Muhammad, for you have not been just. He said: Woe to you, who will be just if I am not just? Umar said: Let me strike his neck. He said: No. This man and companions of his recite the Quran without it passing their throats; they pass out of religion as the arrow passes out of the game animal."
"4079","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Masruq, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Nu'm, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, he said: Ali, while in Yemen, sent a nugget of gold in its dust to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) divided it among four men: al-Aqra' ibn Habis al-Hanzali, Uyaynah ibn Badr al-Fazari, Alqamah ibn Ulathah al-Amiri, and Zayd al-Khayr al-Ta'i. Quraysh became angry and said: He gives the chiefs of Najd and leaves us. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I only did that to reconcile them. Then a man came, thick-bearded, with prominent cheeks, deep-set eyes, protruding forehead, shaven head, and said: Fear Allah, O Muhammad. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Who obeys Allah if I disobey Him? Does He trust me with the people of the earth while you do not trust me? Then the man turned away. A man of the people asked permission to kill him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No. From the offspring of this man there will be a people who recite the Quran without it passing their throats; they kill the people of Islam and leave the people of idols; they pass out of Islam as the arrow passes out of the game animal. If I were to meet them I would kill them as Ad were killed."
"4080","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Mahdi ibn Maymun narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from his brother Ma'bad ibn Sirin, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: From the east there will emerge a people who recite the Quran without it passing their throats; they pass out of religion as the arrow passes out of the game animal, then they do not return to it until the arrow returns to its notch. It was said: What is their mark? He said: Their mark is shaving, or close-cropping of hair."
"4081","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Khalaf ibn Khalifah narrated to us, from Hafs ibn Umar, he said: Anas took me to Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan with forty riders of the Ansar, and he assigned stipends for us. When we returned with him, until we were at Fajj al-Naqah, he prayed Zuhr two rak'ahs, then saluted, and entered his tent. The people stood and added to his two rak'ahs two other rak'ahs. He said to his son Abu Bakr: What are these people doing? He said: They add to your two rak'ahs two rak'ahs. Anas said: May Allah disgrace those faces; by Allah, they neither hit the Sunnah nor accepted the concession. I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: There will be a people who go deep into religion and pass out of it as the arrow passes out of the game animal."
"4082","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Spoils were not made lawful for any black-haired people before you; a fire would descend from heaven and consume them. On the day of Badr the people hastened to take spoils, so Allah Almighty revealed: Were it not for a prior decree from Allah, there would have touched you for what you took a great punishment. So eat of what you have taken as spoils, lawful and good."
"4083","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Abu Ma'shar narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id, he said: Were it not for a prior decree from Allah: that I made spoils lawful for you in My knowledge; there would have touched you for what you took of captives a great punishment. He said: Meaning the day of Badr."
"4084","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, that the battle-cry of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Musaylimah was: O companions of Surat al-Baqarah."
"4085","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj ibn Artah, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Samurah ibn Jundub, he said: The battle-cry of the Muhajirun was: O Abdullah, and the battle-cry of the Ansar was: O Abd al-Rahman."
"4086","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Sharik ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Namir, from Ata ibn Yasar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by the women of Banu al-Ashhal when he finished with Uhud, and heard them weeping over those of them martyred at Uhud. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: But Hamzah has no weeping women. Sa'd ibn Mu'adh heard it from him, so he went to the women of Banu Abd al-Ashhal and ordered them to go to the house of Hamzah and weep over him. They went and wept over him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard their weeping and said: Who are these? It was said: The women of the Ansar weeping over Hamzah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to them and said: No weeping; may Allah be pleased with you and with your children and your children's children."
"4087","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Hushaim narrated to us, he said: Mughirah informed us, from al-Sha'bi, he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned on the day of Uhud, he found the women of the Ansar weeping over their slain. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: But Hamzah has no weeping women. The chief of the Ansar, Sa'd ibn Mu'adh, heard that, so he came to the women of the Ansar and said: I adjure you that no woman among you weep in grief until she begins with the grief of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So they began weeping over Hamzah. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard that and said: What is this? They informed him of what Sa'd had done. He said: I did not intend that. And he forbade wailing."
"4088","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Hushaim and Khalid narrated to us, from Husayn, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, from Salim ibn Abdullah, he said: When we ascended we said Allahu Akbar, and when we descended we said Subhan Allah."
"4089","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Uthman ibn Khuthaym, he said: Sa'id ibn Jubayr informed me, from Ibn Abbas, that the chiefs of Quraysh gathered in al-Hijr and pledged by al-Lat, al-Uzza, and Manat the third, the other, that they would kill Muhammad. That reached Fatimah bint Muhammad, so she came to her father and informed him. He called for water and performed ablution, then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out while they sat in al-Hijr until he came to them. When they looked at him, Allah struck their chins down to their chests. He came until he stood over them, then said: May the faces be disfigured, may the faces be disfigured. He took a handful of dust and threw it at them. He said: None of those pebbles hit anyone except that he was killed as a disbeliever on the day of Badr."
"4090","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr, from Ikrimah, he said: Badr was a marketplace in the Jahiliyyah, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had promised Abu Sufyan to meet him there. A man met them and said: There is a great gathering of polytheists there. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) urged the people on; they came to Badr and found no one there. The coward returned and the bold went on; they traded there and found no one. Then there was revealed: Those to whom people said: People have gathered against you, so fear them, but it increased them in faith, and they said: Allah suffices us and He is the best Disposer, so they returned with blessing from Allah and bounty."
"4091","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: Your two fathers were indeed among them. [Flag: referent of 'them' unclear in the excerpt]"
"4092","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Abu Awanah, from al-Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: Abdullah was of those who responded to Allah."
"4093","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Hushaim narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from his grandmother, she said: My mother came to me, desiring favor, in the time of the treaty of Quraysh. I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Shall I maintain ties with her? He said: Yes."
"4094","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Yunus narrated to us, from Ikrimah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave gifts to some of the polytheists, to Abu Sufyan and others, and he accepted their gift. [Flag: subject of 'he accepted' is terse in source]"
"4095","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pair his Companions as traveling companions. A group came praising a man, saying: We have never seen the like of so-and-so; when we camp he prays, when we ride he recites, and he does not break his fast. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Who saddled his mount for him, and who worked for him? Sufyan mentioned other things. They said: We did. He said: All of you are better than him."
"4096","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Damdam ibn Zur'ah, from Shurayh ibn Ubayd, that Abdullah ibn Qurt al-Azdi said: A young camel of mine knelt down on me while I was with Khalid ibn al-Walid; the army outpaced me and I wanted to leave it, so I prayed to Allah to make it stand, and it stood. I kept following the tracks until I caught up with them while they fought the Romans on a height; the wives of Khalid and the wives of his companions had their garments tucked up, carrying water for the Muhajirun and chanting rajaz verses."
"4097","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Safwan ibn Amr, that the Romans fought Istiban al-Ahzam, who was their king, and cast him onto an island of the islands of the sea. Merchants passed by him and recognized him, so they carried him until they brought him out to the land of Hawran. He came to Muhammad ibn Marwan and sought his help; he used to call him: my brother. Istiban said to Muhammad ibn Marwan: Will you permit me to pass through your land until I reach the land of the Romans? He said: I cannot permit you until the Commander of the Believers permits you. Istiban said: I have pledged to Allah that if He returns me to my kingdom, I will not leave in the land of the Romans any Muslim who prays toward the qiblah except that I will free him and equip him to fight with me. Muhammad ibn Marwan asked permission for him from Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan, who permitted him. He crossed his land until he reached the land of the Romans toward the Fourth Armenia. He sought the help of the Muslims, and they fought with him until he overcame his enemy of the Romans. He kept killing his enemy and the men of their power until he prevailed over them and took firm hold of their kingdom, and the land of the Romans submitted to him. Then he freed the Muslim captives; he brought them from all the land of the Romans, freed them, carried them until they reached the land of Qinnasrin, and gave them five dinars each. Abd al-Malik and the Muslims approved of that."
"4098","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Najih and Fudayl ibn Fudalah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered his Companions to wear headbands, and said: If one of you finds nothing but a rag, let him wrap his head with it."
"4099","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Abi Ablah, from Yazid ibn Yazid ibn Jabir, from Habib ibn Maslamah, he said: On the day of the conquest of Jalula, a Muslim man killed a polytheist man. It was written about it to Umar ibn al-Khattab, and he wrote: Give him his spoils, and take the fifth from it."
"4100","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Umayyah ibn Yazid al-Qurashi, he said: I asked Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz for a stipend for a son of mine. He said: How old is he? I said: Six, seven, or eight years old. He said: If I were to assign a stipend for a child of mine under fifteen, I would assign for him."
"4101","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Abu Bakr ibn Abdullah, al-Ahwas ibn Hakim, and Artah ibn al-Mundhir, from Abu al-Ahwas Hakim ibn Umayr, that Umar ibn al-Khattab wrote: Whoever you make a contract with, fulfill it to them, beware wronging them; beware wearing qaba cloaks and thin khuffs; wear the izar, wear sandals, discipline horses, and practice archery."
"4102","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Muawiyah narrated to me, from Iyad ibn Ghudayf al-Kindi, that he came to Umar ibn al-Khattab wearing a qaba and thin khuffs. Umar disapproved of that and said: What is this? He said: O Commander of the Believers, as for the qaba, a man tightens it over himself and it gathers his clothes; as for thin khuffs, they are firmer in the stirrups. He said: Yes. And he permitted him that."
"4103","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from al-Ahwas and Abu Bakr, from Hakim ibn Umayr, that Umar ibn al-Khattab wrote to the people: To proceed: This world is sweet and green, so beware of it; reckon your deeds as for Allah; know that you are in the land of your enemy who do not understand your speech, so fulfill to them the covenant and protection. If one of you gestures with his hand to his enemy toward the sky and says: By Allah, if you come down I will kill you, and he comes down, he came down only when he gestured toward the sky, and that is his contract."
"4104","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Artah narrated to me, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, that Salman ibn Rabi'ah raided Balanjar and sought help from some polytheists. He said: The enemies of Allah carry against the enemies of Allah."
"4105","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Hushaim narrated to us, he said: al-Awwam ibn Hawshab informed us, he said: A man narrated to me that he heard Abu Salih, the freedman of Umar ibn al-Khattab, narrating, he said: He used to order us that three share together: one brings goods, another sells, and another fights in the path of Allah. He said: I saw Abu Salih in that year stationed at the frontier. He said: This is my turn."
"4106","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Matar narrated to us, he said: Abu Hariz narrated to me, from Amir al-Sha'bi, he said: The Muslims took captives from Awtas, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade intercourse with pregnant women until they deliver their burden, and whoever was not pregnant must observe waiting by one menstruation."
"4107","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Zibyan, from their elders, from Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, he said: He went out as a fighter in the time of Muawiyah and fell ill. When death came to him, he said to his companions: When I die, carry me; when you meet the enemy, bury me under their feet. I will narrate to you a hadith I heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); were it not for what has come to me I would not have narrated it to you. I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever dies not associating anything with Allah will enter Paradise."
"4108","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Muhammad ibn Talhah ibn Yazid ibn Rukanah, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab said: That I had asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about a people who say: We affirm zakat in our wealth but we will not pay it to you, would be dearer to me than red camels."
"4109","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from one who narrated to him, that Abu Bakr, when the people withheld zakat from him, wanted to fight them. It was said to him: Did not the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: I have been commanded to fight people until they say: There is no god but Allah; when they say it, they protect their blood and wealth from me except by its right? He said: This is part of its right, that they not separate what Allah has joined; if they withhold from me anything they acknowledged to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), I will fight them for it."
"4110","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ayyub al-Ta'i, from Qays ibn Muslim, from Tariq ibn Shihab, he said: The delegation of the people of apostasy from Asad and Ghatafan came asking Abu Bakr for peace. He gave them a choice: either an expelling war or a humiliating peace. They said: As for an expelling war, we know it; what is a humiliating peace? He said: You pay blood-money for our slain, and we do not pay for your slain; you testify that your slain are in the Fire; you return to us those of us you took, and we do not return to you what we took from you; we strip from you arms and horses; and you are left following camel tails until Allah shows the successor of the Messenger of Allah and the believers an opinion by which they excuse you. Umar said: As for what you said, it is as you said, except that our slain were killed for Allah; their reward is upon Allah; there is no blood-money for them."
"4111","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, who heard Jabir ibn Abdullah saying: Uthman sent us with fifty riders, our commander being Muhammad ibn Maslamah. When we reached Dhu Khashab, a man met us with a mushaf hung on his neck, his sword slung over him, his eyes shedding tears. He said: This one orders us to strike with this, meaning the sword, upon what is in this. Muhammad said to him: Sit down; we already struck with this upon what is in this before you, or before you were born. He kept speaking to them until they returned. Amr said: I heard Jabir saying: They claimed they found a letter to Ibn Sa'd, and Allah knows best."
"4112","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Abu Ma'shar narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: I was besieged with Uthman ibn Affan in the house. A man of us was shot and killed. I said to Uthman: O Commander of the Believers, has fighting not become lawful? A man of us was killed. He said: I adjure you, O Abu Hurayrah, to lay down your sword; only my soul is sought, and today I will shield the believers with my soul. Abu Hurayrah said: I threw my sword away, and I do not know where it is until this hour."
"4113","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: I came to Uthman on the day of the house and said: O Commander of the Believers, shall we fight? He said to me: O Abu Hurayrah, would it please you to kill all people, and me with them? I said: No. He said: By Allah, if you kill a single man, it is as if you killed all people. So I returned and did not fight."
"4114","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Salih, he said: Abdullah ibn Salam said on the day Uthman ibn Affan was killed: By Allah, you do not shed a cupping-glass of blood but you increase thereby in distance from Allah."
"4115","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Thabit ibn Ubayd, from Abu Ja'far al-Ansari, he said: I entered with the Egyptians upon Uthman ibn Affan; when they struck him, I went out running, having filled my legs with running, until I entered the mosque. There was a man sitting among about ten, wearing a black turban. He said to me: What is behind you? I said: By Allah, the man has been finished off. He said: Woe to you for the rest of time. And it was Ali."
"4116","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, that when he was told what was done to Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, he wept."
"4117","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Zakariyya narrated to us, from Asim al-Ahwal, he said: Abu Abdullah and Abu Zurarah informed me; they said: We testify by Allah concerning Ali with a testimony Allah will question us about; we were present with him at battle scenes, and we heard Ali saying: By Allah, I did not kill Uthman, nor participate, nor order, nor approve."
"4118","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Qays, from Ali ibn Rabi'ah al-Walibi, he said: I heard Ali saying: By Allah, I wish Banu Umayyah would accept that we provide them fifty men of Banu Hashim who swear: We did not kill Uthman, nor do we know his killer."
"4119","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Zakariyya narrated to us, from Abu Malik al-Ashja'i, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, he said: Muhammad ibn Ali said to Ibn Abbas: Do you remember the day when I was on the right of Ali and you on his left, on the day of al-Mirbad? He heard a noise from the direction of al-Mirbad and sent a messenger to see. He said: I left Aishah cursing the killers of Uthman while the people said amen. Ali said: And I curse the killers of Uthman in plain and mountain. Ibn Abbas said: Yes. Muhammad said: Are I and Ibn Abbas not two just men?"
"4120","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr, from Muhammad ibn Jubayr ibn Mut'im, he said: Uthman sent to Ali: Your cousin is killed, and you are robbed."
"4121","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, he said: I heard Abdullah ibn Amir ibn Rabi'ah saying: I was with Uthman in the house, and he said: I adjure everyone who sees he owes me hearing and obedience to restrain his hand and his weapon; the best of you in serving us is he who restrains his weapon and his hand. Stand, O Ibn Umar, and keep people apart. So Ibn Umar stood, and with him stood men of his people from Banu Adi, Banu Nu'aym, and Banu Muti'. They opened the door and he went out; then the people entered and killed Uthman."
"4122","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Faraj ibn Fadalah narrated to us, he said: Marwan ibn Abi Umayyah narrated to me, from Abdullah ibn Salam: He came to Uthman while he was besieged in his house, greeted him, and said: Welcome, my brother; shall I tell you what I saw this night? He said: I said: Yes. He said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this skylight, and he said to me: O Uthman. I said: At your service, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Have they besieged you? I said: Yes. He said: Have they made you thirsty? I said: Yes. He sent down to me a bucket of water and I drank until I was quenched; I still feel its coolness between my breasts and my shoulders. He said: O Uthman, choose: if you wish, break your fast with me, and if you wish, prevail over the people. I said: Rather I will break my fast with you. He was killed that very day, may Allah be pleased with him."
"4123","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, from his father, from Ali ibn al-Husayn, that Marwan ibn al-Hakam, while he was governor of Madinah, said to him: I never saw anyone with a finer victory than your father Ali ibn Abi Talib. Shall I tell you of his victory over us on the day of al-Jamal? I said: The governor knows best. He said: When we met on the day of al-Jamal we faced each other, then some of us charged others, and the people of Basrah were soon routed. A crier cried out for Ali: No fleeing man shall be killed, no wounded man shall be finished off; whoever locks the door of his house is safe, and whoever lays down arms is safe. Marwan said: I had entered the house of so-and-so, then I sent to Hasan and Husayn, the sons of Ali, and Abdullah ibn Abbas, and Abdullah ibn Ja'far, and they spoke to him. He said: He is safe; let him go where he wishes. I said: By Allah, I will not be content until I pledge to him. So I pledged to him. Then he said: Go where you wish."
"4124","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, from his father, that Ali would not take spoils, that he fought in person, and that he would not finish off a wounded man nor kill one who fled."
"4125","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Ma'mar narrated to me, he said: Sayf ibn Muawiyah ibn so-and-so al-Anazi, my maternal uncle, narrated to me, from my grandfather, he said: On the day of al-Jamal, when the horses were in confusion, people came to Ali claiming things and spoke at length, and he did not understand. He said: Is there no man to gather their speech into five or six words? He said: So I rose upon one leg, then stood tall and said: O Commander of the Believers, speech is not five or six, but two words. Ali looked at me and I said: Settled by compromise or by retaliation. He gestured with his hand and folded thirty [uncertain number in source], saying: Qalun. Then he said: Whatever you have counted, it is under my foot. [Flag: several words in the anecdote are obscure or possibly miscopied in source]"
"4126","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abdullah narrated to us, he said: Ata ibn al-Sa'ib narrated to us, from Abu al-Bakhtari, he said: When Ali prevailed over the people of al-Jamal, he said: Do not finish off a wounded man, do not pursue one who flees; what was in the camp is yours, what was outside it is not yours; slave-mothers are not lawful to you; and women shall observe waiting from their husbands for four months and ten days."
"4127","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Abu Fakhitah, he said: A neighbor of mine informed me, he said: I brought a captive to Ali on the day of Siffin. The man said to him: Do not kill me. He said: I will not kill you in bonds; I fear Allah, Lord of the worlds. Is there good in you, that you pledge allegiance? He said: Yes. He said to the one who brought him: His weapon is yours."
"4128","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abdullah narrated to us, he said: al-Shaybani narrated to us, from Arfajah, from his father, from Ali, he said: What remained of the wretched goods of the people of al-Nahr was brought and placed in the courtyard. He said: Whoever recognizes anything, let him take it. The people kept taking until only a pot remained for a while, until a man came and took it."
"4129","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, he said: Sulayman ibn Hisham wrote to him asking about a woman who joined the Haruriyyah, left her husband, testified shirk against her people, married among them, then returned repentant. Al-Zuhri wrote to him, while I was witness: To proceed: The first fitnah erupted while many of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who witnessed Badr were numerous. They held that the matters of the fitnah be dropped, and no hadd be imposed in it upon anyone for a private part he deemed lawful by interpretation of the Quran, nor for retaliation he deemed lawful by interpretation of the Quran, nor for wealth he deemed lawful by interpretation of the Quran, unless a specific item is found. I hold that you return her to her husband, and punish with hadd whoever slandered her."
"4130","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: One who saw Amr ibn al-As on the day of Siffin informed me: he was on a pulpit of his, carried on a mule [word uncertain in source], and said: O Abdullah, keep the rank straight; shall the mustache be trimmed? Then he said: Bring me arms. They cast around him like a black lava field. Then he said: Take them, for these people committed an error reaching the canopy of the sky. The people came forward and took arms. He said: Upon you is al-Dajjal, meaning Hashim ibn Utbah al-A'war. [Flag: two phrases in the description are obscure in source]"
"4131","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Shihab ibn Khirash narrated to us, he said: al-Awwam narrated to us, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Abu Wa'il, he said: Amr ibn Shurahbil al-Hamdani narrated to me, and I never saw a Hamdani better than him; I said: Not even Masruq? He said: Not even Masruq. He said: I was troubled by the matter of the people of Siffin and the merit I knew in both parties, so I asked Allah to show me something of their affair I could rest upon. I was shown in a dream that I was raised above the people of Siffin, and there were the companions of Ali in a green meadow with flowing water. I said: Glory be to Allah, how is what I see, when some of you killed others? They said: We found our Lord Kind, Merciful. I said: What happened to Dhu al-Kala' and Hawshab, meaning the companions of Muawiyah? They said: Ahead of you. Then an arrow like a wing [Flag: word obscure in source] came, and I descended upon the people in a green meadow with flowing water. I said: Glory be to Allah, how is what I see, when some of you killed others? They said: We found our Lord Kind, Merciful. I said: What happened to the people of al-Nahrawan? They said: They were cast far away, or he said: each met hardship."
"4132","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Umayyah, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) held a race between horses, sending the trained ones from al-Hafya to the mosque of Banu Zurayq."
"4133","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Muhammad ibn Jubayr ibn Mut'im, that Sa'id ibn al-As held a race between horses at Kufah, setting one hundred reeds, and for the last of them a reed worth one thousand dirhams."
"4134","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab, he said: There is no harm in an entered extra horse if it is not between the two competing horses."
"4135","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr, he said: It was said to Jabir ibn Zayd: The Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw no harm in an entered extra horse. He said: They are more chaste than that."
"4136","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, he said: al-A'mash narrated to us, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, he said: He had a hackney on which he used to race."
"4137","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, he said: al-A'mash narrated to us, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, he said: He had a hackney on which he used to bet."
"4138","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Hazm ibn Abi Hazm narrated to us, he said: I heard al-Hasan saying: Sa'd ibn Mu'adh was struck by an arrow on the day of al-Ahzab. He said: O Allah, do not let me die until You heal me from Qurayzah and al-Nadir. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished with al-Ahzab and turned to Qurayzah and besieged them, Sa'd ibn Mu'adh took charge of judging them. He judged concerning them that the fighters be killed and the offspring taken captive. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) killed of their fighters whom he willed to kill, and took their offspring captive. Then Sa'd ibn Mu'adh was carried; there were hypocrites in his funeral that day. Some said: How light he is. Others said: Why is that? They said: Because of what he judged concerning Banu Qurayzah, and they lied; Sa'd had been fleshy, corpulent among men, and great. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), as they carried him, said: They say: How light he is; by Him in Whose Hand is my soul, the Throne shook for the soul of Sa'd ibn Mu'adh."
"4139","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Sufyan, from Jabir, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The Throne of Allah Almighty shook for the death of Sa'd ibn Mu'adh."
"4140","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Sa'd ibn Ibrahim, he said: I heard Abu Umamah ibn Sahl narrating from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, saying: When the people of Qurayzah came down accepting the judgment of Sa'd ibn Mu'adh, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent for him. He came on a donkey; when he was near the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to the Ansar: Stand for your chief. He came until he sat beside the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: These people have come down accepting your judgment. He said: I judge concerning them that their fighters be killed and their offspring taken captive. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You have judged with the judgment of the King, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him."
"4141","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Hushaim narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr informed us, he said: Atiyyah al-Qurazi narrated to me, he said: I was among those presented to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Qurayzah. They doubted about me, so they looked at my pubic hair; they did not find me grown, so they let me go."
"4142","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, he said: I met a man in the mosque of Kufah and he narrated to me, he said: I was among those concerning whom Sa'd ibn Mu'adh judged; they doubted about me, and found the razor had not passed over me, so they let me go."
"4143","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Ayyub narrated to us, from Abu Qilabah, from his uncle, from Imran ibn Husayn, that Thaqif were allies of Banu Uqayl in the Jahiliyyah. The Muslims captured a man of Banu Uqayl with a she-camel of his. They brought him to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). When he came to him, he said: O Muhammad, for what did you seize me and seize the forerunner of the pilgrims? In the Jahiliyyah, when a she-camel outran, she was not kept from a watering place she came to nor pasture she grazed. He said: For the offense of your allies, Thaqif; Thaqif had captured two Muslim men. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pass by him while he was confined, and he would say: O Muhammad, I am a Muslim. He said: Had you said it while you controlled your affair, you would have attained all success. Then he passed by him again, and he said: O Muhammad, I am hungry, so feed me, and thirsty, so give me drink. He said: That is your need. Then it occurred to him to ransom him, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ransomed him for the two Muslim men, and kept the she-camel for himself; it was al-Adba. An enemy raided the pasturing livestock of Madinah and took it; they used to bring their camels home at night. There was with the polytheists a woman they had taken captive; she went off and came to the livestock. She kept coming to each camel and it bellowed, until she came to her and she did not bellow. She mounted her and set her loose. When she reached Madinah, the people said: Al-Adba, al-Adba. She said: I vowed that if Allah saves me on her, I will slaughter her. They informed the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Evil is how you repaid her; there is no fulfilling a vow in disobedience, nor fulfilling a vow concerning what the son of Adam does not own."
"4144","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Salih ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Muawiyah narrated to us, from Nu'aym ibn Abi Hind, from his uncle, he said: I was with Ali at Siffin. Prayer came, so we called the adhan and they called the adhan, we performed the iqamah and they performed the iqamah, we prayed and they prayed. I turned, and there were the slain between us and them. I said to Ali when he finished: What do you say concerning our slain and their slain? He said: Whoever of us and of them was killed seeking the Face of Allah and the Hereafter will enter Paradise."
"4145","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Salih ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: al-A'mash narrated to us, from Shaqiq ibn Salamah, from Sahl ibn Hunayf, that he said on the day of Siffin, while he was with Ali: O people, gather your opinion; by Allah, we never placed our swords on our shoulders except that they led us easily to a known matter, unlike this matter of yours; so suspect your own opinion. He sheathed his sword and went back to his family."
"4146","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: I asked al-Hasan ibn Muhammad, and I never saw anyone more knowledgeable of people's disagreements than him: Did Talhah and al-Zubayr pledge allegiance to Ali? He said: They climbed up to Ali in a loft of his; when they came down, the people said: They pledged, they pledged."
"4147","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr, from al-Hasan ibn Muhammad, he said: The Arabs on the day of Siffin were pure stock."
"4148","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abu Nadrah, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: My ummah will become two parties; a renegade group will emerge between them, and the one of them closer to the truth will kill them."
"4149","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Ayyub narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, he said: Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas said: I do not claim I am more entitled to the caliphate than this shirt of mine; I fought when I knew jihad, and I will not strain myself for it to be said a man is better than me. By Allah, I will not fight until you bring me a sword with a tongue and two lips that says: This is a believer and this is a disbeliever."
"4150","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Ayyub narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, he said: A man said: There is none of us whom the fitnah overtook but that, had I wished, I could have spoken about him, except Ibn Umar."
"4151","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Ayyub informed us, from Muhammad, he said: A man said: O Allah, keep Abdullah ibn Umar alive as long as You keep me alive, that I may follow him; I know no one today upon the original matter except him."
"4152","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Ayyub narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, he said: I was told that Ibn Umar said: I met my companions upon a matter; if I oppose them, I fear I will not join them."
"4153","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Ayyub narrated to us, he said: I was told that Ibn Umar was with Muawiyah, who said: Who is more entitled to this authority than us, and who disputes with us about this authority? He said: I intended to say: Those who fought you and your father for Islam, but I feared my saying this would shed blood, and that my saying would be taken otherwise than I intended; and I remembered what is with Allah of Gardens."
"4154","Saeed narrated to us, he said: Attaf ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Siddiq ibn Musa ibn Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr narrated to me, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Madinah and his mount knelt with him between the house of Ja'far ibn Muhammad ibn Ali and the house of al-Hasan ibn Zayd. The people came to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, stay with us. His mount rose with him, and he said: Leave her, for she is commanded. Then she went out with him until she brought him to the door of Abu Ayyub al-Ansari and knelt with him. The people came to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, stay with us. His mount rose with him, and he said: Leave her, for she is commanded. Then she went out with him until she brought him to the place of the minbar, where she knelt with him and then settled. The people had there a trellis they used to sprinkle, set up, and cool themselves under. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) dismounted from his mount and went to the shade; he stayed there, and Abu Ayyub came to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, my house is the nearest of houses to you, so move your baggage to me. He said: Yes. So he took his baggage to the house. Then another man came to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, stay with me. He said: A man is with his baggage wherever it is. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) remained in the trellis until he prayed with the people there for twelve nights. (End of the Book of Jihad.) [Flag: a copyist colophon follows in print and is omitted]"
